      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>SOME folks love equality so well that the success of others makes them miserable. A LONG head is a great help in preventing a long face. ORATOR : " Gen tlemen, lam a self-made man." Voice : " Where wcii de p'lice ?" Pur a beggar on horseback and he'll run into debt. BREAD may be the staff of life, but a saddle of mutton makes the journey easier. WREN the fool hath nothing to say, he telleth it; but when the wise man bath something to say he kcepeth it to himself. THERE is a certain kind of charity that would attach balloons to birds of the air, that they might be saved from fatigue. A CLEAR DisriNcxfox.—Agent : "There are a dozen fraudulent imitations on the market, but this is the original genuine article." Retailer: "How can I tell it from the rest?" Agent : "All the others have their labels marked, "Beware of imitations.'"</p>
        <p>A BOY ANGEL. —Mamma : "Have you eaten that big apple already?" Litt e Dick: "No'm. I gave it to a poor little boy in the back street." "Mamma's little angel! Do you want another ? " " No'm. I have got the toothache." NOT WHAT SHE EXPECTED. —Mrs. De Vere : "They say my daughter took her beauty from her mother." The Count: "How shameful zat she</p>
        <p>ahould hat so deprived you!" A DISAPPOINTING CIRCUMSTANCE. Traveller (inquiring at feudal castle): "Can I see the antiquities to-day ?" Servant: "I am afraid not, air. My lady and her daughter have gone to</p>
        <p>town." JOHNNY EXPLAINS. —Teacher : "Why did you hit Willie Wink urn with a stone?" Little Johnny: _ _ ___._ . , ...</p>
        <p>"Re—he got mad, and looked as if he'd like to hit me with something, so I jus' chucked the stone over to him, so he could throw it at me." HE REALLY NEEDED Pr.—" I've bought a type- writer," said the senator. "To write your speeches on?" "No, not that; but since I've been in the Senate I've had so many requests for my autograph that I got tired of writing it with a pen and ink." BEHIND HIS BACK.—" Blykins doesn't seem like a very energetic fellow," said the charitable man; "but there is a good deal that he doesn't get credit for." "Maybe there is," replied the gossip. "But his groceryman says not." THE CAUSE oF THE TROCBLE.—Wing : "Things seem quiet at the lodge lately." King: "Yes. The Past Grand Worthy Supreme Mogul's wife won't let him out at night, and he was the life and soul of the meeting." THE VANITY OF MAN.—She brought out a lunch, this pitying maid, To the tramp, 'neath the linden tree. He thanked her, but inwsrdly chuckled and said, "There s another girl mashed on me."</p>
        <p>- , BEFORE IT DEVELOPS.—Fond mother: "Yes, sir; I have a little fellow who is only ten, and yet he writes beautiful poetry," Old Editor: $. Well, there's some hope for em when you catch 'em young, You can whip it out of 'em easier then." Miss Bunn (to famous pianist) : "That musio was truly divine, monsieur." Monsieur: " Ah, mam'selle, zat is indeed praise; for who but an angel would know divine music?" PARENTAL SOLICITUDE—Daughter: "But, ma, I don't like him." Mother: "He is an only son, and his father is very rich." "Well, as to that, his father is a widower, and may marry again." "True. I did not think of that- Perhaps you'd better marry the father." "HAVE you made any special preparation for the whist party to-night?" Mrs. Smgleton : " Yes ; I've picked up several new bits of gossip." A GOOD HEM:SR.—Wife : "George, do you know that you swore in your sleep last night?" Husband: "Well, it was excusable. I dreamed that I was putting the studs in my dress shirt." STRANGER: "My young friend, I would not amoke that cigar." Boy: " Yer just bet yer wnnicin't, not when I bought it and Nal for it.' " I DO not think Binks was entirely to blame, but there are some features of the ease which look dark for him." " What are they ? " "Mrs.</p>
        <p>AN EASY WAY OUT OF Ir.—Father "He says that he loves you. But can he support you in the style that you have been accustomed to?" Daughter : "Even better, father, dear, if you will furnish the money. That is all that dis- courages him.' Arzum ExTkAcr.—" THEY say that Time is a great teacher. That is true, but, unfortunately, he kills all his pupils." CLARA : "I'm so fond of music. I want to play the piano awfully !" Laura: "Well, you do play it awfully." NEW OFFICE REQUTSITE.—VISITOR : "Why do you have that dog sitting on your writing desk ?" Clerk : "I have mislaid my sponge, so I am getting him to lick my postage-stamps for me." A PLAUSIBLE THEORY.—A French servant declares that fishes can Talk. This may be so, but all the fish we ever became acquainted with were silent. Possibly they were dumbfounded at the lies that the fisherman told about them. THE WRONG Dook.—Ginr. (jokiogly) : I'd like a place where I'll have everything I want, nothing to do, and no one to boss me. Clerk : "This, miss, is an employment office, not a matrimonial agency." WAITER : "How do you like your eggs? "Cus- tomer : "Not hard; about three minutes' boiling will do, but be as quick as you can." Waiter: "All right, sir; you shall have them in half a minute." HONEST PRIDE ---Magistrate: "You are brought 'up on the charge of stealing wood, but as you are poor, you shall be let off this time; only don't do it again." Peasant: "Nonsense I I don't want *ay favours. I steal my wood, and pay my fine, And there's an end on't." OUR SERVANTS —Husband. : "I like that new servant of ours; how well she can polish a pair of boots!" Wife : Yes, the vain hussy ; I had to remove the looking-glasses out of the kitchen, and now-- IF your friend meets with adversity do not offend him by inquiring into the particulars. His neighbour will be glad to tell you all. Thus you not only avoid °rending your friend, but you give pleaoire to the neighbour. BELLOWS : t* Does your daughter play on the piano?" 0!il Farm r (in tones of deep disgust) : "No, sir. She wurks on it, pounds on it, rakes it, rolls over on it, but there's scrapes it, jumps ou it, no play about it, sir A SLIGHT MISTAKE. —GPEt : "How. came you to put your hand in my pocket? Pickpocket : " "Beg your pardon. lam so absent-minded. I had once a pair of pants just like those you are</p>
        <p>ftoyal Lot:mdse.:es say Ordet‘lev's " IiTARCO GLC.B3 is unequalled for impvting s Teantitnl enlsh to starOhed &amp;e„ either cold or honing aster starch. Mr. —, Corrk. wall. says :--'• My wifefll not finish one /inan WlthOUt your Starch Gloss.— ?rout Mrs. qrzlfor "Dear sir,- 1 turn pleased to Inform MI that h-4,e tried MSG'S, ,o-Call" Starch Glazes and Po 4bes, tad n ; non. to yours." It should be used in ever's' bmse. . arid Rd., t y Most starch sellers tln.uuIt. 1. CRITCMAY.</p>
