      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>• rITA CliAt</head>
        <p>• rITA CliAt</p>
        <p>So far as the Conservative party are concerned, there is no doubt that the a•surance of Mr. T. P. O'Connor, M.P.,that the Irish members of the House of Commons will not oppose any legislative pro- posals of the Government in favour of Voluntary schools is extremely welcome. lamin a position to state that the almost universal feeling among those who pu:l the strings of Tory organisations here is that something substantial should be done to assist denominational education. The Tories are in a temper io ride rough-shod over Liberal Union- ist private members and Liberal Unionists in the country also, without compunction, but they have Liberal Unionist Ministers to reckon with. This is of course, the recognised difficulty, and it is the candid opinion of an eminent Conservative that be does not know how it will be got over.</p>
        <p>The members of the London School Board who, following the lead of Mr. Athelstan Riley,are intent on securing the sanction of the Home Secretary to the infliction of coporal punishment on girls will not get their own way. I have excellent authority for the statement that Sir Matthew White Ridley will absolutely decline to entertain the idea. He has already indicated that he does not approve of the policy of caning girls, even in industrial schools, and many people consider it an impertin- ence that lie should be coolly invited to stultify himself.</p>
        <p>I!understand that the proposal of the Melbourne Cricket Club,that an English team should visit Aus- tralia after the Australians have visited England next year, will not be considered at present. Nor is it very probable that Mr. Stoddart will, in any case, undertake to place himself at the bead of an- other eleven. He is naturally nervous lest a second venture might not be so successful as the last, and if an English team goes out to the Antipodes in the winter of 1896 some other well-known amateur will very likely be the captain. Mr. W. W. Read is spoken of as eminently fitted to lead such an under- taking.</p>
        <p>The story which comes from Hereford, represent- ing the clergy of the diocese to be resigning fat livings, and vacancies to be going a begging.becau,e of the friction caused by the Liberalism of Bishop Percival, is a little too " tall" for acceptance. So long as the church is one of the over-crowded pro- fessions, neither surrenders of livings nor refusals of offers of them are likely to happen to an extent injurious to the religious teaching of the diocese. If it be true that four resignations have taken place, and that two more are to follow,so much the better for the multitude of unbeneficed clergy who have , been eating their hearts out through absence of preferment. The concoctor of this story practically refutes his own contention, for he glories in the manner in which the diocesan conferences ride rough-shod over the bishop's views on disestablish- ment and education. If the bishgp be in a minority of one, why should there be a stampede of clergy from the diocese?</p>
        <p>One is glad to hear that the Government have Promptly acceded to the request to provide a site rr the statue of Oliver Cromwell. The question re- mains as to the choice of a place. There are several alternatives. There is the Tyburn site ; there is a space outside the House of Lords ; there is Palace- yard ; and there is Parliament-gquare. Perhaps the i)est an. most picturesque of all the suggestions is that the statue—an equestrian one, of course— should have some association with the widening of Parliament-street and the opening out of the Square by the great improvement which the Government havelin band. Supposing Parliament- street ends in a spacious and stately opening, could there be a better idea than that the houses should fall back in ordered row, so as to disclose the mighty shape of the great Abbey, and above all to ct the eye rest first of all on the Protector, horsed and accoutred as if for Worcester field ? The point is one of the deepest historic interest for the nglish people. and I hope the Government will keep it steadily in view. Now, however, that the Government have shown so liberal a hand in re- :.;ard to the statue,might they not go a step further, and satisfy a strong and keen sentiment among many supporters of their own, by taking steps to investigate the identity of the Cromwell bead?</p>
        <p>A name has not yet been found for horseless carriages. "Authomobile cars,"" autocars," "Auto- motives" do not appear to have caught on. The Iqtest suggestion we have had is" motor car." Mr. 2. R. Sims. who is responsible for it, urges that this would be a name giving a clear distinction from the . xisting ordinary carriages, beside implying a new mode of locomotion.</p>
        <p>The tunnel under the Thames at Blackwalhwhich is flaw completed, is one of the monumental • ngineering works of the age. It may be eclipsed `iy the Tower Bridge, though it is of a character that scarcely admits of comparison with that colossal undertaking. There are two borings to it —one for pedestrians and the other fo- vehicles— and it will bring the populous and busy centres of Mackwall on the one side of the river, and Green- wich on the other, into easy communication. The ferryboat which has hitherto been the means of crossing from the one bank to the other at this point, of the river will now,l presume, be discarded. Like many other great enterprises, it was deemed at one time an impracticable undertaking, but. the engineering genius of the age has surmounted all I difficulties, and it will survive as a monument of the courage and large-mindedness of the county council,</p>
        <p>.6propos of the present differences in the Irish party, l learn on reliable authority that strong pressure is about to be put upon Mr. Sexton with a view to induce him to return to his place in Parliament at the opening of next session. A satis- factory feature in regard to Mr. Sexton's position is that Mr. Healy and hi i friends are as strongly in his favour as those members who usually act with Mr. Dillon. Should Mr. Sexton consent to return, it is extremely probable that he will be elected chairman of the party.</p>
        <p>The announcement of the engagement of the Princess Maud of Wales to Prince Charles of Den- mark has elicited not a few hints at the undesir- ability of cousinly marriages. This is a matter on which different people have different opinions. It is celculated to suggest that there are alliances omitted from the table of prohibited relationships in the Prayer Book not less curious than some which are included. But, however, views on this point may differ. there is pretty general unanimity in the exclamation anent the betrothal heard every- where, "Thank goodness. it is not a German this time." The Princess of Wales is understood to be particularly pleased that the parting of one or her daughters, which is especially hard for her affec- tionate nature to bear, is mitigated by the know- ledge that the Princess goes to her old home, and to he among her own family. There is a marked contrast between Princess Maud and her betrothed. She is petite ; her future husband is as tall as his grandfather, the King of Denmark, who is a son of Anak The Princess is exceedingly gentle and natty; the Prince is bluff, robust and almost brus- que. There is no information as to the arrange- ments made between the parents of the high-con- tracting parties as to the manner in which an es- tablishment is to be provided for the young people. It is not surprising that Lord Salisbury is dis- satisfied with the fine promises of the Sultan as to the future humane government of his Asiatic pro- vinces. To suppose that he could be, would be to attribute to him either a child-like innocence, or a measure of insincerity with which his worst de- tractors would be sorry to credit him. Those best acquainted with the Turk know well that, while the Sultan will probably make some attempt to keep up appearances, his affected compliance with the representations of Europe, means for the Armenians larger deceits, more false wit nesses,more forged documents, and more tortures to wrest from .them confessions of non-existent sedition, on the strength of which outrageous treatment may be justified. The friends of the Armenians are by no moans disposed to regard the ameHoration of their country's lot as a mere patching up of the wounds caused by Turkish cruelty. This struggle is one for the religious and civil liberty of themselves and of the rest of Turkey. With nothing less will they be satisfied. The reforms to the demand for which the Armenian persecutors have given emphasis and European recognition are not restricted to compen- sation for the past and guarantees that such things shall be impossible in the future. N^r do they aim only at the redemption of an oppressed nation. They have for their end the civil and religious , liberty of the whole Ottoman Empire.</p>
        <p>Thn wages dispute at the Barrow Steelworks has been mettled, and the works will be re-opened at the earliest possible moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>1-4,XAMINEIt, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 2, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE 1-4,XAMINEIt, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 2, 1895.</p>
        <p>For young girls tulle dresses are fashionable. Very dark and very light are the shades sought after. In Paris no jewellery is worn by young unmarried girls in the evening. rhe newest hats have nearly all a crown of one material, and a brim of another. Draped backs are much used with tea and ball gowns. Fine black lace is:much used again, festoon fashion, on evening skirts. The newest pocket handkerchiefs are guiltless of frill, but are elaborately embroidered and checked. Sleeves in Paris are enormous, but they all droop from the shoulder. Flower posies tucked Into the waist-band are very fashionable. For srna t afternoon wear the Langtry shoe is much worn. Candle moulds are supposed to be the invention of a Frenchman about the year 1400. The natives of India believe that elephants have a religion and form of worship. Feathers have the place of honour as far as the trim- mings are cencerned, bands of ostrich feathers being well combined with j.3t on many swagger Autumn gowns. The thinnest sheet of iron ever rolled has a surface of fifty-five square inches and weighs but twenty grains. It would take one thousand eight hundred such sheets to make a layer an inch thick. In Norway a law has recently, been passed •which makes girls ineligible for matrimony until they are pro- ficient in knitting, baking and spinning. Certificates of proficiency have to be earned, and without these so girl may marry. The best way of treating a stove which has not been blacked for some time is to rub well with a newspaper, using a little clean grease. It will take a polish quickly after a few treatments of this kind. . .</p>
        <p>A medical correspondent writea :—" A good house- wife who has prepared the stalk of rhubarb for a pudd- ing, and also the mid-rib of the leaf, informed me that the leaf itself,when boiled,made a very good vegetahle." Size for size, a thread of spider silk is decidedly tougher than a bar of steel. An ordinary thread will bear the weight of three grains. This is just about 50 per cent, stronger than a steel thread of the same thick- ness. Jet is to be much worn, an extensive variety of :de- signs being seen. Sometimes jet and spangles are corn- bined,and often beads are also to be seen. Jetted yokes and other accessories are used largely on dressy gowns. Coloured sprangles are in vogue, and form elaborate designs in flowers, scrolls. Sic. It should be mentioned in the interests of antiseptic purity and suffering humanity that a good stout tooth brush, plenty of water and some antiseptic dentifrice, applied morning and night, afford a greater safeguard against more diseases than most people are aware. To make black japan varnish for small wooden articles, take of Naples asphaltum fifty pounds : dark gum anirne, eight pounds. Fuse. Add twelve gallons of linseed oil, boil, then add of dark gum amber ten pounds, previously fused and boiled in two gallons of linseed oil; next add dryers, and thin with oil of tur- pentine. Many persons like a nicely-cooked bloater, but object to its strong aroma. The smell is not so strong if the fish are split and boned, then cooked very slowly on the gridiron, and, just before sending to table, a piece of butter on the end of a knife is spread over each to take off the dry appearance. They should be served very hot. Of the human heart Dr. Ephraim Cutter writes :—" I have listened to the heart sounds of one woman and one man, both over ninety-nine years of age, with feelings of awe, and have thought how wonderful it was that these hearts had pulsated so long, propelling blood through vessels which if possible to be ranged on one line would on a very moderate estimate reach twice around the earth, or forty-eight thousand miles, with a leeway for the average red disc of one forty-eighth- thousandth of an inch."</p>
        <p>Snowy Clothes with Little Care. A grievance with almost every housekeeper Is the 11l treatment linen and lingerie meet with at the hands of the average laundress. Washing should, when possible, be done at heme—an easy matter if one has a good sized laundry, a garden, or a drying room, and a know- ledge of how to manage the process in the right fashion. All the white clothes should be put to soak the night pre- vious, !Indy linen being put into cold water, softened wil h a little borax, the soiled parts rubbed with some good soap and placed well under water. Bed linen should he put to soak in another tub,of course, and table linen in still another. All articles touched with iron rust, fruit or ink stains may be snaked in some milk for twelve hours, and if very obstinate they should be treated with Salt and lemon juice until they diqie- pear. For flannels use soft water moderately warm. and tin snap powder or soda. All the little odds and ends of snap should be saved in a jar kept for the put. pone and reduced to a jelly by adding half a p:nt of water to a quarter p end of soap scraps and letting it heat. The jelly may be used instead of soap, and the flannels should he washed very thoroughly,working them about well in the water. The white ones should be done first and the cnlnured ones next in the same water. Wring them lightly.shahe out and put aside until all are done, taking can that one does not touch the other_ Then take clear tepid water, put them through it to re move the snap,and put through the wringer. Shalce,pull them into shape, and hang in the shade to dry. Coloured prints, if there is a..y doubt as to the fastness of the colour. shonld he anaked first in salt and water,and then washed just like the flannels. They should be rirsed in clear cold water,with one tablespnonful of vinegar to two gallons of water. If they are wanted slightly stiffened, they should then be put thr-ugh thin starch made with boiling water ; if very stiff, as for skirts, &amp;c., they must be dried firat.and starched afterwards. Coloured things should always he dried in the shade and well shaken be- fore hung to dry.</p>
        <p>Chinese WiVPS. Chinese bridal processions are often really pretty. If the groom is a wealthy man there area number of priests going in front with their musical instruments,then Come the two flote-plavers, who are generally youths, and after them the bride, shut quits out of sight in a beauti- ful red chair. This sedan is elaborately carved in relief, with little red lanterns burning here and there in unique niches. Everything at the marriage, even the bride's veil, is red, for.this colour indicates joy,and white grief. !Arming the bride are the friends of the groom, in chairs, of course. None of the bride's family form part of the cryrtoge, as she is expected, after marriage, to belong heart and soul, not only to her husband, who has bought her, but to his family also, and to have nothing further to do with her own relatives. When the bride has been taken to her husband's house, she awaits his corning covered from head to foot in a long, red, cloud-like veil. When he arrives he is received by an elderly female, who conducts him to his wife's presence. The latter is discovered sitting in a corner still entirely veiled. The husband falls on his knees beside her and begs that she will re- move her veil and allow him to see her. This she does, and the two behold eac% other face to face for the first time. They then sit down to a frugal supper of rice and chicken, after which the elderly servant discreetly retiree. If the wife is clever she may be able •so to amuse and please her husband as to remain the only wife, but after a year or so another is almost sure to be added to the establishment as a general rule. The first wife is always addressed, even by her husband, as the First Lady. The next wife is the Second Lady, and the third the Third Lady. Even should the husband write to his wife, he always addresses her thus, never by her name. When his mother enters the family apartments all the wives stand until shelallows the First Lady to sit down. She in like manner. passes on a eimlilar perms. boo to the Second Lidr, who, in her turn, tel!9 the Third Lady that she may be seated. Even the children do not take the name of the father, but are know* AS the children of the lady who is their mother. The wife who happens to be in favour for the time takes charge of her husband's clothes. When he thinks he would like an-ther companion for a change,he signifies this wish to the wife by presenting her with semen gift. On receipt of this she retires, and does not venture to enter his presence until especially recalled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, I know you agree with me in liking plain skirts, so I know you will deplore as much as I do the hideous—as I think it —fashion that is creeping in of multiplying flounces. This change began, you know, at the end of the summer season, and the unexpected return of July weather in September is largely- reAponsible for the change, as it caused a second season for organdis and muslins. First of all we had little flounces cut on the cross and edged with a "French hem" or a tiny Valen- ciennes, amplifying our skirts. Then they grew to five, and it is probable that we shall find them on the winter alpacas and on all the light woollen materials. Plain skirts will, however, lam happy to believe, be reserved for useful wear ; we can- not give up the tailor-cut gown for the street, while every kind of trimming will be used on even-</p>
        <p>ing gowns. I may tell you that fur capes are likely to be as fashionable this whiter as they were some years ago, and, prodded a good one is purchased, there is no more useful outdoor garment. You can wear out good fur to the very last shred. Stylish full-length lloaks of fine cloth, lined and trimmed with fur, are to be had from about 3 guineas, and smart three-quarter cloaks cost from '2l guineas. Here, for instance, is one made of black velours du Nord and brocade, the brocade being fastened up on the shoulders to stand in an erect frill, while the trim- mings are of jet. This cloak sets tightly into the back in a most becoming shape.</p>
        <p>Coats continue in double-breasted reelbr or tight-fitting Si apes. They are smartened by the addition of revers of velvet of a contrasting colour, edged with metallic braid or gold braided leather. The long driving coat or redingote is to be pushed on the market of fashion, and scarlet cloth is in great request. I notice one curious thing with respect to coats. Although dress sleeves are certainly less inflated than of yore, some of the smartest coats have the new godet sleeves of truly balloon-like proportions; they are certainly neither artistic nor, as a rule, becoming. For all that, I like the godet shape, provided it is of medium size. A very nice one was made of fine black cloth, perfect-fitting and double-breasted, with strapped seams, and a peculiarly smart strapped effect in front, from shoulder to waist, terminating by the natty pocket. But amongst the idols I have found worthy of my sincerest affections in fashionable quarters is the loose- backed coat, and this is being made under the most attractive aspect in dark blue, with a velvet collar, a double row of buttons in the front, sleeves •14athered round the armhole, and a lining of silk. Besides being smart, this coat is extremely com- fortable to wear. It will slip on over the most elaborate of blouses, and, of eourse, it can be made</p>
        <p>with a fur collar and cuffs or fastened straight up to the neck with a fur boa. But these are minor details; the great fact is the hang of the back and of the front.</p>
        <p>-- ----- As the season advances we begin to think of the advisability of choosing an autumn hat that will be in readiness for wearing with heavier gowns. For a useful hat nothing could be smarter than the French sailor with the bell-crown. It is necessary for this hat to be simply trimmed with a ribbon round the crown, and an aigrette of quills, in order to preserve its correct character, so that it is too simple a style to form the subject of a lesson. For smart wear this hat will also take a prominent position among the early season novelties, but made in velvet in preference to felt, either en costume or in pretty harmony with the gown, and having a panache of self-coloured feathers at the left side. Here is a very pretty little bonnet, and it could, of course, be carried out in any colour.</p>
        <p>That special example, however, shows kilted bows of green and heliotrope shot satin ribbon in the front, while the crown is made of chenille edged with jet, the indispensable feathers curling in the centre and either side of the back.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>JE'SNIE.</p>
