      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>LIBERATION SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>THE LIBERATION SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Council of the Liberation Society met in London on Ttraday. Mr. It. Illingworth presiding. rofessor Mosey (Oxford) moved a resolution calling for extended efforts to influence the electorate in favour of the Society's principles and to defeat any legislative or ari- -1 ministrative measures cf the present Government which I might be hoatile to religions equality. Mr. P. W. Clavden seconded the resolution, wdich was agreed to. A resolution moved by the Rev. J. C. Carlile, a member of the Loudon School Board. and seconded by Mr. LyillPh Stanley, urging strenuous opposition to the de. menus which are being made by sopporters of Church of England and Roman Catholic schools was also passed. Mr. Stanley,in the course of his remarks, said he had ob- served the sort of dry rot which for years had been coming over the great mass of the Liberal members for Lancashire—how pusillanimous they were, and how sensitive they were te the pressure of the Roman Catho- lic section of their constituents. At a Conference in the evening, open to all the Society's friends, the Rev. R. C. itillingham, vicar of Hexton, suggested that the endow- ments of the Church might very well he used for provid- ing old age pensions. The Rey. J. Hirst Hollowell (Roc- d,o gave an address on the present duty of Liberation- ists in regard to national education. At the hearing of the Liberator case on Tuesday the Official Receiver stated that the total indebtednesa of the Balfour companies to the creditors was £5,327,650. and the capital nsid nu by the public 0,033,154—making a total of £8,360,000.</p>
