      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>'WARRINGTON TOWN COUNC I L.</head>
        <p>'WARRINGTON TOWN COUNC I L.</p>
        <p>,A meeting of this Council was held at the Town Mill on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Councillor Je.rairelough) presided, and there were also present _4ldermen Harrison, Wright, Sutton, Blackhurst, Itltehinson, Francomb, Bolton ; Councillore If ep- beerdi Monks, Beaman, Plumpton, Baird, Evans,Lee, Vrinett, Woods, Longton, Snillcock, Smethurst ;Pi Greening, Shaw-Green, Roberts, R. T. Fair- '4ollgb, Skelton, Cannel' ; Messrs. Whittle (town Clerk \ . ), Longdin (borough surveyor), Haddock (gas , ,711gineer), Madeley (librarian), Fairhurst (borough ' "reasurer), and S Wilson (assistant town clerk) THE WIDTH OF THE STREETS. ..eAlderman SUTTON moved and Alderman BLAcKEITIRST .70110leerl the adoption of the minutes of the Street ' .4213tOvement and Lighting Committee. ..Coancillor ROBERTS suggested that it was desirable !eat an additional lamp should be placed in Liverpool- Plld between Milner-street and Green-street. where the quidsoline Catholic chapel had been erected, as the road l'as badly lighted at that point. ,Councillor BENNETT asked what was being done with "Ord to a resolution proposed by him some time ago, rcl relative to the improvement of their streets. othing had been said about the matter since the passing e",tile resolution,and in the meantime boiling had been on all around them. He referred to that portion • Springfield-street where the committee had sanctioned • building of certain property, and asked whether the C°„erstaittee thought the width of the street would be "uequate when the building was completed. He had I heard many cemplaints that Winmarleigh-street would 1 4(4 in future years be frund wide enough for all pur- -1 and that it should have been 60 feet in width. tirugh he did not agree with this, he was of the opinion at Winmarleieh-street was not wide enough ; neither g°l he think that Springfield-street was wide enough. 2, moved an amendment that the portion of the ntlutes referred to should be sent back for further con- "aeration. e Councillor WoODS seconded the amendment, saying • as the Government were going to build very impor- tionit offices in this locality the Corporation ought now take the opportunity of securing a good approach. Alderman Sams, in replyime, slid that Councillor 40, berts' suggestion should have the consideration of e'lge committee. With respect to Couneiller Bennett's 'Pestion as to whether sufficient space had been re- lerved for the width of Springfield-St. he said it would be next to impossible for the Corporation to make the kreet wider, for they had no power to deal with the ~°,lteration in any way. The owner of the property in "e.,"at neighbourhood could build in defiance of them. e_‘‘Jries of" No.") He thought the committee had no l'ovier in this matter, and even if the Corporation did e..°lske any alteration in the street, the expense would VA be defrayed by the owners, the Council would have (0 bear it. Councillor WOODS: Is the Corporation to allow all 44tri of incongruities to take place just because it is tot--- Councillor Roamers cootended that Couneillnr Woods not in order. " All sorts of incongruities" were not •sentioned on the minutes. (Laughter, and hear, hear.) ~, The TOWN CLERK pointed out that as the owner of the vtonerty alluded to had complied with the bylaws he °Died build as he liked. „Councillor BENNETT replied that be was quite aware of (sat •' but as the improvement was a matter of such greatimportance he thought the Corporation might buy tilC land. .The amendment on being put was voted for by Coun- °ors Langton, Woods, and Bennett only, the remaining blembers of the Council voting against it. ....._The minutes were, therefore, carried. PROpOsED SMALLPDX HOSPITAL.—PROPERTY OWNERS AND THE CLOSING OF INSANITARY DWELLINGS. The minutes of the Health Committee contained the ort with regard to the proposed smallpox hospital Tinted in the "Examiner" last week, and also the fol- °Wing resolution :—" That in all proreedinga under the tifousing of the Working Classes Act if the owners of the , °Uses in question are willing to comply with the notice tved upon them no further action be tiken for 14 Sys if. in the meantime, the owners will employ a sur- tel'or and submit a report in writing stating the condi- efult of the premisee and the alterations and improve- • nts it is proposed to carry out." Alderman Simon in moving the confirmation of the inutes, said the subject of the smallpox hospital would aye to be gone into by the committee and would be 41,40 brought before the Council. -eoe uncillor ROBERTS seconded. t Councillor SHAW GREEN objected to the resolution re• t°rlng to the proceedings under the Working Classes °. He considered that when the Medical Officer 140demned property as unfit for habita ion' the borough eLlerveyor should examine the house and inform the L'wriers what alterations it was necessary to make. He *"oved that the resolution referred to should he rejected. ...Alderman BLACERITEST seconded this amendment,and that when an officer of the Corporation condemned Itilalonse he ahou'd give reasons for condemning it. Be lemlght it was ton had to compel property owners togo • the expense of repairing property at haphazard. ~ Alderman BOLTON also supported the amendment, and .06d that 14 days was toe short a notice to give property "Mers for repair'. eCouncillor Weems was astonished that any Committee 11°Stild place such a resolution on the agenda. Councillor ROBERTS said there was a serious misunder- titeAnding as to the meaning of the prmosal. It was p:Ill' intended to Resist prneerty owners, and the Health Mmittee enuld if it chose simply stand upon their :tosition under the Act of Parliament and give certain tics&amp; It was not property that belonged to working- Cr, ES one speaker had said, that came under the I:me of the Cenamittee for condemnation. This pro- e, rty in many cases belonged to gentlemen who could rell afford to put it into a condition fit for habitation. ;tl:',,r, hear and "oh, oh.") That statement could he verified by reference to the rate book. Why tticlald the borough surveyor he specially engaged for te: Property owners of Warrington? Be for one ob- Letted to the borough surveyor holding a brief for pro- , owners. (Rear, hear.) He defied any property 1 '''lli_Per to receive a notice and say truthfully that he was ; irtlY unable to get to know what to dn. There was ‘, t, Such notice given until property was not in a enndi- -01 to make it unfit for habitation. (Hear, hear.) teCouncillor PLVMPTON said that in going round with k`e'e Paving and Sewerage Committee they had come h Pose lots of cottages that were not really fit for human obitation, and all the membera expressed themselves telt, Strongly to the effect that the Health Committee w‘l+v! not been doing their duty in allewing finch houses to ii- inhabited. There were cottages in King-street and tltindry.at, which were not fit for dogs to he in. Yet Criers of that kind of property were bare-faced enough °stand up anti defend it. (Hear, hear.) I'lle amendment was supported by Councillor Lome- ir, Alderman Wanner, Alderman FeAncowee Alderman trTart, Neon, and Alderman HARRISON. fee'ile MAYOR : It strikes me that a crest many of you raltlemen are property owners. (Hear, hear, and 40elater.) e ap Alderman WRIGHT: There is no disgrace in that I ge. ' lderman Streneeer remarked that many of them pro- to be the friends of the ratepayers and property Oirners but by suppnrtir g this amendment they were in teall be ty the greatest Pi:Pt:lies of the ratepayers and pro- -9 owners for if that amendment waft carried, and t:l' minutes sent beck to the committee,the latter would ire to carry out the law as it stood. They must remem- that it wag not the duty of the Borough Surveyor 1,!..e m ,rramipe these dwelling hoes; it was the duty of the ."clical Officer, who afterwards reported to the cern- kittee that there were certain dwellings which were in all tr i • anitary state. It was then for the Corporation to I,r linen that report, and give notice to the owne ellanitary preperty. Alderman WRIGHT : Give notice of what? Alderman SUTTON : Of the insanitary cendition of the Con:Uses. Then, if the owners don't think lit to put them k_ a proper sanitary condition, the law says they are to ;7 brought up before the magistrates. Concluding, Al- i!tinan Sutton said that the committee were desirous. :. fact anaions,q-n assist the owners, hut when they were ed to instruct the Borough Surveyor to examine and Port, for the benefit of the owners, upon insanitary ellings, it was a little unreasonable. If it was de- this shnuld be done, they weuld have to appoint reral other borough eurveyors.fer the present borough ilrVeyor had as much as he ceuld do. The question had ci "ts fully considered in committee, and they had con- by ree,nmmendin the course embodied in the ee_4(l°ll er triVe resolution. If 28 dart would meet the needs of th; Owners, instead of 14, he was agreeable. on behalf of 47 committee, te consent to that alteration of the , hint,. .• ce HI le,e"uncillor GREENING supported the CoMittPe. He idered that it was Pimply menstrous for property ijo",etS to come there and mak that rine of the most im- tit "ant servants of the Corperatinn shnuld do private °tit. The next thing would he that the Borough Sur- , would he seked to give plans of cottages. el MaTert also supported the Committee, and said it ▪ ed as thnueb they wanted to take the bread out of . nee mouths of the surveyors and architects of the town. 'tart hear.) thpri being put to the vote, the amendment was lest by eelteen to ten. The minutes were then formally put Carried. be Sing GREEN next proposed that 28 days' notice ven to owner. of property. r., Ite TOWN CLERIC : Yen eannil put it in that form. Sterrore : You must give notice. • olleillor SMETHURST referring to the smallpox hospi- teeegaid that he thnlicht £3.000 wag a very large som , teet's spent upon a building for only 10 male and 10 le patients a rntderman SUTTON replied that the report WAS gimply_ ilaort ot the Medical Officer and the Borough Sur al% It had not been acted upon. UnalOt SMETHURST : But you have given us a Vidte number ..f pßti.ots. th: dertnan StrrroN : Bet we are not responsible for nee ',limber. We asked for a report and WP have got it. th: are net Inonnd to ace upon it. When we consider 11.7 mrellOtt fully we may make 50 augeeetions. (Hear, ✓. 1 ~</p>
        <p>ten of i</p>
        <p>In anqwer to Councillor BENNETT,</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON said the q I,tion of the joint hos- pital had for the time fallen in abeyance, as a site could not be found which would be suitable for all the authorities.</p>
        <p>The subject then dropped, and the minutes were agreed to.</p>
        <p>SANITARY WORKS COMMITTER.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this Committee which were of a ron. tine charaoter, were moved by Councillor MONKS, and se..:oncled by Councillor BEAMAN. ColDlCilhr RICHARD FAIRCLOUGH wished to allude to the collection of fish.</p>
        <p>The Mayer : It is not on the minutes. (Laughter.) G.UORIIIOr RICHARD FAIRCLOUGH : Bat it is about the he dth of the town. The MAYOR : I cannot allow it. You are out of or- der. (Laughter.) The minutes were passed. PAYING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE.—THE RIVER AT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WILDERSPOOL.</head>
        <p>WILDERSPOOL.</p>
        <p>Alderman WRIGHT moved the confirmation of the mimutes, which were published in our la,t issue, and al- luded to the minute with reference to the construction of the sewer He said it wag part of a permanent scheme, although the matter was not finally settled. The commit tee were waiting for the report of the Borough Surveyor. He thought that when the matter had been decided it would give satisfaction to the Council generally. (lien', hear.) Councillor FIEATON seconded the motion, and said the Council would see from the minutes that a tender had been accepted for the labour required in forming and paving St. James'-st. for the sum of £BO. The minutes were agreed to. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE—A CHANGE IN HOURS OF OFFICERS' ATTENDANCE. _ _</p>
        <p>COURCiikr SHAW GREEN moved the adoption of the minutes of the committee, and alluded to the question of the attendance of one of the school attendance officers. He said this had been done to suit the convenience of those fathers who wished to see Mr. Jones. It was be- lieved that in many cages the mothers did not tell the fathers about the irregular attendance of the children at school _ _ .</p>
        <p>„,. CntITICHIOr SHILLCOCIC seconded, and the motion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>MUSEUM, LIBRARY, ARTS AND TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE. Councillor ROBERTS moved the confirmation of the minutes which appeared in our last issue, and said the only reason the boxes presented by Mr. J. D. Buckton, had not been circulated was that at present they had not the money to do so. The specimens were for the pur- pose of giving instruction in natural history to children in the elementary schools. The boxes were r .ther expensive, but the school teachers might now bring their classes to the Museum, and the curator would be glad to assist the school teach,rs in giving the natural history lessons. The Education Department had consented to allow the attendance at the Museum to COW, t an attend- ance for the purposes of the grant. He declined to be a party to the management of any class which was not directly under the control of the Technical Instruction Committee, and in regard to which the money expended did not come through that committee, and therefore he was not a party to the formation of the plumbing CIORS. (Hear, hear.) As a committee they were not satisfied I with the treatment they were receiving from the County Council. Their grant had been seriously reduced, and if the County Council wished to have a class outside the Technical Instruction Committee they must take the management of it ; that was so far as he was concerned. (Hear, hear.) Councillar GREENING seconded, and the minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>lILRIEETS COMMITTEE.-TRE PROPOSED SITE FOR THE</p>
        <p>NOVEMBRR FAIR.</p>
        <p>Alderman BLACICHURST moved the confirmation of the minutes of this committee which appeared in our last issue. Referring to the question of horse and nettle fair, he said he did not think the committee should do away with them altogether. A temporary place in which to hold them had been found in Orford-lane. Alderman HUTCHINSON seconded. . . .</p>
        <p>Councillor GREENING contended that the land in ques- tion had been bought for the purpose of an open space for the public, and should not be used for horse and cow fairs.</p>
        <p>Alderman BIACIEHURST said the arrangement was only a temporary ens. Councillor SKELTON contended that the resolution as it appeared on the minutes were different to the wording as it was agreed to by the committee. The question- arose as to where the neat fair should he held, and it was decided that it should he held on land recently pur chased inn) the trustees of the late Mr. Bolton.</p>
        <p>.. The MAYOR: Reference is made only to the November fairs.</p>
        <p>The minutes were then paved. FIRE ENGINE CONMITEEE.-.-THE REPORT OF THII SELECT</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Alderman HUTCHINSON moved, and Councill:r SignTrroasT seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor BENNETT said he had ben informed that the Select Committee appointed Some time ago to inquire into the condition of the Fire Brigade had sent in its re- port, and he wished to know why it did not appear on the agenda paper. The MAYOR: This is nct on the minutes. Councillor BINNETT : I am asking why it is not on the minutes.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: You will get it later on. Councillor SKELTON : But we may not all be here then. (Laughter.) rhp minutes were then agreed to. GENERAL PURPOSES COSTMITTEL—THE PARR HALL CHARGES, AND BILL POSTING QUESTION. The Mayon moved, and Alderman &amp;Amman. seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor LONGTON said that he and others were very pleased to have had pined in their posoeooian the re- port with reference to Parr Hall and he con 4ratulated the committee on the proceedings in elnneotion with the opening of the hall. There had been a loos, but he was told that it would be recouped on a future occasion. He shouldliko to ask if the Parr Hall Committee had beou' appointed by the Town Council. The Dlerolt : By the General Purposes Committee, confirmed by the Town Council. Councillor LONGTON : I don't remember it. _</p>
        <p>..... . The. M&amp;YOU : The committee will bo re-appointed like ether committees on the 9th November. The Town Clerk says the appointment is an annual one. Councillor LONGTON : I hope the committee will be appointed by the Council, and not by the General Pur- poses Committee. • The MAYOR: The Parr Hall Committee is a sub-com- mittee of the General Purposes Committee and you might as well say that the whole of the other sub-com- mittees should be appointed by the Council. Councillor LINGTON : This is a different committee, it has the control of a public gift and I shall always pro- test against it. The MAYOR : You are wasting time Councillor Long,- ton. We cannot sit here all night listening to you. I will not for one.</p>
        <p>The Tows/ Cuiag here read the resolution showing that the appointment of the Parr Hall Committee had keen confirmed by the general body of the Council. Councillor Woos asked how it was that the report of the Parr Hall Committee had been kept back until the last moment in such a systematic manner. They were told that there was a loss of nearly £lOO on the opening 'of the Parr Hall. He considered that it was gross mis- management on the part of someone that that should have arisen. He knew something about getting up good entertainments, and he said without fear of contradic- tion that the matter had been grossly mismanaged. Had the sub-committee any ground to purchase a grand piano? Was there any mention in the minutes read be the Town Clerk of the sub-committee letting, or sub- letting, or giving away two valuable costing stations? No. If he were Mr. Grossinith, who had taken the hall for Saturday night, he should want to go to that posting company who would do the posting the cheap- est, and he should ask every posting company in War- rington to give him ati estimate. Had the sub-committee the power to give away that right to any one who en- gaged the hail? He said " No, ' and he proposed as an amendment "That the minutes of the General Purposes Committee be confirmed with the exception of the report of the Parr Hall Sob-Committee." He had the beat in- terests of Warrington at heart, and always had. The six members of the Committee had been appointed lay 26 or 28 members of the Council, but what about the other eight or ten left off. It was a remarkable thing that out of those eight or ten members, some of them had mere to do with public entertainments than any of the Council. If there had been any emoluments from it, he hoped they had enjoyed it. Councillor LONGToN tOge to second the amendment, but was uot allowed by the Mayor, as he had spoken before. Councillor SMETHURST hereupon seconded the amend- ment. It was, he said,very strange that the Corporation should become caterers for the public of Warrington against those who had expended money in building for the same purpose. They were acting in direct opposition to the public entertainers in getting up Saturday night concerts. They might as well shut up the Co-operative Hall and the Gymnasium. With oegard to the bill-post- ing, it was not fair to force a man who took the hall to do what was proposed. Those who came to the Hall ought to post their hills where they thought tit. Alderman Hungnam; thought that those who came to the Hall ought not to have their hands tied with regard to the posting. The MAYOR : Are you a shareholder in a bill posting company? Alderman Flurcunisow : No, I am not. Councillor ShIRTHURST : Nor I either, Mr. Mayor. Alderman HUTCHINSON (continuing) said that he thought that the Corporation ought not to do anything to injure any entertainment, or run counter to any other enterprise in the ton. Councillor Sitevrox : I wish you would close the dis- cussion, Mr. Mayor. I want to go canvassing. (Laughter and applause.) The amendment was then put to the vote, hut was lost, only Councillors Woods. Smethurst and Longton voting for it. The minutes of the committee were then carried.</p>
        <p>VOTH OF TRANKS TO YRS MAYOR. Alderman HARRISON moved a vote of thanirs to the Mayor for his services during the past twelve months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>EXAMINER, *eVTURDAY, NOVIVIEBER 2 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, *eVTURDAY, NOVIVIEBER 2 1895.</p>
        <p>I He had, he said,no hesitation in saying that anyone who took exception to the resolution would manifest a great want of knowledge of the manner in which his Worship had acted. (Hear, hear.) To undertake the duties of the Mayor of a borough must at all times be very ardu- ous, but under the circumstances they had been pecu- liarly arduous in Warrington,for in addition to his ordin- ary duties, which were by no means light, Councillor JatnesFaircloug,b had no fewer than three separate and distinct functions to undertake, and these had been carried out with the greatest satisfaction to the public generally. (Hear, hear, and applause.) To these im- portant functions, namely, the Parliamentary election, the opening of the Town Hall gates, and the opening of Parr Hall, Alderman Harrison referred, and in conclusion said they were by no means unmindful of the graceful manner in which the Mayoress had acted, giving his Worship all the support and assistance she could during his terra of office. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Councillor ROBERTS seconded the motion, which, he ' geld, had been very ably and eloquently moved by Alderman Harrison. Every word the Alderman had utterad that night found an echo in the heart of every member sitting round the table. He wished to allude to a curious omission in the report with regard to the P.irr Hall. It was intended that the words " James Fairclough, Mayor," sheuld follow the inscription to be affixed on the Hall. (Hear, hear.) The TOWN CLERK : rhat is my omission. Instructions have been given for the name to be affixed. (Hear, hear.) Councillor ROBERTS (continuing), mentioned the fact that his Worship had made the unprecedented number of 237 attendances at the various meetings of the Cor- poration. (Applause.) He did not think any Mayor of recent years had made anything like so many attend- ances. (Hear, hear.) He also endorsed every word which had been said by Alderman Harrison with reference to the Mayoress—(applause)--who had endeared herself to all the inhabitants of the town by the kindness,geniality, and courtesy she had shown to all,no matter what his or her position was. (Applause.) Alderman SUTTON expressed a hope that his Worship would consent to be elected Mayor for the ensuing year, also that he would be elected an alderman. (Hear,hear, and applause.) Councillor GREENING said his Worship had treated the Council fair and square, and he had brought them up, and had called them to order when they had raised questions which had "not been on the minutes." (Lauehter.):He had also admirably discharged the duties of Mayor outside the Town Hall, and although what he wan saying was not on the minutes —(renewed laughter) —he hoped to have the opportunity of speaking to a similar resolution next year. (Applause.) Councillor Sriew GREEN also spoke to the motion. He said that one matter ha+l net been mentioned in the dis- cusaion, and that was the proposed extension of the borough by which the dream of several years passed had been accomplished. It had been hoped that the Mayor would have been the first Mayor over the borough as ex- tended. . .</p>
        <p>The motion was carried with acclamation. The MAYOR, in reply, said that he was very much ob- Heed to the Council for the resolution they had passed. At the same time he should like to thank them all for the uniform kindness he had received at their hands while he had been Mayor. He heard something about , "ripples on the water." Certainly they had net got throuah the proceedings without a few ripples, but it I did not matter so long as the ripples did not become too big. (Hear, hear and laughter). They had all signed the requisition asking him to become Mayor for another year. Well, he considered they had paid him a very high compliment, because they had Tories and Ra.dicals in the Council, and had all signed the requisition. He felt the compliment very deeply. He told the Town Clerk at the time that he thought they ought to give him a rest and appoint someone else. At some future time he would not mind being in office again. However, he would not give an answer that night but would wait and see the result of--(Cries of" Oh, oh.") He was not joking. Councillor FIEPHERD Put us out of our misery. The Mayon Fie wished they could in these municipal elections do away with politics,because he believed they were doing slot of harm by introducing them. When an election came on they went at it like "Kilkenny cats," and created a lot of ill-feeling in the town. He wished they would see their way to drop that sort of thing. What had politics to do with the widening of :a street, the enlarging of mains, the improvement and the cheapening of the gas, and making the town healthy, sweet and clean? (Applause.) He did not mind telling ' them that he was a Tory, but hoped the electors would send members to the Council,be they Tories or Radicals, who were efficient men to conduct the affairs of the town. (Applause.) He was desirous of thanking the whole of the Corporation officials for the great help they had given him during his year of office, and more parti- cularly the Town Clerk for the sound advice and able assistance he had Riven him. He had practically taken all the onerous duties off Isis (the Mayor's) shoulders, and he was pleased to have that opportunity of pub- licly thanking him. (Applause.) Be thanked all for the resolution just proposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>UNDISPUTED bfINIITHS.</head>
        <p>UNDISPUTED bfINIITHS.</p>
        <p>The tninutes of the Watch Committee, the Paving and Sewerage Committee, the Cemetery Committee, the Town Hall, Park, and Baths Committee,Gas Committee, Rivers Committee, and Water Committee, were passed without discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ENGLAND'S FAMOUS COLONISER AND IRRIGATION EXPERT.</head>
        <p>ENGLAND'S FAMOUS COLONISER AND IRRIGATION EXPERT.</p>
        <p>Probably few men who commenced at the bottom of the ladder have become more widely and favoJrahly known or mere successful than Mr. A. F. Spawn, of 122, Pall Mall. That he is a man of great resource cannot be donbted when we read that he was the original inventor of the Hand Grenade Fire Extinguisher, also many other inventions of minor importance, and recently perfected a scientific hot air apparatus for curing and preserving fruits and vegetables on a large, practical, and economi- cal scale, which is being adopted, not only in England, but Holland, Australia, and America. Mr. Spawn was appointed by a Syndicate some years ago to investigate the precess of growing and preserving fruits in Australia and while there stated a well-organised and successful irrigation colony in that country. In fa.t, as a public lecturer and adviser on colonisation and irrigation he Probably stands to-day unrivalled. Business has brought him in contact with people of skill in every part of the globe, and naturally he has been through many adven- tures, some pleasing and others dangerous ; but the most astonishing adventure he recently experienced right here in London, and im- immediately after his return from an extend,d tour of immigration investintion on the Pacific Coast of America. Mr. Spawn's own words explain clearly how he was attacked, and to vhe escaped. "1 had always always been uncommonly strong and healthy, and have not known a day's illness during my past ten years' tra- vels and adventures, and was always able to scoff at disease, but a few weeks ago, on leaving my office at Pall Mall, I was overcome with giddiness, and this re- curred frequently; during the next few days; I began to notice a bitter taste in my mouth, and my tongne seemed coated and burning; my appetite left me—could not enjoy the greatest delicacy, everything I ate seemed to distress me, and caused spasmodic pains in the pit of my stomach, and flatulency ; my complexion turned sallow, and the customary office duties required more exertion than I could seemingly command. Mr ailment also see rn ed to make me nervous, and tended to cause melan. chilly and gloomy feelings. I tried chemists' and doc- tors medicines, and kept growing worse. I had alwaye derided medicines outside the regular channels, and although I bad heard Warner's Safe Cure favourably spoken of for Liver diseases, not only in England, but Australia and America, I never thought of using it ; but fortunately a friend who knew of my suffering succeeded in overcoming my prejudice, and I decided to try it. Almost from the first dose the dis- agreeable symptoms began to lessen in severity. In a few days I was able te enjoy light food, and after using four bottles all the above described symptoms had en- tirely disappeared. I know now what to do if my liver Frets out of order, or if there are signs of an approaching bilious attack, and certainly can recommend this medi- cine to others with candour and confidence." There can be no question as to the soundness of the views ex- pressed or advice given by a gentleman of Mr. Spawn's standine, and your experience will be just as satis- factory if you are guided thereby.</p>
        <p>Commenting on the way in which men of mark now contribute to the Press in various ways, the "Spectator" says:—" We do not doubt that Messrs. Longmans or Mr. Murray c3uld, if they tried, issue a number with one paper by the Bing of the Bel- gians on his 'Object in Buying the Congo,' a second by Sir William Harcourt on • The Overthrow of the Rosebery Government,' a third by Mr. Rhodes on ' The Secret History of the Conquest of Matabele- land,' a fourth by M. Hanotaux on 'French Plans for the Future,' a fifth by M.Lobanoff on 'Russia in the Far East,' a sixth by Lord Kelvin on ' The Hopes of Science,' a seventh by one of the Vander- hilts on ' American ; their Joys and orrows,' an eighth by Count lto on The Aspira- tions of Japan Real and Imaginary,' and a ninth by Cardinal Gibbons on 'The Expansibility of the Papacy,' and perhaps a tenth by Lord Dufferin on ' My Political Acquaintances,' though the time for the last-named, to be ia hardly yet."</p>
      </div>
