      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>OLD COUNTRY DOCTOR.</head>
        <p>THE OLD COUNTRY DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>Many and many a night when a boy have I heard the wheels of my father's carriage roll out of the yard into the street as he departed upon a visit to some distant patient—often several miles away. weer a rough and dismal road. For he was a country doctor and a good man, and often answered a call when be knew there was no urgent necessity. "If people had any ides about there own bodies," be would say to me, "they could treat themselves as well as I can. In nine cases out of ten I use but one class of medicines, anyway, and the only trouble is we haven't yet got held of the right thing, even among them."</p>
        <p>What he meant: was—but you (the reader) will under- stand better what he meant when I have told you what happened a few years later. And curious enough it was too. For if anything in the world would stir up my father's disgust and wrath f rltn the very bottom, it was when he heard somebody praise any medicine made or administered by a wonian. "Let the woman sew on buttons, cook, and mind their children." he would shout. "and let medicine alone ; they'll kill more people than they'll cure."</p>
        <p>He was inconsistent, you say, and denounced the very thing he at other times advocated. Exactly so ; but who is consistent P Then, too, when I asked him how the doctors could contrive to live if the people doctored themselves, he would laugh and answer, " We won't worry over that, my boy, for they will never dare to risk it. Every time they get a pain they will send for the doctor,whether they ever pay him or not," he added a little bitterly ; "but the time will come when the people will find out what most diseases really sum up at, and somebody will find a remedy for one fundamen- tal trouble. When that comes to pass,and lam sent for to attend patients outside ef the village, I simply won't go. I'll send you with a bottle of the medicine, and I'll stay at home and take life easier in my old age."</p>
        <p>Now read the following extracts from two letters. " I was always strong and hearty," says 'uthe first, " up to the spring of 1880. Then I broke down. Poor appetite, bad taste in the mouth, pain after eating, flatulance, retention of the kidney secretion, and great weakaeis were among my symptoms. Month after month I was in this way, and no medical treatment helped me. I Finally my sister told me of a medicine, which I took, land was soon as well as ever. I keep it in the house aow, and it has saved me pounds in doctoring. (Signed) Mrs. Hannah Dixon, 18, Cross Keys, Idle, Bradford, December 18th,1892." The second writer describes how he suffered from the I same symptoms and was cured by the same remedy. He adds: "During the time we have used it we have never bad any occasion for a doctor. (Signed)tWilliam Osborn, 38, High-street, Plumstead, near London, Dec. 2nd, 1882." Both these cases were of indigestion and dyspepsia,thel malady which my good father said he hoped somebody would finally discover a remedy for. "For if we can cure that we can cure almost any disease," he said, "as they are only different names for the same thing." The dear old man is in his grave now, and therefore there is no harm in my letting out the truth. He lived to hear of and appreciate the very medicine he used to allude to us as among the possibilities of the futuremnd many a time has he sent me to a patient with a bottle of —what do you think P—Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, a remedy discovered and prepared by a woman!</p>
        <p>He was not consistent, you say ? No, but he was better. He was honest, and gave up a prejudice when he saw how foolish it was.</p>
        <p>B. W. C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>A. DREADFUL LYNCHING SCENE IN</head>
        <p>A. DREADFUL LYNCHING SCENE IN</p>
        <p>TEXAS. A NEGRO BURNED TO DEATH.</p>
        <p>----.-- - - NEW YORK, WEDNESDAY.—According to intelligence received here from Tyler Texas, a negro named Henry Hilliard was burned at the stake on a public square at four o'clock yesterday afternoon. The man had violated and murdered a white woman named Mrs. Bell, and had then mutilated the body. The negro was pursued by bloodhounds &amp;ad captured. He confessed his guilt,and was being conveyed to gaol by the police officers when an armed mob of three hundred persons seized him and carried him to the scene of the murder. The crowd, which rapidly increased to two thousand, appointed a court to decide upon the form of punishment. They re- solved that the prisoner should be burned in the public square. Hilliard, while awaiting the decision, wrote to his wife a note of farewell,which he gave to the sheriff. He said :—" I have been arrested by Wig Smith. You know what they will do to me. If I don't see you any rnore,,^goodbye. He was subsequently marched to the main square, on which 7,000 persons were assembled, including many women, children, and negroes. Waggons were stationei round the square, and these as well as trees, buildings, and every ether point of vantage, were crowded with sightseers, numbers of whom arrived specially from the country round. A scaffold was erected in the centre of the square, and upon it were piled faggots, coat and straw, which were drenched with oil. The negro was seen to srpak but his words were inaudible. He subsequently prayed in a loud voice, which was heard for some distance. He was lashed to an iron rail, and when all preparations were complete Bell, the husband of the murdered woman, ap- plied the match to the inflammable material. Flames immediately burst forth and enveloped the negro, who was heard crying for mercy. The faggots, however, did not ignite well, and after burning for a short time al- most died out,the man being still alive. The fire was re- lighted, and after fifty minutes of torture the wretched negro died.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
