      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>ST. ANN'S SALE OF WORK.</head>
        <p>ST. ANN'S SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>A sale of work to discharge liabilities, to meet current expenses, and to supplement the mission nurse fund in connection with St. Ann's parish, was opened in the Dannett-street schools on Thursday by the Mayoress (Mrs. James Fairclough). The ladies of the St. Ann's congregation have been hard at work for some time past, and the result of their labours was seen on Thursday at the sale of work, for the stalls were filled to their utmost capacity with a large number of articles, both of prac- tical and ornamental value. There was a large number of stalls, which had been erected by Councillor W. J. Shaw, and each stall had its name placed above it in old English characters, this being the work of Mr. Naylor. The stalls were also tastefully bedecked with art muslin, and the centre of the room was covered with plants which added considerably to the appearance. The fol- lowing is a list of the stall holders :—Church stall : The Misses Davies (vicarage), Mrs, F. Taylor, Mrs. Randall, Mrs. Glover and Miss Parker; Orford stall: Mrs. Plunkett, Mrs. Quarter Master Sergeant Taylor; Win- wick stall: Mrs. Shaw, Mrs. Savage, Mrs. W. J. Shaw, Miss Downey, Miss C. Roberts; school stall, junior school: Miss M. Rigby and teachers ; infant school: Miss Rigby, Miss R. Bunch, Miss Thompson and teachers ; refreshment stall: Miss Johnson (Froghall-lane), Miss Hughes and Miss Johnson (Orford-lane) ; book and general stall ; Mrs. Winter ; flower stall : Miss R. Rigby and Misses Bunch; sweet stall Miss Marion Randall. Wax works had been provided for the amusement of the ' ViSitoTB, and they were under the care of the day school teachers and others, and there was also a museum in charge of Mr. Bretherton. Concerts were held at inter- vals, the organiser on Thursday being Mr. Percy Randal's, the late organist, and on Friday, Mr. Padmore, the present organist. THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>There was a good number present on Thursday at the opening ceremony, and among these present,in addition to the Mayoress, were the Revs. J. Davies, P. J. Byrne, B. Percival, and E. C. E. Carleton, Mr. F. Taylor, and Mr. Naylor.</p>
        <p>Previous to calling upon the Mayoress to declare the sale of work open, the Rev. J. DAviss said that there were so many sales o work, and bazaars held in War- rington, that it would be necessary to give a few words of explanation with regard to the present one. Every one knew that St. Ann's parish was not a rich narish,and that they were obliged to go outside to enable them to discharge their liabilities. He did not believe in going in debt and as they had several items of expenditure to meet it was necessary that the sale should be held. Their church had been notorious for its draughtiness, and a short time ago they had spent £3O to remedy this de- fect. It was also alsolutely necessary that the various p irochial organisations should be assisted, and also that the nurse fund should be supported. lie might remind them that, besides his stipend, the total expenditure in parochial organisations amounted to £220. He was thankful to say that £7O per annum was received from, the Church Pastoral Aid Society,and that Mrs. Beamont and the the Bishop of Liverpool each kindly contributed £5 a year, and an orwanisation in connection with the parish also raised £26. Mr. Davies also went on to speak of the nurse fund, and hoped that they might be able to get a good sum for this as well as other organisa- tions. He then called upon the Mayoress to declare the bazaar open, expressing regret that the Mayor could not be present owing to a cold.</p>
        <p>The MAYORESS-, who was received with applause, said that she had great pleasure in being there that day. She hoped that they would soon empty their stalls, and she also said that the Mayor regretted very much that he could not be present. She then declared the bazar open. Mr. FRANK TAYLOR proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayoress, and said that it was so long ago as IEB2 since the last sale of work had been held in the parish. He hopPd that the present sale would be a successful one.</p>
        <p>Mr. NAYLOR seconded the motion and spoke in eulogis- tic terms of the Mayoress, and the motion was carried unanimously, after which the more serious business of the sale was commenced. FRIDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The opening ceremony on Friday was performed by Mrs. Beamont, and there were also present the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis), Revs. J. Davies, and P. J. Byrne, Captain Buckton, and Mr. R Davies.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. DAVIES said that the takings of the pre- , vious day were very satisfactory, as they amounted to I £5B 48.11 d., which together with subscriptions received, money received from a concert recently held, and money collected from the junior and infant schools, made a total of £BO 4s. sid. (Applause.) Re considered that this was a result ot which they should be thankful. To go into details' the stereoscopic views, the previous day, realised is. 61., the cloak room, 43., museum, 12s. 4d., concerts, 13s. 4d., wax works, 303., sweet stall, £1 03 30., flower stall, £1 1.65. 100., junior school stall, £2 12s. ld., refreshment stall, £3 2s. id., also from the junior stall, £2 14s. 10d., book and general stall, £4, in- fant school stall, £4 is. 61.,a150 £3 73. 2d. received from the children in the school, Winwick stall, £6, Orford stall, £6, and church stall, £23 lie. 30. Referring to Mrs. Beamont and her family, he said that the St. Arms, parish was particularly indebted to them, as on looking on the past records he found that the late Mr. Beamont gave £2,468 towards the building fund of the church,and also endowed it to the extent of £303 per annum. It was also not generally known that two years ago Mrs. Beamont kindly endowed the church with £3,000 to al- low of the formation of various organisations in the parish. Re thought that Mr. Beamont's portrait ought to be placed in the vestry of the church, and also that a parsonage be erected as a memorial to the Beamont family. Mr. Davies then proceeded to describe in detail various improvements which he considered necessary, and to whilh any balance over the sum which was necessary for tt,e object proper of the bazar would be devoted. He then asked Mrs. Beamont to formally open the sale of work.</p>
        <p>Mrs. BELMONT said that she was glad to he present to have nn opportunity of congratulating the parishioners of St. Ann's in having such an excellent pastor as Mr. Davies, who had bravely met all the impediments and difficalties of that long neglected pariah. She was glad that he had been so successful in his work. She had great pleasure in declaring the bazaar open. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. GLOVER proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs Beamont, and said that he thought that the sunshine in that parish was beginning to break forth and that had been brought about to a large extent by Mrs. Beamont's interest in the church. The Rev. P. J. BYRNE seconded the vote of thanks as Mrs. Ber,mont had shown such a warm sympathy with the parish, and he could endorse everything which had been said about her.</p>
        <p>The vote was passed unanimously, and the formal proceedimrs then terminated. We desire to draw attention to the fact that the sale of work will be continued by special and general solicita- tion to-day (Saturlay), opening at five o'clock. The stalls are still loaded with usefulornamental, pretty and valuable articles at reasonable prices.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SHIP CANAL</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL</p>
        <p>THE PORT OF MANCHESTER: LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>During the week ended Friday, October 25, 169, sea- going vessels with an aggregate net registered tonnage: of 43.388 tons, arrived at and sailed from the various decks, wharfs, tco., on the Ship Canal. The arrivals and mailings during the preceding week numbered 144, and the aggregate net tonnage was 31,488 tons, showing an increase in last week's traffic: of 25 Shirr and 11,900 registered tons. There was an increase of 2,508 regi&lt;tered tone in the foreign traffic., and of 25 shins and 9.392 tons in the coastwise traffic. Amongst the cargoes im- ported were over a score each of China clay and various kinds of stone, five of pigiron, two of bath brick, and one each of timber and fruit. Three cargoes of salt, six of coal, and a large consignment of machinery were ex- ported. Amongst the timber cargoes recently entered from abroad were 1.350 loads of sawn pitchpine, ex the Norma, from Mobile ; 750 tons of pit props, ex the Llethea (a), from SundswAll ; 650 standards of deals, ex the Greenock, from Bay Ter, e ; and 1,750 loads of pulp- wood from Finland ex the steamer Firdene. ACCIDENr AT SILTPORT„</p>
        <p>A member of the crew of the N,rw—eeian barque Norma, named Pasnus Rusmusen, met with sariona in- juries on Friday. He was assisting to discharge the timber when a log which was being slune, canted, and struck him.ertishing him between it and the other Um- her. Dr. Selby, of Frodsham, was caled to attend him, and on examination found that he had sustained serious internal injuries. On Saturday mernin s, the captain,E. Anderson, had him conveyed in the ship's boat to Run- corn, where the aggishanrA of Mr. Shaw, of the S.Namen' Institute, was requisitioned, and he assisted to convel him to the Atation, and thence by tr An to the Rota Infirmary, LioPrnonl. Mr. William R 4nkg. of ilicth-ss". member of tho St. John Ambulance Association, also ac companied the unfortunate man, and did all he possibly could to make the journey as easy and comfortable as possible. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ACCIDENT AT BARTON.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT BARTON.</p>
        <p>At Barton Lo—clr;;;;'—'iu. e-Wrilst.y—WWinr;rl an accident occurred which occasioned serious damage to the steam tug Li herator, owned by the Weston Point Towinq Com. pans'. The tug was assisting the new Clan liner Clan Macrae on her voyage down the canal, being Flta tintrd astern. On orriying at Carton,through soma misunder- etandin7 or mismanagement, the ateamer and the tug Caine into collision. The stern of the Clan Macrae, which was deeply laden, oteve in the side of the tug amidships. and the latter had to be taken to Runcorn, where she arrived in a sinking condition. The Clan Macrae, which is a steamer of 1,664 tons net register,was little the worse for the collision. She tied no at Barton for the night, and reurned . her storage to Bombay on Wednesday morniug,leaying the canal in the afternom</p>
        <p>A high diver named Patric* Ring, Fininng whnse ex- pinita are number/0 jamnv frnm Blaektriars Bride int the Thames and from Brooklyn Bridee in'o the EA9 • River, has suvtained fatal injuries by jumping into the Hudson river at Poughkeepsie.</p>
        <p>"PRACTICALLY DEAD."</p>
        <p>HOW A TRADE UNION TREATED ITS SICK MEMBER. REMARKABLE STATEMENTS.</p>
        <p>The "Market Flarborough Advertiser "reports a case of interest to rill Trade Union men and their families, in the form of a statement taken down by a reporter fram Mr. George Bilton, Willow Cottage, Pelham-at., Lincoln. Mr. Bilton, was four years ago " paid out "of the Boiler-maker's Society, as practically dead. There is no ground for supposing that the Society dealt otherwise than fairly with him. " Three doctors." said he, " examined me, and pr:)- nounced me unfit for further work, and so the Society paid me out, which, of course, they would only do after they were convinced that I was incurably crippled, aud that medical science could do nothing for me." It is rnp-irtant to note this,in view of Mr. Bilton's subsequent history. " After that," continues the statement, " I went into Lincoln hospital. I used to suffer excruciat- ing pain. I determined to leave. This I did against the wishes of the el.ctors—the torment I had suffered bad made me careless,and I seemed to court death." Asked as to the previous course of hie diseaae, Mr. Bitten said : " Fifteen years ago I was working in Lon- donderry. I was taken very bad, and the doctor said if wished to get home alive I had better go at once. A. year and seven months elapsed before I could do another day's work. Since then I have been a chronic invalid I have spent pounds and pounds in medicine, to no cur- pose. During the last six years I have only worked for about three months. I belonged to the Boiler-makers' Society, and the sick pay I received, eked out with what little I had saved, kept me and my family from t arving." The above, therefore, was the record of this " prac- tically dead" man—a man of high respectability and great intelligence and integrity. At the time of the statement, so far from being practically dead, he was working an foreman on the construction of a gasometer at a small private gas-works at Gumley, near Market Harborough, and he appeared per- fectly well and strong. " I bad a good constitu- tion," said he, in explanation of this apparent miracle, " and took a good deal of killing." But it was not his good constitution which saved him from a lingering, death. Note what follows. " One day I read about Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People,and remarked to my wife that I should like to try them. She said it was useless,as I had tried everything almost. However, I wrote to Dr. Williams for a box of the Pills, and some were seat me. I soon began to feel the good effects of taking them. Now, as you:eee, I am bale and hearty. Of course I could not at erg take on a very heavy job, but this one suits me all right. On Sunday I walked all round the adjaining villages, a thing I could not have, done a few months ago."</p>
        <p>" What particular effect did the Pills have upon you ?" "The first effect was that they increased my appetite snd my weight. Before having them I could not eat any meat ; now,l can.take anything, and what is more keep it down. I have increased over,s stone and • half in weight, and feel a different man altogether." "Then you think the public should know of this ?" "y. Dr. Williams has done me very much good and I should be ungratef al if I did not acknowledge the truth. One young man I know had two boxes of . Pills and was doing well ; then he went to some drug store for a third box. but ;was persuaded to try some pills of their owa make, ' very similar to Dr.% illiams but cheaper.' lie grew worse after taking them, and has been glad to go back to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. My nephew here can bear me out." Thus appealed to, a young fellow who stood by con- firmed the absolute truth of all that had been said. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills cure paralysis, locomotor ataxy, neuralgia, rheumatism, St. Vitus' dance, nervous headache, and indigestion ; diseases of the blood,such a scrofula, chronic erysipelas, and restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow col health. They are s specific for all troubles peculiar to the female sex, and in men cure all cases arising from wory, overwork, or ex- cesses. Sold by all chemists, and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 48, Eiolborn-viaduct, London, at 2s. 9d. a box, or biz for 13s. 9d. Genuine only withfull name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale Peeple.</p>
        <p>HOW THE ARMENIANS ARE MURDERED. APPALLING NARRATIVE HY AN EYE WITNESS. COLOGNE, OCT. 25. A native of Cologne, who was on board the Austrian Lloyd steamship Venus writes to the " Holnische Zeitung " from Trebizonc; on the Bth just, giving as an eye-witness some details of the massacre of the Armenians at Trebizmaci. lie writes :—" At seven o'clock this morning our steamer anchored close to the town of Treisizond. After all the passengers had landed except the three Europeans, of whom I was ono, and the un- loading of the cargo had begun, the sound of a shot came from the eastern side of the town not far from the share. All the Turks on board looked to see what was going on. Soon shuts were fired in quick succes- sion, snd then the Turks, leaving everythins, as it was, jumped into their boats and rowed in wild "haste to the shore. The whole town was clearly in a state of great excitement. People were running about in all direc- tions. A moment later an Armenian rushed Iran- : =ally round the corner of the Custom House, making for the shore, when be was struck by a bullet and fell to the graund before my eyes. The military came up, and with their rifles loaded marched through the streets. Close to where the fallen Armenian lay another one was seized and thrown into the sea. But toe water was not deep enough to drown him, and his head appeared above the surface. The people then began to hurl stones at the unfortunate wretch, and one struck him on the head, so he sank, but he soon again rose to the surface,when a new volley oilstones was poured upon him. 'This,however, did not suffice,asid at length a Turk got into a boat, and, rowing out to him, smashed in his skull, The excitement in the town increased every moment. The soluiers were firing dow.. from the fort- ress unceasingly, and cavalry patrolled the streets, and now another Armenian WaS caught on the shore and beaten to death by ten to twelve boatmen with their oars. At the same 1.111113 a boat with three Armenians put out, hotly pursued, and there began a race for life and death. Scarcely a huadred metres from us lay a Russian steamer, which was Use goal of the fugitives. As they came near to her tile lurks gave up the chase, but tien the most horrible thing happened. Brutally and inhu- manly the people on board the steamer threw the Armenians down the ship's ladder, kicking them with their feet. When the Turks saw this they pushed forward with renewed courage, and beat the Armenians to death. Filled with horror, I turned my eyes from the scene, but only to encounter a fresh spectacle. Three Turkish boatmen reized an Ar- menian—one of their mates—threw him out of the boat into the water, beat him about the head with their oars, and finished up by holding him underneath the water with an oar until he was drowned. Then' a 5 if nothing had happened, the Turks rowed quietly and slawly back to laud. On the Eastern shore an Armenian was shot dawn, but he was only wounded, for he tried to get up. When the Turks saw this they gathered round him, and whenever he eudeavoured to rise they hurled stones at him. This lasted halt an hour, until at length a Turk fractured his skull with a iarge stone. So horrible was the spectacle that 1 had continually to look away. The places of business were, ot course, all closed, but the Turks, alter a time Wake them open and began plunder- ing them, throwing the goods out of the windows Into boats below. " Firing went on the whole morning, and we awaited with great anxiety.the return of a gentleman from Vielana will, had landed early. Towards three o'clock he reached the Venus safely, This is the story which he had to tell :—"I had scarcely reached the hotel and was about to put my things in order when I heard shots, and looking out of the window saw soldiers marching through the streets with rifles on their shoulders. A tow minutes later the hotel keeper, a Greek, was brought streaming with blood into the house. He had been wounded in the streets. At half-past two, went in the company of an officer and two soldiers down to the quay. 'rhe street is thickly strewn with corpses, the strops of the Armenians are closed or plun- dered, women are screaming, children are crying for their parents. Whole families have been destroyed. Two thousand women and children are in the Jesuits' Hos- pital. Six hundred Armenians if not moie have been killed." Writing later the correspondent says The bodies remained in the roadway a day and a half. Two days after the massacre I walked through the town, and my feet were wet with the blood of Christians, for the pools of gore were often so close together that it was lin- possihle to avoid them. The Austrian Consul bad on that ' day 200 worlen and children in his charge. Of the Twits only five men fell, a sign that the Armenians were unarmed and taken by surprise.l am prepared to assume every responsibility for what I have related." 'I hen comes the following postcript :—" 1 must add that the rising has broken out in the whole neighbourhood. On a hill near Trebizmad are some Armenian settlements. tried (yi tweersecsauprer uansd esdh oatn dd swe nt fi r ird teh. e Ap ne po lnee whoere burned alive. In the evening the columns of fire rose high into the sky Austrian Lloyd steaamnedr hbagstitbed eenudpettaiiiileddabrykntelsise CeT) nh sul in or len that the Europeans may be able to. take refuge on board of her if another outbreak occurs„</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MR. GE,MGE Gl; oSSMITH WHEN A BOY.</head>
        <p>MR. GE,MGE Gl; oSSMITH WHEN A BOY.</p>
        <p>As Mr. George Grossmith will give one of his humor- ous and musical recitals at Parr Hall, Warrington, this (Saturday) evening, the following interesting. and amus- ing inter-view ‘sith the popular entertainer which ap- pears in " Chums "for November will be read with in- terest. " Yes, come by all means ; I have nothing to tell you." Such was the laconic epistle I received in I reply to my letter (written as a representative •of 1" Chums ") asking Mr. George Grossmith, the well known actor and entertainer, to favour me with half att hour's chat about his boyhood. "About my boyhood 2" said he, replying to my first question without hesitation (heprobably noticed my determined air), " WhY. I act • a boy vet." Mr. George Grossmith was born in Decem- ber, 1847. " However." he went on, " I know what you mean—you want to hear something about my younger days. First of all," continued Mr. Grossmith seating himself comfortably in a chair, "let me tell you that I still possess many of the things which used to amuse me when I was at school—toys, retorts,batteries, a toy printing-press, a camera, and a number of other things. I know boss, as a rule, soon break up such articles ; but, if they take my advice, they will use their Playthingscarefully and save them until they grow up. It :s astenThing what a host of pleasing recollections will be conjured. up on viewing them after a lapse of years. There Is the camera, for instance. Both my brother lA'eedon and I wont in for amateur photography; but as the fad was started by me, I was usually principal operator. We took—or rather,tried to taire—everything and everybody. Mr. J. L. Toole was • taken 'scores of times, as wall as our family, all my father's friends, the I servants and the tradespeople. We must have become [ a nuisance, for our dogs, directly they saw the ! camera in hand, would sneak into the house and hide a way into the coal celler. Then I also had a bicy- cle. Medan had one ton,but for a long time the bicycle was more often on him than her on the bicycle. I remember that machine well. It was bought at an I auction room near Covent Garden, cheap. I being the champion rider of the family was sent to bid brand buy the bicycle, and to ride it home in style. I bought it. 1 That was easy enough ; riding it home was another matter. Anyhow, I leaped into the saddle before an ! admiring crowd of the usual frequenters of Convent Garden, and—came to the ground with a crash. The springs had beau very carefully made of cast iron I I intended that the bicycle should carry me home—l carried the. bicycle." "About your schooldays, Mr. Gressrnith," I then queried. " Where did you firstgo to school 2" " Well, I went to school first at the age of five to an academy for (very young) ladies and gentlemen. This was where I first fell in love with a lady about my own age. At my next school I invented and acted in shadow pantomimes. The school was kept by three ladies, and the eldest was a gr at favourite with the boys. I always appreciated her kirdness, for she occas- ionally gave me permission to fire off a brass cannon with real gunpowder in the kitchen. She was very good to us, and I remained at the school until I was nearly twelve years old." " And how came you to take to entertainments, Mr. Grossmith 2" I then asked. " I really can't say," was the reply. "At the age of twelve I could play the piano very well, and I used to like sing- ing comic songs; in fact, I don't remember a time when I did not sing comic songs, and the rest—well I think the rest came by itself. I think the first regular entertainment in which I took part was lin 1864 at our houe, and it included a , burlesque on " Hamlet" (written by my father), by my brother (Walter Weedon) and myself. I played Hamlet, of course, and my brother played Ophelia and the Gravedigger." Mr. Grossmith is also greatly inter- sited in the Dorset Square Railway—which, by-the-way, i only possesses on locomotive engine—he being, n fact, proprietor, director, manager, station-master, booking. clerk, ticket-collector, porter, engine-driver, stoker, and guard. Although Parliament has not yet been applied to for powers to build the railway,it undoubtely exists, ! and the " express " runs—occasionally. The Hue, let me explain, runs through the popular entertainer's resi- dence, from the main terminus (the reception room), ! through the hall to the stables, the other terminus being the coach-house. Mr.Grossmith is rather reticent about this, his pet hobby. Nevertheless, I learned that the engine—which is a perfect working model, stands three [feet high, and is capable of attaining a high rate of speed—with tender and railway-line, cost the original ' owner about £5OO. Mr. Grossmith says that he bought • it at a sale for a mere song, as the machine was too small for use and too big for a toy. One of the most humor- ous sights imaginable is to watch Mr. Grossmith as, seated in the tender in company with one or two juvenile friends, he starts the train, peering anxiously in front, in order to avert any possible accident, turning on and shutting off steam, as occasion requires, and invariably arriving at the journey's end with passengers and train, intact.</p>
        <p>AN AMBULANCIC CLASS FOR DARESBETRY. On Monday, at 7.15, a class in ambulance work was commenced at the Board School, under tha otu•pices of the Technical Instruction Committee. There was a good attendance, about 26 male mem- bers being present, and there is every indication that the class will be successful. Dr. Bowers, of Stretton, is instructor, and gave an interesting lecture.</p>
        <p>OF THE DARESDURY SUPERINTEN. DENT OF POLICE.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the ordinary business of the Dareshury Petty Sessions on Tuesday morning, the Chairman of the Bench (Mr. J. Charlton Parr) thanked Superintendent J. Atherton, who resigns his post on the 14th November, for the able manner in which ha had discharged his duties. He said he very much regretted they were about to lose suck an excellent superintendent. Mr. Atherton had given every satisfaction during his aztendance at that Court. (Rear, hear.) Mr. Lottg said he strongly approved of all the Chnirman had said, for he had always found Superintendent Atherton's services to he lf great value. Mr. H. Greeaall said that as he was an official of that Court he would lilto to corroborate what had been said with regard' to Mr. Atherton for the kindness shown to him (Mr. Greenall) when he was in attendance at the Court. Superintendent Atherton briefly responded. Tintr</p>
