      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <p>_ _</p>
        <p>'VHF EX A v 1NE!:. SATETWM V NOVEMBER 2 H 95</p>
        <p>pLIFE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>W OSTH LIVING</head>
        <p>W OSTH LIVING</p>
        <p>IN HELL</p>
        <p>Such was the subject of a celebrated sermon preached in Manshester,but the more practical problem of the day is how to make life worth living on earth. One of the important tri- butaries of the river of happiness is the possession of a com- fortable home. A home made bright and cheerful not only by the kind and loving dispositions of those living there, but made attractive by the good taste displayed in the furnishing. Furnishing is a very important factor in the matter of con- tinued self-satisfaction. So much Jerry new London furniture is being now retailed by drapers that one needs to pause be- fore rushing impetuously into the first draper's because of a cheap n d ticket. High-class maker*, such as Lamb,Gillows, and Garnett,still exist, to whom the wealthy can still go with a long purse but to most people this would be a too expensive experiment. Again, the average man nowadays has a strong liking for a bargain ; anything obtainable at about half or quarter the cost has a strong fascination for him. Is it possi- ble to obtain such an advantage in furnishing P Yes, and more than possible, it is an absolute fact. We cannot do better than recommend a visit to MR. WORSLBY'S FUR- NISHING. PANTHEON, GAYTHORN, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>MHII3 Successful Establiskment of any of the large ware- houses of Manchester is always of interest to the citizens. One of the most brilliant examples of the valoe of perseverance, enterprise, and tact is the now busy hive of Industry so well known a. The Pantheon, which occupies such a prominent position in Gaythorn, near the antral Station, Mancheter. The building is a massive pile of modern design, 150 feet long, six storeys high, with show- room accommodation covering an area of over one million square feet. The plate-glass windows are each 12 feet square, each square if broken costing nearly £3O to replace. len of these immense show windows form a portion of the frontage. This will give a faint idea of "The Pantheon" in outward appearance, but ah l—.—here pen fails altogether in attempting to do justice to anything like an adequate description of the Interior. "The Pantheon" was speciAly built to give the largest and most convenient floor space for showrooms, and cost £28,000. the floors specially constructed to carry enormous weight. To-day it stands as one of the smartest furnishing houses in England, containing by far the largest and most comprehensive stock of any two houses in this large city. From basement to roof, from floor toceiling, " The Pantheon" is filled to overflowing with thousands of well bought, care- fully selected bargains, to attempt description of which would be as futile as Liverpool attempting to crush</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>_ THE MAhHESTER SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>_ THE MAhHESTER SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>The destinies of' The Pantheon" are c tstrolled and pre- sided over by its sole absolute proprietor, Mr. W. H. Worsley, whose sole study is an ever increasing desire to satisfy and please the thousands of customers who daily patronise and support his efforts. We invite the reader to accompany us in an imaginary visit to " lhe Pantheon" to see what it con- tains and how it Is managed. . . .</p>
        <p>On arrival we are at once struck with the magnificent</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>STARTLING APPEARANCB</head>
        <p>STARTLING APPEARANCB</p>
        <p>of " The Great Par.theon," the immense long line of stupen- dous show windows displaying for sale capital specimens of high-class Second-hand Furniture ny high-class makers, the sale of which has made "The Pantheon famous.</p>
        <p>UPON ENTERING THE BUILDING we see at once that as far as stock Is concerned the?e is sufficient furniture for nearly the whole of Manchester. We see at once visitors walking round inspecting from Oldham, Bolton Bury, Burley, Bleckburn,Bacup, Rochdale, Preston, Warrington and all parts of Lancashire and Yorkshire, Cheshire, the Potteries. Wales, &amp;c. It is the cardinal rule of The Pantheon "to give the fullest facilities for inspec- tion without feeling compelled to buy. The ground floor is filled with the large specimens of bedroom furniture suitable for large rooms; we are teld the smaller suites are on the top floor, to which we can travel by means of the hoist, or walk. Bedroom suites on the ground floor we notice by Lamb, original cost £l5O, price £35 ; by Goodall, cost £lBO, for £5O ; by Henry Ogden. cost £2OO, for £65. These are among the best suites, but one that strikes us with special attention is a fine handsome Bedroom suite by Behan. In solid American walnut, heavily calved in bas relief, with large wardrobe fitted with brilliant crystal bevel mirrors ; dressing table unique with neatly fitted jewel drawers, sur- mounted by large swing dressing glass ; washstand fitted with statuary marble and shaving mirror, three chairs, pedestal table de unit, and towel horse; what originally cost £6O you can obtain for £25. Over 200 Bedroom suites are in stn.*, on show, from 355. to £lOO, forming a dia play so impressive that wears almost</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>STRUCK DUMB WITH AMAZRMENT.</head>
        <p>STRUCK DUMB WITH AMAZRMENT.</p>
        <p>We now go up stairs to the Dining-roons Showroom. Wkat a sight, long avenues of Sideboards Is the first impression; hundreds of Sideboards, with hundreds of Suites, also Dining Tables, Overmantels, splendid collection of Bronzes, Ormolu Clocks, Vases, .fcc. In this department we find magnificent Sideboards, original cost from £lOO to £2OO. at prices simply astounding, £lO to £2O. The next floor above is the Drawing- room Furniture Showroom, which is simply a show of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>DAZZLING BEAUTY</head>
        <p>DAZZLING BEAUTY</p>
        <p>dissolving one's sen;itriv-e7p;re-cirifitit-inti impressive loveli- ness. No dep.rtmerit is so interesting to holes as the draw- ing-room shqwroom; a keen delight is experienced in selecting weird and fantastic furniture for the drawing-roore. /Esthetic, Moorish, and Egyptian Cabinets, peculiar Green and rronzed Tables, Occasional Chairs of charming variety. Cosy Corners. Comfortable Lounging Settees and chairs equal to the highest class firms in the Kingdom at quarter the price The largest showroom for Drawing-room kurniture in Manchester, c.ontainiow over 100 Suites, 100 Cabinets, 200 Overroantels, 500 Tables. Suites from £3 10s. Cd. to £5O. Cabinets from 30s. to £45. Marvellous value, worth in roost cases three or tour times the money charged. The next department adjoining is cases three or four times the money changed. The nexs d.. partment is the Bedstead and Bedding Department. which contains an enormous stock of Bedsteads. The brilhant- burnished brass displayed here is so immense as to present almost a</p>
        <p>SBA OF GOLD. Magnificent specimens of the brassworkers' art is laere dls played to perfection. Brass Bedsteads may be purchased solid and complete for as low a price as 355. to the best at £35. A elear saving of 50 per cent. is effected in bedsteads and bedding. Brass and Iron Bedsteads, strong and durable, maybe secured from 6s. Ed. to 30s. The bedding is all new. manufactured on the premises; everything is new in this department, the work kere giving employment to over 50 hands. Nothing is allowed to be used here except it has pre- viously been wasbekl and purified. All bedding sold at Wor- sley's may be guaranteed as of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>SPOTLESS PURITY.</head>
        <p>SPOTLESS PURITY.</p>
        <p>Wool Beds compiet;,-iiTit-r-an-te-ea-rali- wool, from Bs. 80., Hair Mattresses from 18s. 6d., Feather Beds from 22a. 6d. The next place of interest to visit is the Bedroom Furniture Department, which is displayed in a showroom, 63 yards by 25 yards. larger than most of the combined showrooms of most furnishing establishments. One is bound to admit that The Pantheon is certainly far ahead of any rival establish- ments in point of size and convenience. The showroom visited no far are lofty, well lighted, and of immense area. The Bedroom Ferniture Showroom is magnificent; 300 Bedroom Suites are displayed in as' noes, tke finest collec- tion extant, at prices simply incredible, astonishing every one, pulverising and exterminating all competition. Com- plete Bedroom Suites, solid throughout, at £3 10s. up to £3O, magnificent In style and finish. Enamelled Suites trona Ws. to ET. The next departme3t is for Kitchen Furniture, the varied stock of which is quite sufficient to satisfy the most exacting. Thousands of chairs, hundreds of tables and dressers, every requisite for the kitchen at half the usual prices charged. A department Is also provided for Iron- mongery. But we must move on, our examination and in- spection is taking to loug and occupying too much spice. We now are shown into the Carpet and Oilcloth Department, which contains thousands of DEW rolls of carpets, Brussels from Is. 114 d, Tapestry from 1C d., Axminster from 2s. 1110 and so on. Then we see hundreds of Second-hand Carpets. If you want • carpet to beat creation, buy one of Worsley's I second-hand carpets ; they knock the competition of Jew and Gentile into smithereens. You can get a second-hand Brussels carpet, no. tapestry ; no, real. genuine Brussels. from 4 to 5 yards by 3to 4 yards, for under a sovereign. Rugs of im- mense variety, leveiy Skin Rugs, Axminster, in facto' every conceivable design, shade, and colour, forming a gorgeous display. RIVALLING THR RAINBOW. 011eoths and Linoleums are here out to pieces; thousands of yards are here daily slashed and cut into all shapes to fit the various kouses they are intended for. The prices are also cut, in fact, cut to fine that you would require a magnifying glass to discover any profit made eut of this department. We saw Oilcloths from lid. per yiud and Linoleums Mom 6d. per yard. a he best Scotch Oilcloths and Line'e .ms are displayed from Is. 3d. per yardlto 2s. lid. We should like to speak of all the wonderful things displayed for sale, but we must be pre- paring to leave The Pantheon in order to catch our train. We, therefore, hu.ry throug'i the Office Furniture Department, the Brass Vender and leirebrass Department, the Miscellane- ous Department quickly on to the latest addition, the Music Department. Hark! What do we hear mAGNIFICENT Bargain in Fine POLIARD OAK FURNITURE by James Lamb, consisting of a truly laandsome Sideboard, modern design, 71t. long, a fine massive piece of high-class cabinet work, rich coloured pollard oak with ebony lines, brilliant crystal thick English plate back; elegantly fitted through° with modern conveniences, richly Carved panels in bar-relief. The Suite consists of a dining- room couch, luxuriously upholstered; two lounge chairs and 12 fine standard single chairs, with stuffed backs, all finish.rd in English morocco and curled hair. An 18ft. Pollard Oak Dining Table, on six carved supports. The lot cost nearly £.90; price for the lot. £75. A dead bargain, just removed from Newland', Cheatham Hill. Inspect early.-- WORSLEY'S.</p>
        <p>MISCELLANEOUS BA RGAI NS.-Lot 63, very handsome DINING-ROOM SUITE, by Lamb, cost £l5 for £25, in brown oak and morocco; lot 68, heavy mahogany Dining- room Suite, by Goodall, in morocco, cost £65, for £lB 18*., bargain; lot 73, excellent Dining-room Suite by Ogder, In Utrecht velvet, massive carved trames, coot £6O tor an ; lot 75, good substantial Dining room Sui•e, in walnut. by Dyson, worth £35, for £7 its, a bargain; lot 79, Lollard oak Dining- room Suite, cost £l5O, in morocco, perfect condition, almost new, by Balfour, £35; let 125, magnificent walnut Sideboard, cost £5O, tor £l5 ; lot 128, large Sideboard, in oak, cost £45, for £8 ; lot 12.9, 61 c. Sideboard In oak, cost 250, for £lO 101.; , lot 131, massive mahogany Sideboard. cost £6O, for £l2; lot , 133, mahogany Sideboard, lovely condition, cost £.85. for £l5; lot 141, carved walnut Sideboard, early English, £3O, for £7 75.; lot 142. solid heavy Sideboard. wrath £25. for £3 1054 lot 143, a real chance in a Sideboard, for 355. ;lots 215 to 416, 220 Overmaetels. from 10e. 6d. to £5. equally as good bar- gains 'Lathe sideboards; lot 410. Drawing-room Suite, in silk. £6. lot 411, Drawing-roem Suite, in inlaid rosewood, £k; lot 412, Drawing-room Suite, cost 80 guineas, tor £25 ; lot 415, Drawing-room suite, carved Chippendale, for LIZ; lot 428, !Drawing-rams Suite, walnut and rep, £3 105.; lot 425, Drawing-room Suite, worth £lOO, for £3O; lot 516, Drawic.g- room Cabinet, cost £3O, for £10; 200 Cabinets in Stock. Lot 715, Complete Bedroom Suite, walnut, for £5; lot 716, Com- plete Bedroom Suite, massive, cost £25, for £7 ; lot 718, Com- plete liedroolo Suite, heavil carved, £10; lot 719, Complete Bedroom Suite, a marvel, for £3 10s. ; lot 720, very handsome Bedroom suite, by Ratcliffe. beautifully carved in bar-relief, 5f t. wardrobe, fitted with every iuxurious convenience, bril- liant crystal bevel plates, dressing table, washstand, with I Camara marble, thre, chairs, and towel rail, in finest selected Americas' walnut, transparent finish, the suite is worth £5O, price 20 guineas. WWoRSLEY, The Pantheon, Gaythorn, • Manchester. Close to Central station. HOURS' OF BIJIIiNIO3B EMU DAY V to 7.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>PRESS OPINIONS ABOUT</head>
        <p>PRESS OPINIONS ABOUT</p>
        <p>J• GERSHON, THE UNIVERSAL, FURNISHING ARCADE, 44, 01DHAU-SIIEE r, MANCHESTER, (Next to the Alelphl Bank).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>READ WHAT THEY SAY</head>
        <p>READ WHAT THEY SAY</p>
        <p>_ One of those modern monstre establishments, whose re- sources arc so boundless, and wh‘tse stocks are so comprehen- sive, that it can, with equal care and despatch, furnish throughout a cottage or a mansion, is to be found in the famous house of J. GBRSHON, furnishing contractor of 44, Oldham-street, Manchester. This colossal business was commenced in MO, and being cozdtvited with remarkable ability, energy, and foresight, and or, sound commerciil principles, it rapidly grew in public favour and patronage until at the present time it is the largest and most represen- tative house of its kind in Manchester. Mr. Gershon at- tends to every customer personally, and des not leave cus- tomers in the bands of salesmen. Operations are carried on In a magnificent block of four-storey buildings, occupying a prominent and attractive position, and having a splendid frontage, They comprise shops, offices, and capacious shavr- rooms on the ground floor, and further suites of extensive showrooms on the first, second. and third fl.mrs, together with showrooms at the rear of vast capacity, and filled to re- pletion with an Immense collection of valuable goods. Mr. Gershon le a thorough exponent of the general house furnishing, arm stocks held are unrivalled in Manchester either in magnitude, variety, or worth ; if wide experience, and far-seeing policy are absolutely requisite in proper selecting and getting together such a stupendous conglomer- ation of mi.cellane 'us goods, it is equally true that a great amount of ability of no common order Is necessary to arrange and group them with such admirable effect, and to maintain at the same time such regard for the comfort and convenience of visitors. Where everything that can possibly be considered to belong to this branch of business is fully represented, it is an endless task to attempt anything like an enumeration in detail, it will be sufficient to indicate the leading lines with which the house is occupied. Superb and comprehen- sive stocks are held in Drawing, Dining, and Brsakfast Room Suites, made of all the best woods, artistically elegant in design, and magnificently and gorgeously upholstered in all materiats of various colours, shades, and tints. Bedroom Suites made of the most dainty materials, and of the choicest patterns. And substantial, solid-looking Library, Study, and Hall Furniture, together with inex- haustible supplies of splendid couches, ottomans, and so as, duchesse stands. sidebo .rda, book-casee,..heffoniers, cabinets, Davenports, easy, occasional, and other chairs, dining tables, dressing, work, card. 100, a d occadonal tables, whatnots, overmantels, chests of drawers, as well as a large assortment of bedsteads of every deserittlon. p.ttern. size and price ; bedding, mattresses, curtains, fenders, fireirons and carpets, I rugs, and mats of every pattern and design, from all the most celebrated makers. Briefly, the stock embraces every article of whatever kind that can possibly be required in furnishing any or every kind of residence, from the cottage to the mansion of the noble; and not only is each in- dividual article to be found in these marvellous stores, hut each article is represented by a large collection, often unique in its kind, &amp;Ili including a wide selection in style, quality, and prices This enterprising firm have also manufacturing premises of consider- ble extent, and thoroughly equipped with plant and machinery of the newest and best kind and a large number of skilled workmen are constantly employed In the mar ufacture of cabinet work of all .teserlptions. Repairs, too, are executed on the premises, a little army of assistants is employed in the control of this stupendous establishment, and competent men are sent all over the kingdom to fit up private residenees, hotels, offices, public buildings, and mansions, in conjunction wi h their business. The connection of this representative house is far-reaching and most valuable, its goods /save acquired the highest repu- tation in Lancashire and the neighbouring counties, and the name of Gershon "Is an ample guarantee for everythlog that is superior to his line of business. The proprietor is a man of extensive experience and ot great arid varied know- ledge in the various branches of the business in which he is engaged. His able and energetic supervision is given to the whole of the concern, and the utmost vigilance is used that nothing shall emanate from his estab.ishment which shall tend to detract from the high and well-merited reputation the firm has achieved. Mr. Gershon is held in great e.teem in private life for his abil,ty, public spirit, and commercial Integrity. We strongly advise those about Vrnishing to call at Mr. GBRSHON'S Universal Curnlshisig Arcade, 44, OLDHAM - MANCM HST 13 la. (Next door to the Adelphi Bank).</p>
        <p>I ETTIIRS ARRIVE BY EYE ".Y POST expressing thanks 1,1 at thelmanner orders are csrried out, and the following are published with the consent or the writers. _ READ WHAT THEY SAY. _</p>
        <p>L. Samson. Esq.. the :Managing Director of the Civil Service Dental Company. Limited, writes 150, Brompton-road, South Kensington, S.W.. "August 30, 1895. " Mr. J. Gershon, Manchester.</p>
        <p>" Dear sir,—l have returned your packing materials, and consider it only just to say that everything arrived In perfect condition. I am very pieased with the goods. Shou'd I require more, you shall have my order, and I shall be pleased to recommend you.—Yours truly. L. SAMSON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>READ ON.</head>
        <p>READ ON.</p>
        <p>_ Fred Bentley, Esq., writes f ' Victoria Hotel, Manchester, September 25, 1895. 'Dear sir,—l expected to be able to call on you to-day on passing through, but time prevented my doing so. Every- thing has arrived in a satibfactory manner, and my wife and self are perfectly satisfied with the various suites you furnished me with, and now the work is completed the houst looks exceedingly nice. I enclose chcque in settlement. Please acknowledge to Southport, and oblige yours faith- fully, FRISD. BENTLEY. Mr. J. Gershon, Oldham-street, Manchester, It/CAD ON.</p>
        <p>W. Broadbent, Beg , writes neynard Cottage. Penkridge. September 27, 1895. " Dear sir,—All the goods arrived safely, and give the greatest satisfaction. If they turn out equal to the goods I had from you 14 years ago, when I lived at Blackpool, I shall have no need to complain. Cases and racking returned. —Yours truly, "W. BROADH URST. "J. Gershon, 44. Oldham-street, Manchester."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>M ARVLLOUS BARGAINS.</head>
        <p>M ARVLLOUS BARGAINS.</p>
        <p>IN—Consequence of Financial Difficulties and a Death in the Family, I have had placed in my hands for immediate dis- posal the contents of e handsomely furnished Villa, late the property of • solicitor, who, through the death of his wife and unfortunate speculation, has come to grief. In addition to the dining, drawing, bed rooms, and library furniture, and a quantity of miscellaneous articles. I have a number of Law Books, particulars of which will be given below. Only a brief description of the goods can be given. Intending purchasers are cordially invited, an* upon examination will find tie goods high class and in spotless condition, they having been made to special order less than 18 months ago. The hall is furnished with a most uncom- mon combination Nall stand, which forms a neat, bench, bracket, fitted with receptacles for sticks, umbrellas, brushes, gloves, &amp;c., and the back is finished with brass pegs, and a 1 massive bevelled-dige glass mirror measuring about 31t. long; price £.5 15s. A pair ball chairs to match, 21s. 1 The Dining-room is well worthy the attention of those alsout furnishing. It consists of a noble sideboard, which is guarantee.' solid walnutwood through ;it messsurea 6ft. long and about 9ft. high. The lower portion is fitted with every convenience, plate drawers, wine bin, and cupboards, with splendid carved panels, and a truly magnificent back, with a massive shield ships centre bevelled glass and four smaller bevelled glasses, between which are brackets, supported by massive carved pillars. Next comes the solid walnut extend- ing dining table. with two extra leaves opening out to a. large size. and resting on finely carved fluted legs of rare thickness. To match are the half-dozen standard upholstered chairs, two large easy chairs and couch, all studded with brass studs, and upholster-d in a rich claret-colour leather of undoubted quality. To complete this remarkably handsome lot, I have the pair of real Japanese vases, standing about 2ft. high. Now for the price. Some houses would ask £9O for them. I am in a position to pack and deliver the lot for 38 guineas. The sideboard alone is worth the money. _ .</p>
        <p>The Drawing-room really batiks description, it is so unique, and cannot be described in the space of an advertisement. The four small occasional chairs, two conversational chairs, pair of Eugene easy chairs, and the gondola double-ended shaped settee, are most profusely inlaid with choice and rare woods • the upholstery is most uncommon, being a real broaden° of chocolate and silver, which is handsomely brocaded with towers and sprays, trimmed with suitable gimp and highly finished. Next comes the magnificent inlaid cabinet, measuring about glit. 91n. wide and nearly Bft. high, fitted in a most artistic manner with bevelled glasses, small cupboards enclosed with inlaid doors. It is simply a work of art, being complete in construction, design, and finish. The overmantle to match is very unique, most beautifully inlaid, finished with bevelled glass and side brackets to match the cabinet. There are two of the handsomest inlaid tables ever seen, octagonal shape, on curious shaped legs, with wine tray fitted between the legs. Now for the price. If £72 was quoted it would not be anything like the cost. lam able to offer the complete fittings fir a stylish drawing-room at the low price of 38 guineas, The Library is fitted with 10 solid oak chairs, one large easy ohair, and very fine lounge. All Sr. Doveeton's make. they are covered in leather, which is somewhat worn. When new they cost £5O. Can now be bad for £lO the let. There I. a very handsome carved oak bookcase, enclosed with glass door with carved pillars running up this sides. The lower portirn is carved with curious heads. faces, &amp;AL Price, £lO. Very fine carved oak corner cabinet. 5 guineas. Superior black oak carved dinner waggon or side table, and several other oak pieces. Useful mahogany bookcase, with folding glass doors, MOP, )1. shelves, lower portion enclosed with cupboards and drawers. A great bargain; for #l4 15a. A fine old Spanish mahogany dining table. No better table ever made, on bold fluted legs, lye extra leaves, opening out to 201 t., also the leaf case. A dead bargain; for £l2. There are several Bedroom Suites. The best room is in solid American walnutwood, and cast MO. price 50 guineas The second room is in solid rosewood, which cost £Bi. for 40 guineas. The third room, which is very handsome, consists of a solid American walnut suite; the wardrobe is 81t. wide, fitted with two bevelled-glase doors, reaching from plinth to cornice; between the doors are lock-up drawers and cup- boards ; pedestal dressing table, fitted with drawers In the pedestals, large bevelled glass attached; the double wash- stands, with cupboards in the pedestals, dark marble slab,tile back •, bevelled shaving mirror, towel steer, beside cupboard, and three chairs, upholstered in plush; a £5O suite for 25 guineas. Several smaller suites, complete with wardrobes ; 13 guineas. Solid ash suite, with wardrobe, dressinx table, washstand, bedside cupboard, chairs and towel airer. for £7 103. Several odd wardrobes, dressing tables, aid wash- stands.</p>
        <p>Kitchen oddments. Dinner set complete, 54 pieces. 18s. fel. two corner cupboards, nearly new ; wicker invalid carriage, on spider wheels and rubber tyres ; cost four months ago, £8; price 5.55. 4%. number of law books, among which ars the following William's Law of Real Propert7, Leading Convey- ancing and Equity Cases, " Indermanls ;l Leading Common Law Cities. " Inderman's ;" Law of Companies T. Rustsee smith ; the Conveyancing Acts, 1881 and 1879, with notes. forms, and precedents. by E. P. Wolstenholme and R. O. Turner; IntrodUetton to Conveyancing, H. W. nip- hinstone ; 4 vole. Stepheus's Commentaries on the Laws of England. to.; a fine registered writing desk, pedestal writing desk, both nearly new ; number cf bordered carpet squares, velvets, Brussels, and others. Some pleasing oil paintings and engravings, musical box, two pianos, quantity of china and gloss. Several very fine brass bedsteads. Solid oak case of cutlery. neves been used, a wedding present. The whole have been removed, and are on view at J. GERSHON'S Showrooms, 44, Oldham-street (next to Adelphi Bank). Can be warehoused till required, and delivered free. Note address; J. GERSHON, next to Adelphi Bank. 144, 0/4/mm-street, Bleuebeeter.</p>
        <p>Oairq bp gurtlon.</p>
        <p>tirptlefs, SafOsinturst,earriages, tt</p>
        <p>J. W. TEMPERLEY MUSIC SL Eli, 31, LOWLAND'S ROAD RUNCORN. PIANOS, ORGANS, T lOLINS, &amp;o. Tuning and Repairs. All kinds of Fittings in Stock. Arrangements made tor First-Class Orchestral Band raid Pianists, PIANOS en HIER and on HIIMPUROHASH.</p>
