        <p>ILLNESS OF THE REV. J. SIMPSON. In consequence of a serious illness, the Rev. J. Simp- son, pastor of Bethesda Congregational Church, was un- able to conduct the service on Sunday when the 60th anniversary of the church was held. His place was taken in the morning by Mr. W. H. Chadwick. At the evening service, the Rev. E. Austin, Congregational minister, of Widnes, occupied the pulpit. Mr. Simpson has been most seriously indisposed, but on Thursday he was reported to be much better.</p>
        <p>"LORD BYRON.—TRE MAN AND HIS TIMES." This was the subject of a paper read at the meeting of the Brunswick Mutual improvement Society on Wed- nesday evening, by Mr. J. F. Moulton, headmaster at the Greenwayroad Board schools. Mr. I'. Simister occupied the chair, and there was a good attendance. An interesting discussion followed, taken part in by Messrs. P. T. Lawton, D. Bisbrown, and J. D. Jones. Toe essayist was accorded a vote of thanks on the motion of Mr. Gorle, seconded by Mr. J. A. Smith, jun.</p>
