      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BILLIARD MATCH.</head>
        <p>BILLIARD MATCH.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, the roiurn match between the amateurs of Runcorn and district and those of Liverpool and district took place at the Duchess of Edinburgh Hotel, Birkenhead. The re3ult was a win for the home team by 1,177 points to 980. The individual scores were as follows :</p>
        <p>RIiSCORN.</p>
        <p>Parkinson l5O Golborna 142 - - -</p>
        <p>Broady 7l Coleman W Sadler BB Ciekson l5O Barnes l5O Palethorpe Hunt l2l Tothill Stirrup l5O Earl D Brown 123 Benson J Sadler</p>
        <p>' 1,177</p>
        <p>THE DEOWNING OF RUNCORN MEN.-A CAPTAIN FOUND.</p>
        <p>Information was received in Runcorn on Thursday afternoon that a body which had been washed ashore at Prestatyn, near Rhyl, had been identified as that of Captain Burton, of 11,Cavendish-st., Runcorn,belonging to the steam flat Reufrew,ot Liverpool,which was lost off the Welsh Coast early in October. The vessel belonged to Mr. William Burton, of Sankey Hall, Warrington. Nothing has been heard of the other member of the crew who is missing, and the same remark applies to the men of the steam flat " William Henry " which was sunk on Monday week, resulting in three Runcorn men being drowned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RETIREMENT OF SUPERINTENDENT ATHERTON.</head>
        <p>RETIREMENT OF SUPERINTENDENT ATHERTON.</p>
        <p>The news that Superintendent Atherton of Runcorn, is to retire from the Cheshire police farce on the 15th inst., will come as a surprise to many. He has been a member of the constabulary for over 28 years, and superintendent of the Runcorn division for nearly four years, and it is a coincidence that he should conclude his public duties in his native town. He has been stationed in different parts of the county, and has performed his duties in a way that has gained him the respect and esteem of tho:e with whom he came in contact. His somewhat early severance from the force will be regretted, and many will read of the sentiments expressed by the magistrates on the Daresbury bench on Tuesday last. At a meeting of the Cheshire Standing Joint Committee at Chester on Saturday, it was decided that he should be allowed a pension of £l2O per year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Musical Society commencel their eighth season on Tuesday evening with a grand concert which was given in the Public Hall. The audience was perhaps not so large as might have been ex- pected under the circumstances, but as regards the concert itself, it was a complete success. A miscel- laneous programme had been arranged and included 1 instrumental and vocal items, the performance of which was highly creditable to all Concerned. Mozart's "Magic Flute" served as an overture, and in their first essay the instrumentalists at once gained favour with the audience by their admirable performance. It may be here mentioned that the Run- corn members were greatly assisted by a number of well-known vocalists and instrumentalists from War- rington, Liverpool, and elsewhere. The special artistes engaged were Miss Jeanie Rankin, Mr. Alfred Jordan, and Miss Ethel Barns. Mr. Alfred Jordan is the possessor of a fine tenor voice, and his enunciation is clear and distinct, although not quite so powerful as could be wished for. His first two contributions were " Margerita and " The Daily Question " (Helmund), and both are well suited to his style, and, as a matter ot course, he received vociferous applause. " The Devout Lover" and " Come Live me and be my Love "—and Mr. Jordan seems to have a partiality for love songs— were also exceedingly well rendered. Miss Jennie Rankin was the only lady soloist, but her interpretation of Gounod's cavatina in " Faust " " When all was young," which she sung with 'cello obligato, was such as to at once attest her possession not only of a cultivated and powerful soprano voice, but of those subtle qualities which are so necessary to a successful concert singer. Giordiani's " Caro mio ben "(My Darling Love) in the softer and musical Italian, and the two songs entitled, " Shepherd's Cradle Song," and 6' When Twilight comes," gave Miss Rankin further opportunities of evidencing her ability and capacity to interpret Bangs differing widely in senti- ment and treatment. A violin obligato was a feature of the last song,and as in her first-mentioned contrisution the additional accompaniment introduced delightful touches of tone colour which were a material support to the voice. Mr. J. W. Collinson was the violinist and Mr. Walter Hatton, the 'cellist. Miss Ethel Barnes as solo violinist was a new name in the list of those who took part in the concert. She is, however, a musician rapidly coming to the front, and has already gained many honourable distinctions. Her first contribution to the programme on Tuesday was Svendsen's "Romance" which was an unequivocal suceess in point of execution and verve, and called forth the enthusiasm of the audi- ence. In response, Miss Barns bowed her acknowledg- ments. " Rondo Capricioso." and " Mazurka" (Barns) were two other very agreable items and aroused the audience to a most marked demonstration of approval, her brilliant execution of the second of these, resulting in a loud demand for an additional solo. She complied, and the piece given was a masterly example of her firm, yet delicate, handling of her instrument. Mr. Birkett Musgrove, of Runcorn, was the accompanist on the piano. The work of the orchestra during the evening calls for special mention inasmuch as it is to this department that a great deal of the success of the concert is due. They were fully I equipped for their work and their execution of the vari- ous pieces allotted to them was both adequate and musicianly. The overture has already been alluded to ; their ether contributions in the first part included An. dente, alinuetto, and Allegro di Motto (6th symphony in " The Surprise" by Haydn) and were all excellently performed. Mr. V. Needham's execution of the piccolo solo" Fantasie de Concert," was alike neat and tasteful, and he received swell deserved encore. Wagner's ever delightful march "Tannhreuser," introduced the s.cond part, and the piece led up to the chorus " Hail Brignt Abode" which seemed to bring it to a fitting el -a ix. The gavotte "L 'lngenne " was a per. formar r, which was attentively followed by the an. dien and wah played with fine taste and skill. Ir .choruses and part songs the instrumentalists per.</p>
        <p>med Useful Service, several striking effects being in- reduced which added greatly to their interest. Of the chorus, little can be said, for their duties were very light but nevertheless in the few items rendered they acquit- ted themselves admirably. Two part songs, a choral ballad, and a chorus completed their duties. " Lord tillin's Daughter." (Gam) was the title of the choral ballad, and consists of thirteen verses in which is del picted the daughter's escape from her father and the crossing of Lock Gyle with its tragic ending se pithily told in the last two lines which run as foltowg</p>
        <p>"The waters wild went 'oer his child, • And he was left lamenting." Thepiece gave the chorus an opportunity ef displaying their powers, and the piquant points of the story were made the moat of. " Who is is Sylvia ?" and "The Gallant Troubadour" were the two part songs, the latter being given by special request. Both were sung in a manner which left nothing to be desired, and the same may be said of the interpretation of the itiorus " Hail Bright Abode." Mr. F. H. Crossley, the conductor of the Society, is to be congratulated upon the successful way in which he piloted the orchestra and chorus through the many difEculties—whose name is legion—and also upon their excellent performance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>OUR LQCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LQCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL CHURCH, BOLD-STREET.</p>
        <p>---- - On Sunday the Rev. W. H. Towers (President of the Manchester and District Union of Christian Endeavour Societies) preached both morning and evening at the above place of worship. In the morning the rev, gentle- man preached from the text " For I know him (Abra- ham), that he will command his children and his house- hold after him ; and they shall keep the way of the Lord and do justice and judgment," Genesis 16c. 1.9 v., and dealt with home life principally, pointed out the duty of one to another, children to their parents, and brothers to their sisters, &amp;c. For the evening service Mr. Towers based his remarks upon the remarks upon the words "For before his translation he had testimony that he phased God," Hebrews 11c.5v. The preacher dwelt upon Enoch pleasing God, and said it was possible for God's children in daily life also to live so as to please Him. The attend- ances were fair,and both these sermons were appreciated by the congregation.</p>
        <p>A considerable number of Cheshire farmers met at Nantwich en Saturday to consider their position as af- fected by the prevailing low prices for dairy produce. Their position, it was urged, was very serious, and the need for prompt and substantial relief was pressing. Mr. ' Thomas Parton calculated that the direct annual loss to a man farming 200 acres and making ten tons of cheese a year was, under present conditions, not less than £330 as compared with normal times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>DISTRIBUTION OF PRIZES.</head>
        <p>DISTRIBUTION OF PRIZES.</p>
        <p>In the Technical Institute on Monday evening, Dr. McDougall distributed the prizes gained by students in last year's Scieuce and Art Classes. Mr. E. Imison, J.P., occupied the chair. There was a fair attendance. The Chairman in calling upon Dr. McDougall to dis- tribute the prizes said he would like to be allowed to say how pleased be was to have the privilege of taking part in the first ceremony of that kind which bad taken place in that building, and that pleasure was added to by the fact that the ceremony of distributing the prizes was to be performed by Dr. McDougall. Ever since the School Board had had the control of the classes, Dr. McDougall had acted as chairman of the committee appointed for the purpose. He had taken an amount of interest, and given a quantity of the time and troulsle which would greatly surprise those uninitiated in work, and probably which only those aho had to do with it could properly appreciate. (Hear, hear.) Dr. McDougall afterwards presented the successful students with the prizes gained. The hollowing is a list of the prizes :—Machine construction. George Holt, Jotiu Kenuan ; building construction, George Pennington, W. Cropper, Edward Dobson ; steam, Harold Davie , Percy Buckley; applied mechanics, Harold Davies, Harry Turton, George Williamson ; physiography, Flora McLeod ; freehand drawing. Thomas Clare, Mary Hay- ' ton; model drawing, Eleanor Davies, Walter Howard, and Flora McLeod. Miss Hayton had also received a Queen's prize of £2 for excellence of freehand drawing. The awards given by Mr. Speakman teacher of maceiue construction were awarded to the following :—Percy Williams, George McNorton,and John Alfred Rawlinsou. Afterehe had performed the duty, Dr. McDougall said it was a duty which was particularly agreeable to him- self, and he most heartily congratulated all the studeats on the success they had obtained during the last session. ' He wished the numbers were larger, but he thought the number that were there that night gave strong evidence that they had done diligent work during the past ses- sion. The prizes consisted of three classes, prizes given ' by the Science and Art committee of the School Board, those given by the teachers, and the Prizes awarded by ' the Science and Art Department. He thought they had reason to be proud that one of the students,Miss Hayton, had been so successful as to obtain a department prize. He thought Miss Hasten had done very well in obtain- ing about half-a-dozen books. They might be sure that . they would have to do hard and diligent work if they hoped to gain distinctions in the particular branches they had taken up. The selection of the books by the students was strong evidence of their intentions to de- vote themselves assiduously to particular work. Every- one must admit that this country had not done as much as had been done on the Continent of Europe in the way of the technical instruction,but within the last few years technical instruction had made wonderful strides and no doubt in the next few years we should be in advance of any country in that matter. They had in that building, opportunities for continuing their studies which they had not had previously owing to the limited means of the Runcorn Science and Art Committee. That Com- mittee perceiving what was their duty, four or five years ago, established with the limited means at their disposal science and art classes. Referring to science, .he said that when the first steam engine was invented there was very little known of mechanism, geometry or algebra by the inventor but the genius was there and he recognised what a great power steam was, and they were indebted to that great man for those wonderful pieces of machin- ery—the moveable engine as well as the fixed one, They knew what that had done and knew what it was likely to do in the future. He was sorry to say that the functions of the Science and Art Committee had come to an end, but on the other hand he was glad that the students were being housed in a place where they had every facility for the continuation of their studies. He hoped they would not be content with those prizes bet that they would go on working hard and plodding on, until they had gained greater distinetion. It was not possible for all to gain prizes but it was within them to main- tain a certain amount of knowledge by hard work. They were indebted to the Chairman for his assistance, as he was always ready to do good work, and felt especial pleasure in countenancing that work. It was gratifying that the closing scenes of the Science and Art Committee should be performed and the obsequies sung over the dying embers in that Institution. He wished everyone a happy and prosperous career. (Applause.) Mr. Moss proposed a vote of thanks to Dr. McDougall, and in doing so said they were that "night to sing their own funeral anthem. With the Science and Art Commit- tee also expired the secretary. Hs had been pleased to engage in assisting and encouraging the establishment of those classes. He hoped that in future rneetiugs of that description would be very much larger. He wished the students and the Technical Institute every possible suc- cess. (Applause.) The resolation was carried unanimously, and in res. pcndiag Dr. McDougall said Mr. Moss, the secretary of the Science and Art Committee, had had the absolute confidence of the members of the Committee in promot- ing the classes and bringing them to the state of perfec- tion in which they had been able to hand them over to their successors.</p>
        <p>A vute of thanks to the Chairman concluded the pro. ceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>At Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, the fortnightly meeting of the Board was held when Mr. J. Knowles presided. , There were also present in addition to the members of the Runcorn District Council, the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. T. Lea, and A. R. N -irman, with Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. tlshton (deputy clerk) and Mr. Gunnell (workhouse master). THE PROPOSED SEVERANCE OF NORLEY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mclntyre submitted the report of the committee appointed to consider the application of Norley to be severed from the Runcorn Union and added to North- wich Union. There were nine members present, and the committee recommended that the application be not granted. Re moved the acceptance of that recommenda- tion.—Mr. Woodward seconded the motion, and it was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MORE ASS/STANCE .NEEDED_AT THR WORK/1011SL</head>
        <p>MORE ASS/STANCE .NEEDED_AT THR WORK/1011SL</p>
        <p>The report of the Visiting Committee stated that the master had informed them that more help was required in the kitchen and laundry, sad added that the question had become urgent. They made no recommendation.— Admiral Parry moved, and Mr. Lea seconded the adop- tion of tie report,—Mr. Williams said if the report were passed it would not lead to any' thing definite.—The report was adopted— Mr. Williams observed that the subject was gone into very fulls' some twelve months ago. He had not attended the meetings of the committee recently for reasons well-known, but when he had visited the house on several occasions it had been very decidedly impressed upon his mind that Mrs. Cunnah required more assist- ance. In April last, the Ladies Committee framed a recommendation that assistance be provided for Mrs. Cunnah but it was allowed to stand over, and in his opinion the time had now arrived when something should be done. Steps should be taken to carry out the recommendations of the Ladies' Committee. There was no one but old women to assist and the pressure upon the matron was very great. In order to bring the matter to a point he would move that the committee bring in a recommendation upon the subject at the next meeting. —Mr. Stokes said he had only been acting on the Visiting 1 Committee a short time but the fact was the matron was short of assistance. There were no able-bodied persons in the house at the present time who could render the assistance the matron required. He thought the Board should decide what steps should be taken. The com- mittee was a very small one and not thoroughly repre- sentstive.—Admiral Parry said he had already stated that assistance was needed, and he would give notice that at the next meeting he would move that an assistant to the matron be appointed. He would then be prepared to state what the assistant's duties should be. A SUGORSTION OF THE LADIES' COMMITTER. The Board considered the suggestion of the Ladies' Committee that the older girls in the house should play with the little ones.—The Master stated that the Local Governmert Board would not allow this as it was con- trary to the rules of classification. Mr. Derbyshire stated that the Matron reported to the Visiting Commit- tee that such an arrangement would interfere with the discipline of the girls themaelves.—The Board resolved that the suggestion could not be carried out for the rea- sons given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>FINANCII COMMITTEH.-ANOTHER BREEZN.</head>
        <p>FINANCII COMMITTEH.-ANOTHER BREEZN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Heywood In moving the adoption of t-h-;;;port of the Finance Committee, said there were 14 members pre- sent of the committee,and he hoped the members would look through the accounts before they passed the report. He might say there were no fewer than six stationery accounts to pass. Accounts amounting t0.£159 135.11. d. were recommended tor nayment. including for not te`i f for Runcorn £B5 ; Frodsham £3O; Budworth £2O. The balance in the treasurer's hands was £713 2s. 11d.—Mr. Derbyshire said the standing orders required that the re- ports of committees should be written. He wished to know where was a record of the meeting held that morn-</p>
        <p>irn....—Mr.Woodward rose to a point- of order.—Mr. Darbysbire : If the report is not written in ac- cordance with the standing orders, and if it were not handed to the Clerk.—The Chairman: You are out of order. He explained that the report was written—Mr. Darbyshire was continuing to speak when the Chairman said : Will you sit down.—Mr. Darby- shire :If you will stand up I will.—The Chairman : Sit down please.—Mr. Darbyshire : You stand up and it will settle matters. The report is not in accordance with the standing orders.—Mr. Williams: I must object to these trivial disturb:knees and waste of time. Mr. Derbyshire has already spoken five times on this matter. —Mr. Woodward said until the resolution was seconded, Mr. Darbyshire was out of order.—The Chairman : He has been ink' that.—Mr. Woodward then seconded the adoption of the report and remarked that the officials should be revelling in stationery. (Laughter.)—Mr. Darbyshire again stated that if the report was written it would be in order.—The Chairman : You have been told before, but would take no notice of it.—The report was then adopted. ' This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>CHURCH CLOTHING CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Friday last, the Church Clothing Club held its annual distribution at Trinity Schools, and on the fol- lowing day the Dorcas gifts were given to the assembly gathered at the 2arish Schools. The Rigby Charity is to be distributed as usual on All Saints' D iy. A CASE OF VAGRANCY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday morning, before Mr. E.3qn, a charge of begging was made against Jane Hughes, who stated that she came from Liverpool, in Church-street, Runcorn, on the previous night. P.C. Wylie proved the case. She was committed t ) gaol for seven days. IMPENDING DEPARTURE OF THE REV. A. J. JAMESON. In his monthly letter to his parishioners in the " Parish Magazine" for November. the Vicar of Runcorn men- tions that the above-named gentleman who has been in the parish for over five years has been appointed by the Bishop 3f the diocese to the incumbency of St. Augustine's, Brinksway, near Stockport.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>DRIJNICENNESS,</head>
        <p>DRIJNICENNESS,</p>
        <p>_ At Runcorn Police Court on Frid ty, before Messrs. F. J. Norman and E. liaison, a woman named Mary Lyne was charged under a warrant for not appearing to a summons on September 30th for be:ng drunk and disor- derly in Mersey-st.on the 9-h of that month. 'The prisoner pleaded guilty. She having several previous convictions recorded against her,a fine of 10s. and 15s. 4J. costs was imposed. In default of payment she went to gaol for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>FIRE IN A CHURCH NEAR RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>FIRE IN A CHURCH NEAR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Aston church narrowly escaped being destroyed by fire on Sunday morning. It appears that the sexton had made a fire in the vestry and during Ins absence s, me- thing had fallen and set fire to the floor. Smoke was seen issuing from the place, and Mr. Robinson, the schoolmaster, with his two sons went in and succeeded in putting out the fire. Had it not been noticed at the time the result might have been serious. Beyond the flooring and a surplice being burnt, no further damage was done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.</head>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.</p>
        <p>The weekly entertainmolt provided at. the Seamen's Institute was given on ruesday evening and was largely attended. Mr. W. Shaw presided and expressed the pleasure it gave him to see such a large number of sailors present. A programme of songs, quartettes and choruses, and also recitations and readings was provided by the choir which was under the conductorship of Mr. D. Price. Slogs were well rendered by Miss It indles, Mrs. R. Shaw, Messrs. Philip Shaw, S. Cross, J. Moore, J. Cliff. and W. Marsh. Recitations were c mtributed by Miss E. Mack, and a reading by Mr. Byfield. Mr. A. Price was the accompani4. At the close,a vote of thanks to those who had taken part, was passed. THE SUDDEN DEATH OF A CHILD.—INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. Worsley, coroner, held an inquest at Runcorn Police Station on Friday afternoon relative to the death of the infant son of George Henry Spencer,of 3.3.Taylor's- row, Runcorn. The child was born on the 21st mat, and on the 23rd it appeared to be suffering from a cold, and as it became worse, the father went for a doctor between three and four o'clo;k the next morning. Dr. McLennan was unable to come as he was suffering from e cold, and Dr. Robinson expressed himself unable to conae out. The child expired about four o'clock. Mrs. Bellfield, a neighbour, spoke to attending the mother at birth.—Dr. McDougall stated thae he had seen the child and he attributed death to convulsions. A verdict in accordance with the medical evidence was recorded. ANNUAL MEETING OF RUNCORN CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>..... _ Mr. R. Jeacock presided at the annual meeting of the Runcorn Cricket Club on Friday evening. Mr. H. Wright presented the treasurer's report, from which it appeared that there was a balance in hand of £l2 4s 41. Mr. G. S. Firth read the general report,shewing that 13 matche3 had been played, of which three were won, eight losa,and two drawn. The reports were adopted. The officers for the ensuing year were then appointed, namly, Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., president ; the vice-presidents were re-el,cted en bloc ; Mr. Jeacock, secretary ; Mr. F. H. Gorle, treasurer; Mr. S. Abrams, captain ; Mr. Jeaceck, sub-captain. The election of sub-captain for the second team was deferred. A committee of 12 was also selec- ted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PAINFULLY SUDDEN DEATH AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>PAINFULLY SUDDEN DEATH AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Worsley conducted an inquiry at Runcorn Police Court on Friday afternoon, relating Co the d-ath of Martha Cooke, 59 years of age, of 40, Robert-street, Runcorn, who died very sudel.mly the previous night.— John Cooke stated that his wife went out, and ten minutes after reaching home she expired.—Jane Cliff, of 11, Robert-street, stated that she saw deeessed sittingon a doorstep of a neighbour's house about 6 30 on the night previous. She could scarcely speak. Witness assisted her home. By the foreman of the jury Deceased's son, Harry, was in the street the night before creating a dis- turbance. This upset her a good deal—Margaret Dean, Robert-street. and Dr. McDougall al se gave evidence. The latter attributed death to heart disease. A verdict in accordance with the medical testimony was returned. The funeral of the deceased took place on Sunday.</p>
        <p>TIT&amp; VACANCY ON TUE BOARD (IF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Tuesday was the last date for the receipt of nomina- tions bY the returning officer. Mr. J. Ashtnn, for the elec- tion of a Guardian to fill the vacancy caused by the death of Mr. Thomas Handley, J.P., who represented Heath ward. By that time two nomina on papers had been handed in, and the Pr• sons nominated were the Rev. J. Simpson, Congregational minister, and Mr. Thomas Reynolds, Norman-road, who recently unsuccessfully contested Newton ward for a seat on the Urban Council. Th. Rev. J. Simpson was proposed by Mr. Jos. James Speakman, Stay '.y Villas, and seconded by Mr. John Speakman,24, Salisbury-at..: and Mr. Reynold's proposer is Mr. William Bate, Brookside, Norman-road, and Mr. John Wilson,of Oxford-road, is the seconder. Withdrawals can be received up to Saturday afternoon at four o'clock. BAND OF Hon ENTERTAINMENT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The first public meeting and entertainment for the season in connection with Brunswick Wesleyan Band of Elope, was held on Monday evening, and was very largely attended. Mr. T. Simister presided, and delivered a suitable address in which he urged upon those who had not signed the pledge to do so. A miscellaneous enter- tainment was then proceeded with. The Nelson-street Drum and Fife band, the members of which were dressed in their new outfit, played selections. A number of recitations, many of them having a temperance bearing, were given by the following :—William Sutton, Annie Sefton, William Burrows, John Parry, Florence Burrows, Thomas Rindley, Martha Basnett and Effie Taylor. Miss Smith very nicely rendered a violin solo, and songs werr contributed by the Misses Maggie Hill, Beatrice Eden, Lily Faulkner and G. Douglas. The choir sang the glee "Hark the tramping of an Army," and Misses E. Smitham and E. Prescott gave a duet. An excellent entertainment concluded with a dialogue entitled "Wanted, a young man," given by Messrs. J. Bisbrown, P. Faulkner, J. Simkin and E. Darlington.</p>
        <p>- - • Sr. PAUL'S MIMS CLkSS, RUNCORN. The usual monthly musical service in connection with St. Paul's Bible Class was held on Sunday afternoon.when there was a very large gathering. After devotional ex- ercises and the bible lesson, Mr. Crosby ably sang "Con- sider the lilies." He was followed by Miss Simkin singing Handel's "Silence and sorrow" in a very feeling manner. Mr. William Cooke performed an organ solo, after which Mr. J. Leathwood sang "The Garden of Prayer" in good fityle. Mrs. Ternperley rendered "My bud in Heaven " very sweetly, and by special request sang" Where will you spend eternity 2" Mr. Charles Atherton sane a new song entitled "Remember the Sabbath Day," in floe style. The wards of this song are taken from the fourth Commandment, and the music had been specially, com- posed by the organist of the class, Mr. William Cooke, upon whom it reflects considerable credit. A young alto of Liverpool, Master Ernest Harrison. sweetly sang "Is this all ?" Mr. William Ceoke was the accompanist. Mr. Kniveton, the president of the class, in a short address at the close thanked all who had taken part in the pro- gramme. Speaking of the great benefits to be derived from such services,he said that each of the solos,sang as they had heard them were sermons in themselves. The meeting was closed by the pronouncing of the Benedic- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>LADY EVANOELIST IN WESTON DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>A LADY EVANOELIST IN WESTON DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>A lady evangelist has been appointed by the Wesleyan denomination to work in Weston and Weston Point. Sister Annie is the person appointed, and she is from the training institution in Halifax, and has been engaged in similar work in London, Birmingham, Manchester,and other places. She commenced her labours on Sunday evening, when she conducted the service at Weston Point. Tha congregational tea meeting was held on Monday evening and was largely attended. A public meeting was afterwards held in the chapel,when the chair was occupied by Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P. • and a cordial welcome was given to Sister Annie. Addresses were de- livered tiv the Rev. T. Brackenbury, Rev. W.George. Mr. James Ford, and others. The lady evangelist suitably responded to the welcome given in appropriate terms. A similar gathering was held on Wednesday evening, when Mr. Norman again ()minis d the chair, and suitable ad dresses were delivered by the Revs. T. Rraekenbury, W George, and C. D. Putron Glidclon, and Messrs. Moore, Herrington, and sister Annie. The object in view In having the mission is to endeavour to reach as many per- sons as possible who are not accustomed to attend any pace of worship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CHILD BURNT TO DEATH AT</head>
        <p>A CHILD BURNT TO DEATH AT</p>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>THE MOTHER CENSURED. L At the Royal Oak Hotel, Weston, on Monday after- , nOOO, Mr. J. E. Worsley, cr.roner, conducted an inquiry relative to the death of Sarah &amp;lies Sefton, aged four years, daughter of Elizabeth Barnes, Company's-row, Weston. Eliz ibetla Barnes, mother,was the first witness called, and stated that she was the wife of Thomas Barnes, a plumber. On Saturday morning she rose at eight o'clock, lighted the fire, and placed the kettle c intaining, water close to. She left the house to go to a neighbour s, Mrs. Stafford,leaving tie deceased in the kitchen,and a three- year-old son in bed. She went out to obtain some water to brew the tea. Her daughter was fully dressed. She was away about a quarter of an hour or twenty minutes. On returning and opening the front door she saw the de- ceased standing by a rocking chair with her clothes burn- ing, and she exclaimed "Oh, mamma." Witness asked what she had been doing, and she replied "I was trying to get a drop of tea ready." She took the girl on her knee, and wrapped her legs in her apron. Witness called I for help and the next door neighbour came in, who ran ' for Mrs. Hazlehurst, who did her best to save the girl's life by using oil and wadding. A messenger was sent I for Dr. Robinson or Dr. McKenzie, but neither of them I came. The child died at twenty minutes past ten o'clock. 1 In answer to Inspector Hicks she.. said she had not been I in the habit of going regularly to the neighbour's house. She had gone sometimes when her husband was working at night. It had not been a usual practice of hers, but I she had gone now and again. 1 Mary Ann Stafford, wife of Edward Stafford, residing i in Company's Row, said Mrs. Barnes had been in the I habit of coming into her house in the morning for a cup of tea. She often stayed from ten minutes to a quarter of an hour. She stayed about that time on Saturday ' morning. She did not think that the little girl had ever made tea for her mother. Mrs. Annie Hazlehurst, wife of Thomas Hazlehurst, deposed that she was called in to see the chill after the ' occurrence. She applied oil and liniments to the wounds. She asked the child what she had been doing to get I burnt like that, and she replied that she had been mair• L ing tea.—The Coroner congratulated the witness upon L her kindly action. After the summing of the Coroner, the jury recorded a death from shock through injuries by being accident- ally burnt, aed at the same time requested the Coroner ' to severely censure the mother for the neglect of her child.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Barnes was:then called into the room, and cen- surdd by the Coroner.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Mclntyre, J.P.,presided at the fortnightly meet- ing of this Council on 'Tuesday. There were present Admiral Parry, J.P.,Messrs. J. Knowles, T. Baxtnr,C.C., J. L. Jones, T. Darlington, S. Woodward, E. Bretherton, E. Aaron, J. Spruce, J. M. Williams, H. E. Heywood, J. Brandreth, A. Greenway, W. Ellison, W. Pickering, J. Weir, J. Darbyshire, J. Stokes, G. Priestner, Jame., Whitlow, J. W. Baguley, T. Wright, J. Hall, and J. Ashton (clerk), G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer),Messrs. Swinton and Diggle (surveyors) and J. Farrington (nuisance inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NV- ISAN.CE AT V7ALTON INFERIOR,</head>
        <p>A NV- ISAN.CE AT V7ALTON INFERIOR,</p>
        <p>- The Walton Inferior Paris!) Council wrote drawieg at- tention to the fact that nothing had been done towards piping over the old brook between the Ship Canal and the river. The Parish Council was of an opinion that this work shoull be done by the Ship Canal Company as they had cut off the water supply, which formerly cleansed the brook.—Mr. Weir said a number of houses were now drained into the brook, and as the water had been diverted from the Ship Canal, a nuisance had in eensequence arisen.—lnspector Farrington replying to a question pot by the Chairman said there was no provi- sion made in the Ship Canal Act for culverting this breok. The overflow water from the Ship Canal went in the brook hut as the Ship Canal seldom overflowed, the brook had become a stageant sewage ditch.—Mr. Swinton said when the subject was last befere the Coun- cil it was resolved that the Ship Canal Company were to blame for the state of affairs existing,and the lerk com- municated with the Company who promised to look into the matter. The question still stood at that.—The Clerk was instructed to again write to the Ship Canal Company —Mr. Aaron stated that during the summer, nuisances had arisen through their sewers being cut off by the Ship Canal Company. The Council should be in possession of plans showing the sewers and streame draining into the Canal.—The Chairman said the cost had stood in the way of having plans when the question had been raised on previous occaeiens.—Mr. Heywood made a suggestion that the surveyors prepare the plans and that they be paid extra remuneratiun for the work.—lt was ultim- ately decided on the proposition of Mr. Heywood, seconded by Mr. Aaron, that a committee consisting of the members representing the townships whose newer., were connected with the Ship Canal, be formed to deal with the subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ROAD IMPROVEMENT.</head>
        <p>A ROAD IMPROVEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jones in a-cc-o-r-din-c;;;;Ch---r-totice given, moved that the Council contribute to the County Council the sum of £5O towards the repair and improvement of the Chester and Warrinv.ton main road at Lower Walton near the Stag Inn, He remarked that the road was now in a dangerous condition.—Mr. Darlington seconded and it was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>CLOSING OF SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>THE CLOSING OF SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles E. Linaker, clerk to the Daresbury School Board, wrote that the Board had passed resolutions in conformity with the recommendation of the Medical Officer of Health, relative to the closing of the Dares- bury, Hatton' and Moore schools. At the same time tht- Boprd thought the order of the medical officer somewhat premature as from the returns to the Board there ap- peared to be very few scarlet fever cases in the district. The school returns from Hatton for the three weeks end- ing 17th October gave a percentage of 90 attendances which he thought was an excellent report. The writer asked how many cases of fever had been reported to the District Council by the medical men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>DANGEROUS ROAD AT LUME BROOK.</head>
        <p>THE DANGEROUS ROAD AT LUME BROOK.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. U. 67,1-lie-r:7l;a;;WrOf Th-e-M—an-c-nester Ship Canal Company (Bridgewater Canals), wrote stating that the engineer of the company was making preparation.; for the repair of Lomb Brook roadway, and that the work would be done immediately.—Mr. Swinton stated that he had received a further communication from the Canal Company stating that the material had been ordered, and no further delay would be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ESTIX&amp;TES OF THE SURVEYORS.</head>
        <p>THE ESTIX&amp;TES OF THE SURVEYORS.</p>
        <p>The report of the committee appointed to consider tha Surveyors' estimates was presented by the Chairman, which proposed that Mr. Swinton's estimate be reduced by 15 per cent. They confirmed the estimate prepared by Mr. Diggle.—Mr. Aaron seconded.—Mr. D.irhyshire proposed an amendment that Mr. Swinton's estimate be accepted as drawn up. The roads were very bad, and needed the expenditure.—Mr. Darlington seconded the amendment.—ln supporting the motion, Mr. Heywood stated that Mr. Diggle's estimate was £1,861 or £2l 2s. 10d. per mile. Mr. Swinton's estimate was £2,190 or £26 is. sd. per mile. He thought there was as much traffic in Mr. Diggle's district as in Mr. Swinton's. It was unreasonable to expend such a large sum of money as that estimated for.—The original motion was carried by 13 votes to 10 votes.</p>
        <p>THE NIOHTSOIL QUESTION AT WESTON. A resolution passed at the special meeting of the Wes- ton Parish Council held on Oct. 25th for the purpose of receiving the committee appointed to consider the ques- tion of dealing with the nightsoil was submitted by Mr. J. Collier. the clerk. The resolution was as follows : tt That the Runcorn District Council be asked to provide a deposit bank in each ward for the nightsoil." The subject, he hoped, would receive early consideration.— Mr. Chadwick moved that a committee consisting of Messrs. Stokes, Aaron, Wright, and himself be appointed to discuss the question in conjunction with a committee of the Parish Council, and that the Clerk be isstructed to write asking a committee to be appointed by the Parish Council to meet the District Council's committee.</p>
        <p>THE BRITISH VISIT TO GERMAN AND BELGIAN IRONWORKS.</p>
        <p>. A Birmingham correspondent seates :—The delay in I . the publication of the report prepared by the deputation for the Critish Iron Trade Association on their visit to ! Belgian and German ironworks is attributable to the I action of certain Continental firms whose works are , Ireported upon. Correspondence is still proceeding between ! Mr. J. S. Jeans and the firms in question, as it is de- sired to place the facts upon which the report is based beyond contravention. It is stated that in taking this course the deputation are mainly anxious to satisfy ' public opinion on the Continent. Since the bitter attack of the " Cologne Gaz-tte " upon the " scouting "visit of the deputation it is regarded as essential that the state- ments contained in the report shall be absolutely proved 'by evidence from German and Belgian resources. Testi. mony of an unimpeachable character will therefore be attached to this document. The members of the depu- tation are fully agreed upon their recommendations. After most careful investigation it has been definitely proved that the aggregate cost of labour in the foreign iron works is eonsiderably in excess of that of English works, in spite of the fact that the remuneration is lower. This is due to the fact that three men are employed,as against two in this country. The operative sction of the deputations admit that the c'aiditions under which the Continental workmen live are superior to those environ- ing the English operatives, and account for it by the greater consideration paid to the foreign workman both by the Government and the employers,in order to arrest the growth of Slcialism. To the extent of having impressed the claims of the workers to better treatment the Socialist agitation on the C )ntinent has, in their opinion, been beneficial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams, in— his monthly report, -stated that the fol- lo wing cases of scarlet fever had been reported to him : —Stockton Heath, Appleton, 5 ; Alvanley, 1 ; Bradley, 4. A case of typhoid fever at Frodsham was also re- ported to him.—The report was adopted. NUISANCE INSPECTOR'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Farrington read a letter from Mr.A. Borthwick,of Widnes, in which he stated in reference to their pro- perty at Weston Point, that a short time ago they did what seemed sufficient, and they were charged £2O for the work done. The Authority now asked them to shut the house up. He was desirous of an interview with Mr. Aaron and the Inspector te see what arrangements could be made. They would comply with the wishes of the Council when the surplus water near the house was di- verted into another channel.—The Inspector said the £2O rnentiened was charged on the township of Weston, and the property had been condemned by the Medical Offi- cer, and he could do no other than serve the notice on the owners.—Mr. Aaron said the ashpits were a disgrace to a Christian country. The places were in a very filthy condition, and the people were placing the refuse in the roads.—No decision was come to, the Inspector remark- ing that the notice had not yet expired.—A certificate af occupation was granted to Mr. William Twiss, of War- rington, for two new houses erected by him in Stafford- st., Wilderspnol. A report of the proceedings relating to Helsby water supply will be found in another column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>CHURCH SERVICE AT DAREEIBUR,Y.</head>
        <p>CHURCH SERVICE AT DAREEIBUR,Y.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening a church service in connec- tion with Daresbury Church was given in the Church Institute, and will be continued during the winter months. The congregation was particularly large. The Rev. G. Gilbertson officiated, Miss Wakefield presiding at the organ. , Baron Hirsch has thus far sent about 4,000 Russian ' Hebrews to the Argentine Republic, ants hopes to have a Hebrew community there of 100,000 within ten years. He sends them oat in companies of fifty families each provided with a rabbi and a doctor, and he expects them to settle in the villages, giving a special tract for each company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WARRINGTON IN FI RUA ItY CONCERT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON IN FI RUA ItY CONCERT.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the annual concert organised by the Workingmen's Committee of the Warrington In- firmary on behalf of that, institution was given in the Parr Hall before a crowded audience, his Wor- ship the Mayor presiding. The performances of the orchestra of which our townsman, Mr. T. J. Down, iq the distinguished head were the chief attraction, but there were in addition some very interesting items in the programme from a soprano and a tenor vocalist, a solo 'cellist and a humorist. The march "Austria " (Nowstny), the overture " Banditens- treiche " (Sopp6), and the waltz by Gung'l " Amosetten Tauze " were undoubtedly the best efforts of the orchestra and the effect gained by the full complement of instruments was specially fine in the overture. The Dead March in Saul was ap- propriately rendered by the full orchestra in com- memoration of the death of Sir Charles Halle. The songs by Mesdame Marie Anderson were all pleas- ing in character and interpretation. It was quite a treat to hear once Again the old songs "The Old Folks at Home and "The Young Recruit" (liiicken), and the enthusiastic manner in which these were greeted by the audience was an ample justification for their revival, if any were ' n,eded. The audience scarcely caught the point of Sullivan's fine song " Take a pair of sparkling eyes "rendered by Mr. Ben Roberts otherwise it could hardly have missed being a success. " Come into the Garden Maud," sung later by Mr. Roberts in response to an encore was a happy hit and elicited loud applause. The humorist wss somewhat disappointing in his opening song but later in the delineations of well-known public men of the past and present generations he was more at home and his efforts served to give pleasing variety to the programme. The 'cello solos were of a high order and M. Van Biene cannot complain of the co:dness of his reception in Warrington after an absence of some 16 years. Encores were, of course, vociferously demanded on each appearance of the virtnoso, and the manner in which M. Van Biene rendered the theme " Home sweet home" in response to one of these recalls served to show how much success depends rather on the degree of soulfulness which can be thrown into a simple air than upon brilliant and often meretricious execu- tion. M. Van Biene has unfortunately some very eccentric mannerisms in deportment and had the audience not been so entirely good humoured as they were the display of these would have detracted largely from their appreciation of his undoubted talents. It is to be regretted that the concert which was already unduly prolonged should have been broken into in the middle of the second part with the proposition of votes of thanks to the Mayor and to Mr. Down which were not only superfluous bu:; we venture to think, distasteful to those whom they were intented to compliment. At least a quarter of an hour was taken up in this way by speeches from Mr. John Roberts, Mr. Francombe, Mr. Morris and Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., whereby the attention of the audience was thoroughly dis- turbed and the premature dispersion brought about of a large portion of those who would otherwise probably have stayed to the end of the very inter- esting programme. We would suggest that, in future concerts these formal and unannounced proceedings might advantageously be dispensed with.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. _ . .</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire representa- tives on the Committee of the Northern Rugby Union, governing the above competition, was held at the Spread Eagle Hotel, Manchester, on Thursday evening. Mr. J. E. Warren (Warrington) presided, and amongst those , present were Messrs. E. Gresty (Broughton Rangers), J. Underwood (Wigan), F. Hewlett (St. Helens), E. Roth- well (Rochdale Hornets), H. Rowson ( rvidesley), J. Lang,shaw (Runcorn), J. M. Mills (Stockport), J. H. Smith (Widnes), and J. Platt (Oldham), hon. secretary. Mr. Boydell (Leigh) sent in his resignation as a mernber of the Committee, which was accepted.—A resolution was passed requesting the Leigh club to nominate an- other representative. It was also resolved that in future applications from secretaries of clubs for the registration of players should be accompanied by a written applica- tion from the players concerned. C. Wilding was trans- ferred from Bradford to Wigan, and G. Derbyshire from Runcorn to Widnes.—&amp; resolution was adopted, to the effect that in case of any player wishing to be transferred before a meeting of the Committee governing tne compe- tition and a Saturday intervening, the secretary shall be empowered to give a provisional transfer.</p>
        <p>GENERAL (good) WANTED, ante to wash, must have good references. for sm ti le roily, no children.—Apply, by letter only. 20, NORTHENDI.N-ROAD, Sale.</p>
