        <p>SAD END OF A ST; 11 RLENB CADMAN AT SWTON.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday mor-Milg—t;bout 5.15, a man named Samuel Hill, of 39 Regent-street, Earles- town, when going to his work at Ashton Green.</p>
        <p>, -- Colliery, by way of Newton Common and St.. Helens-road, noticed a horse and cab standing in the road, and on getting up to it he found the dead body of a man lying in front of the horse He gave information and the body was . conveyed. to the Pear Tree Inn, Collins Green, to await an in-</p>
        <p>- quest. On enquiry it turned out that the body ! was that of Stephen Brisknan, 66 years of age, of 112, Boundary-road, St. Helens, a cabman in the employ of the Dixon Fletcher Cab and Carriage. Company, St. Helena. He left St. Helens a little before 12 o'clock on Monday night for the purpose lof driving three young men to Earlestown. The night was very foggy, and when they had reached the road leading from Collins Green into the St. Helens-road, the young fellows dismissed the cab, , telling the driver they would walk home from there seeing the fog was so thick. Then driver then turned back and when the men left him h'iN was leading the horse, being at the time perfectly sober. After going about half a mile it is supposed be had a fainting fit, and falling in front of the horse, the animal came to a dead stand. He must ~ have lain in this position some three or four hours, and the exposure was no doubt the cause of death. An inquest will be held at the Pear Tree Inn, Collins Green, on Thursday, by Mr. Brighouse, when evidence bearing out the forgoing facts was adduced, and a verdict of " Death from natural causes "was returned.</p>
