        <p>In th-e-0-ct-o-b-er—n—umber of the above -popular magazine is a song of which the words and musie have been composed by Mrs. G. Hubi-Newcombe, Penketh, Warrington. The song is .an excellent one and of some length, no less than eight pages of the magazine being occupied by it. In character it is Irish, lively both in air and verse, and the. Otte is " Irish Potheen."— Fwrso MACHINES, Hand £2 I.Bs. 6d. treadle £4 41. "nun ndsonie walnut oover._,T and w. Durren,l6 and I.Sankey-st.. Warrington.—Adv. ACCIDENT TO A CHESHIRE LINES ENGINE.</p>
        <p>An accident occurred to the 9.40 p.m. train from. Manchester on 6unday night, between Glazebrook. and Padgate,by which a number of passengers were delayed for several hours. It appears that the tyre of one of the wheels of the engine broke, and after a tedious wait the passengers were brought on to Warrington by another train, arriving there at 1 a.m. instead of 10.10 p.m.</p>
