        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROrGasCOHRT. Fafna.e.—Before Alderman Wright (eludrosan), Dr. Gera&amp;ll and Couneillor Roberts. PROSECUTIIN NT THE INLAND REYENI7E.—UNREGISTERED CARRIAGES. Dennis Donnetlan, 25. Kendrick-street, was charged with keeping two carriages without an excise license on the 12th September.—Mr. Thomas May, appeared for the Inland Revenue, and sail the de estdant ergs liable to a penalty of £2O. On the 12th September Mr. Price called at the yard where defendant kept his traps, and saw there two, and on the day after he saw Mr. Donnellan, who said he would take out the license. On the 21st July Mr. Price saw him driving a high dog cart. and later the other convey- ance. Defendant took out one license but contended that he was exea pt from the other, as it was a horse-breaking con- veyance and not for passengers.—Cor oborstive evidence was given by Mr. Priee.—Defendent pleaded ignorance of the law,'and said that one vehicle was for horse-breaking pur- poses only.—Mr. May said this vehicle also was a carriage under the meaning of the Act.—A penalty of 20s. and costs -was imposed. NEIGITEGURS AT TARlANCE..—Marcaret Randles,Bestock-st., was charged with using threats towards Ann Williams, Bos- tock-st., on the 7th October.—Mr. Browne represented the complainant, and stated that defendant entered his client's house' on Thtwsday afternoon aid Mrs. Williams tanned her out. She then used threats and came again on Saturday night and repeated the threats.—Mr .aud Mrs. Williams and a a neighbour corroborated.—Mrs. Maden, a neighbour of de- fendant, said that Mrs. Williams threater-ea to knock Mrs. Bandies' brains out.—Defen 'ant was bound over to keep the peace for six months, and pay the costs (,63 ). ILLTREATING A HOR.E.—Altred Lewis. 88. Chorley-street, was fined the costs, 4. 6d., for illtreat ing a horse on the 12th Oetober.—ln:pectur Luff ;add the anima/ was lame and de-1 fendant lashed it through sev-a al streets. USING THREATs.—Williano Brown was charged with threatening li. rbert Davies on the 19th October.—Davies said defendant threatened to kill him for -ging to his wife to collect a deht.—Defendant stated that enmplainard had used foul language to his wile. —The magistrate said they dead leniently with the case in fining defendant Si. MONDAT.-13efore the Mayor (Chairman). and Alderman Franoomb. DisORDEHLT INES. !NUTS, —Martin Tows], Preston ttrusk, was dismissed with a caution for being drunk and disorderly In Wilderspool-road n Saturday night ; for the same offence Ellen Johnson, 9millatto. woe sent to gaol for 7 days. Mucus Marna IN Burous-feraarr.—John Hughes, Dale-st., Sankey Bridges, was charged with furiously driving . a horse and trap in 13, idge-street on the 22nd October..--tie, geant Rowbottom proved the case and said that when ap- proaching the Bridge the defendant almost collided with a trap coming frost Arpley When accosted the defendant jeered at witness and P.C. Beecham and said he might be Charged with murder. He was soher.—P.C. Beecham corro- borated. and defendant was fined ss. and cots. BELA), I..TRING. A COW WITH A STICIC.—John ChwtOrl (16), 97 Tanners-lane, and Samuel Hicham (1517 Roger-street, were eharded with cruelly ill-treating a cow by beating it with a s'ick on the 21st Oetober.—Gorton pleaded guilty and Higham net guilty.—P.C. Delahrty asserted that be saw the two defendants in Buttermarket-street on the day in question slid when they reached Orford-siret t Gorton who was armed with a heavy stick, commenced beating the ardent on the ribs and stomach whi'e hold of its tail. Higham was strik- ing it on the thee side. When Witness went to them Gorton admitted the offenee and said he was sorry,- Walter Harmon, carter, corroborated and the magistrates ordered de- fendants to be.detained on prison fare Oil 6 o'clock at night, observing that they were sorry the defendants were too old I to be birched. TITESDAY.—Befere Mr. R. Garnett (chairman). Dr. T. Starkey Smith and E. J. Gast. DEUNKENNESS.—john Hughes was discharged with a ' caution for being drunk and di-orderly in Irlam-street, the previous night. P. 9 Chantlerproved the case. IRREGULAR SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.—For neglecting to send their children regularly to school r William Jones, 35, Pier- point-street. was adjourned; Thomas Chrimes, Warburton- street, fined 55. ; Richard Tranter, Warhurton-street, la. William O'Connor, Cookhedge-lane, 2s. 6d. Mr. William Jones. school att nciance officer, prosecuted. WEDNENDAT.—Before Mr. J. Fairclough and Mr. J. H. Fleming. A DRUNTEN MAN ruLL-gri OUT OF THE RIVER.—DIren Owen, Old Barrac s, was charged with having been drunk in Wharf-st. at 7 25 on Tuesday night.—P.C. Coreoran said the defendant had tumbled into the river out of a small boatand when pulled out by some men from the Castle Rubber Works, he was unconscious, and seemed In a riving condi- tion.—lnspector Spinks said it was three o'clock in the morn- ing before defendant cama round. Brandy was given him, but he was drunk previously. The case was withdrawn on 'tot 10s. 6d. for the doctor's fee, and Is. 6d. costs.</p>
        <p>payment ,</p>
        <p>- - NEWTON PETTV SESSIONS. SATITaDSY.—Before Mr. T. J. Gillespie (chairman), Colonel J. L. Wood, Mr. R. Stone and 31r. T. D. Grimk e Drayton. DOGS ST LARGE. —For allowing their dogs V) be at large: William Harrison. Ilayioek, was fined 3s. 6d. ; William Cardwell, Golbeene, 3s. 6d.; Benjamin Witclsworth,Haydcek, ha. • James Jackson, Haydock, to. P'ULL/NO tP POTATOES—Thomas MBIIIOII was charged witia being drunk and disorderly in Hardock. "Ti September 20th, and was also charg d -with stealing a cinant'ty of potatoes, valued 2d., the property of William halharry. Defendant pleaded guilty to the first charge, and Mr. Stuart said, with regard to the second offence, that Mr. Simpson, who was in Mr. Balherry's employ, saw the defe•idant in a fled with hi, poakets full cf potatoes, and it could be seen that several roots had been pul'ed up. Defendant was fined ICs and costs, for the theft, and ss. and costs for the drunkenness. DRUICILENNE.,S.—For this offence. Patrick Lyons, Haydock. was fined Si. and costs ; William Dwyer, Heydock. be. and costs. OBSCENE LATIOU•GE..—Mary Rdwards was fined 26.64. for using obscene language in Viaduct-street. Earlestown, on October 19th. GIVING THE BETEEFIT OF THE DOUBT —Sam lel and Henry Mayne' were each charged with stealing, sometime between the 19th and 20th October, a Leghorn hen, the property of Jeffrey Pemsett, and also a ' inoasa hen, the property of George Naylor. Mr. 't. Stuart rouresented the defends' M. Bram% Bennett, Crow-lane, identified the Leghorn hen. She last saw it alive on Saturday. and the neat time she saw it was at the police station. She had never sees the two defendants before.-- Anne Jane Grundy. servant at the Swan Inn, kept by George Naylor, aitv. identified the Minorca hen, but she also had not peen the dotendants previously.—Thomae Jones said that he had known the defendants for 15 years. On Sunday morning he met them in Crow-lane. Samuel at tie time carrying a red handkerchief with something in it. Witness asked Harry what ha had go' , and the latter replied " Sam's got a rabbit." Witnesa thought that they had been poaohing and put them on their guard against the police. (Laughter.) He saw Harry again td.oron and asked him if he bad got shut," and Jo arry replied that he Ihad. Witness then said "good biz.' (Lau.,hter )—George Brown also de need to seeing the defendants in Crow-Inc on Pundav morning. and when they saw a police officer, they commenced to vial, quickly. W tness then told the policeman that he tionght they had got something whi h did not belong to them. The officer then wel't after them.— P C. Tibney said that about 10 15 on Sunday morning he saw the two defender ts goirg along Crow lane. Samuel carrying a red handkerchief wr4pped round something e to towed them, and when near the Swan Inn. Samuel threw the bundle away, and both of tin m then ran eve ‘y. Wlto ens was unable to catch them, and he then returned io secured the parcel which contain. ii the two hens produced fife apprehended tbe two defendants en Monday, and when charged they asked who saw them take the fowls. Tbe two defendants lived about half a mile fiom Crow-line.—Mr. :Stuart said th,i t he would call sotnesses to prove that et tho time stated by the officer, the Iwo defendants were at home, lie claimed that if there wereany doubt as to the case the defendants ought to be allowed the benefit of it —John ['Junes, Mrs. I omas, and Mrs. Maynes were each cslled,,ttd swore that at 10.15, Harry was cleaning his shoes at hand, and Samuel was also in the Douse and neither of th -rs went ..ut unt,l near eleven o'clock. —The Magistrates retired to ccnsider their deci.ion and on returning, Mr. Gilles, le said that there appeared to be a doubt about the matter, and the defendants would have the beoefit 001 it. They were however of opinion hAt some very disgrace- ful perjury had been committed that morning.</p>
        <p>--- RUNCORN PEVTY SESSIONS. MONDAY —Before Dr. St, ele (chairman), a,cl Mr. B. Imison. NORTON POOR RATE —The magi Rtrates scared their signa- tures to a poo- r.te of Is in the 2. for the township of Nor- ton, estimated to produce £354 is. Bd.</p>
        <p>Tuu NitTZZUNO ORn/qt.—Joseph qixon, IV—Well-lane. au I William C -bley. Pryer at , were summoned for allowing tho:r dogs to be at. I,rce narrinzzled.—uperintendent Athorton stated that the Muzzling Order hod now been revoked. Th.. . Chairman said he wIS very glad that it had. He though.. it ' stots a most cruel thing and he had quite made up his mind ; that he wonld not adjudicate in such eases. He had not the powsr to dismiss the case but he should &amp;cline to hear it Superintendent Atherton said those were the only cases which would be hewrd, no other information would be laid. He would leave them with the rnagi,drates.—The Chairmen said he would not at to hear those Cases. Thov could stand over. He did not believe in the NTIIZZ ing Ord, r : if it was made general oil over the country it might d., some good,bnt It was now en'orced in one district and then an Aher.— Super- tewtent Atherton said under the eirenmstance: be would ask to w'thdraw the summonses. This was agresd to. OWCEIVE LASGUAGE.—Vary Alai Glover and Selina nibtin were summoned .or wing ~tis ene and p-ofane I An- guage in 111orsev-stseet Ghver did 'no apooar. P.O. Richardson preyed the case against. Oiblin, atld she was fined ss. and costs or in default 14 days' imprisonment. he went to prison Onevannryso THE FOOTPATH.—Thomas TtavenSCrOft, a youth, was summoned for havit.g unlawfully and wilfully obstructed the footway in Egerton-street by loitering thereon. He pleaded guilty and was ordered to pay the costs. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.—For ntitgleCtino to cause his children to attend school regularly. Edward ilague, a boatman, was fined 10s. inCluding costs.</p>
