      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>FRODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. Thomas (chairman), and Mr.</p>
        <p>Rigby.</p>
        <p>Pram RATES.—The magistrates affixed their signatures to a new poor rate of is. in the £ for Frodsham Township, estimated to produce £172 158„&amp;i.. and also one for a similar amount for Alvantey to realis i £P7 108. A FR WMANE WATER. SUPPLY CASE.—At the instance of I ' Inspector Farrington of the Rune.. n Rural Council an I application was made for an order far permission to enter the I prem sea of Mr. Pre W. Dunsford, chemist, Main-street, I Frodsham, for the purpose of • eking measurements; to en- , able him to issue applications for tender, t r the work of . affixing water pipes to the premises. Mr A Browne, solici- , tor, Warrington. represented the Rune Tr' Council, and !Mr. Drans'ord conducted his own case.—Mr. Browne stated that at the last sessions he anplied for a similar order against another gentleman in Frodshun, and the order was grAnted. He wee not. going to labour the c,se, but would 'feet, state that they were apnlying for an order so that Mr. Dunsford should have water put upon his premises. and so relieve the rest of the ratepayers. The on'y objection that could be tuned against the order Was that there was already a proper slippy, They (the Runcorn 0 wanctl) said it was I‘,.t a proper supply. Mr Dunsford had to prove that he hid a good and proper supply, and if he failed to do so an order should be. made—Mr Farrington gave evidence as tn takieg a s-mple of the water used by the defendant, and submitting it to an analvist. He reported the result of the analysis to the committee and he was instructed to servea notice on the dafendant. This he did by retest red letter. No notice was takee of It, by the dsfendant, and whets he (Mr Farrington) called to get nartieulars tor the purp,a,e of laving waterpines to Mr. Dunsford's premises. le courteously ref ased him per- mission,—"dr. Dimsford asked for an adjournment if the case. He had sent a sample of the water for an ilysis but had not received the report. He only receive i the notice to attend that Court on Saturday and there had not been suf- ficient titine.—lt was pointed out that a notice w,a sarved on Mr Durisferd en May 28th.—The Chairman: Why don't yo settle the matter ?—',4r. Dunsforcl : Simply because I dee*- , want the public water supnly ' I havers good and suffic •I supply of my own. During the winter, many of the people who had the public water supply were without water whereas I had a good and plentiful supply during the whole time. The eas i hung 'on the one ' point whether the w iter was good, and that was why he asked I fir an adjournment. —Mr. Browns did not oliject lo an I ajtinunament if the defendant was willing to pay the.expenses. The Chai-man said it was rather a serious ,',IFIR for an adjournment, as he understood there was a case of tephoid fever If the defendant would take his advice he wml,l have the public supply, as it would cost as mu 'h for an adjournment as to lay the pipes on.—Mr. Dunsford eventually sail he would gor • Way, hilt he protested against it al; the sae e. The ease had not been tried on its merits.—Mr. Browne sail he WRS instructed to ask for costs, and the magistrates mid. an order and ordered the defendant to pay the court fees. Ss. 61. SUNDAY Dstrvint6—A PUELTC i a 17INED.—Methrs4ath Vicars, of the Volunteer Inn beerhouse, was summoned for onening his licensed premises during prohibited hours (9 a.m.) on Sunday. the 22nd September —Mr. Llnaker appeared for the defence. Sergeant Clays stated that about 8.30 on the morning of the dateMamed in company with P.C. Price he saw three men proceed down an entry towards the Volun- teer Inn, and a short time afterwards 'four other men went down the same entry. They followed them soon after, and In the back kitehen of the Vo'unteer Inn he `SW seven men sitting round a table on which were I wo qnsrt jugs filled with beer. One of the men was named Miahael Joyce wh ) worked at Dodacroft farm, Frodmisam. All the men with one excep- tion stated that they had not been as'-e,l whether they had 1 travelled the required distance.—P.C. Prie- gave eorrobera- tire evidence. and stated that h. had seen the man Joyce in the beerhoose on many oecasions.—Mr. Linaker applied for an adjnnrnment ot the ease. He submitted that there was no proof that the men were not entitled ti go into stey house and ask for something to eat and drink—Inspector Johnson said he objected to the adjournment, and the magistrates de- cided to pro meet with the case.—The defendant said the men were Irish labourers Fi,d were going away. They had a num- ber of bundles. He askedthem where they had come from. mid they told him they had cm. from Mickle Trafford. -He then provided them with the beer which the officers saw. Re did not know one of the men.—The defendant's two Sons al ,C. gave evi- dence as tattle men being asked where theyearee from when they came to the house. —The magiatr , tea imoosed a fine of 205., and costs, 13s. 64.—Mr Linaker said he wished ti ex- plain that if the passage in question was an inconvenience to the police, his clients were prepared to put it in such a way that nothing of the kind would oceur again.—A summons had been issued against Michael Joyce for being tonna on the lieenscd premises. As be did not appear, a warrant was , issued.</p>
        <p>DRUNK ON LTCRICHRD Garner. labourer. was summoned for being drunk on the licensed Premises of Ur. J. Forster, at Frodsham, on the 13th Oct.-- PC. Price stated that on the date named he vi 'Had the Albert Inn. In the tan-room he saw the defendant very drunk The landlord told him that the man had only just come in, and he had not been served wlth deink at the house. The magistrates fined the defendant £1 and Bs. Bd costs or one months' imprisonment. THE MUZZLING ORDEI.—FINES INFLICTED.—The following! were fined ss. including costs for al owing their dogs ts be at large unmuzzled —J .met Andrews, farmer, Frodsham ; Coarles H. Linsker, Frodsham ; JAMAS pruee. Alyanley DRUKK AND DISORDERLY.—SamueI Bross n. jun.. of Trod- sham, was fined 10s. and costs, for being drunk and disorderly at Frodsham en the 19th October. SCHOOL ATTEND/NM—An attendance or ler was made for the child of James Holland, Cliff View, to attend Ov rton ache further application was made by Mr. Jones, schnnt attendance officer, for an attendance order to h made against the son of Mrs. Alice GaTter, of Newtown —Mr. Williams in supporting the application said it was the intention of the committi eto have the lad sent to an industrial school. His mother WAS fined 5s a si,o:t time ago an I she had to beg ths money. The lad was up to all kinds of" devilment," and had got some romantic I. into his head. The previous week he stole a shilling from his in ther and spent t in a knife and cigarettes. He would not go to school. aril his mother WAS greatly handicaopel in earning a livelihood. In her absence he pot her fires out with w 'ter, and slept out at night.--1 be magistrates granted the Order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>-- TUESDAY.—Before Mr. J. CharHon-PArr (in ttv, chair), and Messrs. W. Long, H. Tinsley, H. Nand, H. Talbot and C.</p>
        <p>tf.Broadbent. - DOGS AT LARGE.—ThP folton log persons were fined for having allowed their dogs to run at large unnauszled :—John Pemberton. Appletnn, ss. end costs ; Thomas Plerpotnt Walton, Is. 6d. including costs ; and Levi James. Latch'or I, ordered to pay the costs. Smoot. CesEs —Alfred Kirk and James Curhishlev were charged with having neglected to send their children t —Each case was adjourned fax one month.—Mr. F. J, plans prosecuted. DRUNEENNESS.—James Simcock was charged nith }toying n drunk and disorderly at Appleton on the 18th hitt ,and the case being proved, defendant was fined ss. including costs ASSAI7LT.—CharIeS Hsmp on was charged with having assaulted Thomas Mountield .m the 201 h October at Wh'tley. —complainant said th,t on the day In question, which was a Sunday, defendant was singing songs in a lane as persons were coming from Chapol. lie (witnes,) as'(ed Hammon to desist whereupon he ran at him and struck hfro.—Defendant denied the assault, but was ultimately bound over to keep the peace in the sum of £5 for three months. TEMPORARY AUTHORITY.—EIizAboth Ellen Atherton applied for •emporary authority to sell at the Talbot Arms. Dutton. in lieu of John Tomlinson.—The applicatem was granted. THE MUZZLING ORIPrR.—The Chairman intorrne 4 the Clerk (Mr. Greenall) that the Niuzz'ing Order had h-en I.landonei in Ch.shira. and. Ws was afterwards made known to the per sons assembled In court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>MESDAY.—Before his Honour Judge Ffonlkee. r ANOTHER Monet Les:pima TRANSACTION.—Jnhn Henry iHarrison, of Watarion-roal, Runcorn. sued James Shaw, MU:street. Runcorn, and Thomas Woodier, Suffolk-t.- for the SUM ef £5 83. 91. Mr. Lake, solicitor, repre Panted the plaintiff and stated that the claim was for the amount mentioned. being balance due on a promissory note dated May 18th for £6. There was no dispute re- sp-cting the signing of the note' but a miserviergam. Mg seemed to have arisen about the balance which was due. He gave the items making no the sum total which included an old balance of £3 4s. 14 , and a sum of 30s. interest.—Woedier said he was under the impression that he was only signing for 305., but he afterwards ad- mitted tithe judge that he wag already surety for the previous debt.—An order of 3s. per month was made. ADMINISTRATION ORDER —An application for an 1,1. ministration order was made by Mr. Joseph Mountoeld, 5, Leinster-et., Runcorn. His debts, alcording to the statement he presented to the Court, amounted to Rile 12e. 81. He offered so pay a 91118 nf 4a. in the £ in pay- ments of se. monthly. Fur creditors objected, and Mr. J. W. Allen,snlicitor,appeared on behalf of Mr. E. Bala- ford, Warrington, to mane° the order. His Honour made an order of 63. in tile £ to be paid in instalments of 59. a month. DAMAGE DONE BY • Cow claim for 103. was made by John Edwards, publican, Pereival4 Ine, against Thomas Bel!. butcher, Lowlands-road, for d images cauged by the defendant's cow enterirg. his house and demolishing a shelf containing various enofiq, and deine other damage. The plaintiff stated that the driv•tr was a little careless in driving the Animal, and as a result the onw entered his house and forced itself into the hack kitchen where it knocked a shelf over and broke several things. He asked the boy to tell the defendant of the occurrence. The defendant had nsger come to the h-use, but when he met him in a trap one day he offered to pay 2s. His Honour held that everybody had a right to drive a cow alone the hie,hway and Unless there was something to rausc the animal in into the house, plaintiff could not claim damage 9, and he would therefore dismiss the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVMEBER 2, 1895,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVMEBER 2, 1895,</p>
        <p>FOOT BALL.</p>
        <p>180. G. BUSSEY &amp; CO., are the leaeing makers of appli- -3 snafu, for Football, Hockey. Golf, Lacrosse and all SpoasamiGarnes,also for Gymnasium and Physical Exercises. Illustrated Catalogue msy be obtained from Local Dealers or the SPORTS MANUFACTORY, London S.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>RUGBY. THE NORTEJERN UNION. WARRINGTON V. STOCKPORT.</head>
        <p>RUGBY. THE NORTEJERN UNION. WARRINGTON V. STOCKPORT.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and Stockport teams have usually met on Mondays in previous years, the match at Stock- port being on tho "wakes" Monday. This year, how- ever, the arrangementsfiave been altered, and the first of their meetings this season took place on Saturday. The match was well attended, for there were about 4,000 spectators present, despite a counter attraction, in which Stockport County were engaged in a cup tie for the Manchester cup. Each team had to deplore the loss of prominent players, for Warrington for the first time this season had to take the field without their captain, F. Barber, who was hurt at the county match between Lancashire and Cheshire on the previous Monday. Fors- ter, who did so creditably against Broughton Rangers, took Barber's place in the threequarter line, and Burton assumed command of the team. For a similar reason to Barber, Saville was unable to play, awl Chorlton was full back for Stockport The weather was all that could be desired during the progress of the match, but owing to the very heavy rain which had fallen during the previous day the ground was very heavy. Mr. Slavin of Wigan was the referee, and the following were the teams under his control. Warrington: Heesom, full back; Forster' Burton, ' Cross and Broady, threequarters; Nevins and Hough- ton, half backs; Burrows, Saunders, Fairhurst, Wilcock, Hill, Andrews, Barrett, and Thornton forwards. Stockport : Chorlt3O, full back; William Bailey, W ors- ley, Sands, and Robinson. threequarters ; Harrison and Allen, half backs; Mottershead, Walter Bailey, More- ton, Speet, Gibson, Hallworth, Wrigley, and Roberts, forwards. Burton won the toss, but this was of very little advan- tage. Walter Bailey kicked oft and Houghton's return svaesupplemented by a dribble to the centre line, and for a few moments, Warrington crossed the neutral por- tion of,tbe field. William Bailey however had a slight advantage in exchange kicks with Heesorn, and the Stockport forwards looked like getting away by a com- bined dribble, but Burrows stayed them before they got properly on the stride. A round of passing between Harrison, Worsley, and Bailey looked ominous for War- rington, and who were greatly relieved when the last mentioned player mulled the ball, and knocked it for- ward almost on trie line. Following this let off, Forster secured and his pass was well taken by Cross, but the latter was not able to make much head way owing to ag well tackled. A free kick was granted to Stock- ! ,rt in a favourable position, hut they were unable to erive much benefit thereby, and the Warrington for- wards headed by Thornton, Saunders, and Andrews, took the ball into safe quarters, Another free kick was granted to Stockport. but this was charged down, and fine dribbling looked ominous for Stockport, but Barrett was at length tackled with the ball in his possession and the‘Cheshirernen speedily regained their lost ground ,only however to be sent back by Burton who by a tremend- ous kick put Warrington on the aggressive. The Ches- hire forwards again dribbled (lowa the field but Broady robbing them of the ball, by a judicious kick in touch made them beat a retreat. Burrows and Fairhurst passed well but the latter was tackled near the line. After re- gaining a little ground, Stockport were penalised, and Broady taking the kick failed to place a goal and only a minor resulted. After the kick out, Stockport became dangerous and R I)insois was just prevented from scor- ing, by Broady,and the ensuing scrimmage being carried by the Warrington forwards, neutral territory was reached. Stockport again returned to the attack, but Burton by a marvellous run through the thick of his opponents for a time kept theta at bay, and Hill and Barrett also gained considerable ground Burton again forced his way through the opposing for- wards and was finally brought to ground by Worsley. Warrington still progressed, and had reached the home 25, when suddenly amid loud cheering, the whole of the Stockport team suddenly commenced a dribble which was not stayed until the opposite ead of the field was reached, Fairhurst pluckily dropping on the ball when it was almost over. The cheering lead scarcely ceased, when a smart round of passing between Robinson,Sands, Worsley and Bailey ended in the latter scoring near the corner. Worsley took the kick, and just missed landing a goal, the ball hitting the pests and then falling outside. Broady re-started the game and made a tremendous kick, hut as it went in touch well in the Stockport portion of the fielri, the hall was called back again. His second kick was replied to by Rokinson and for a short time, play continued with Stockport aggressive. A dribble by Hill, Burrows and Wilcock raised this attack, and War- rington gained the upper hand ,Burton kicking into touch caused a line out on the coveted line, and though this was repulsed and a scrimmage formed in slightly safer quarters, Nevins suddenly secured the ball and rushed to the line, and passed to Wilcock, who promptly deposited it on the right side of the line, and put the teams level once more. Burton failed to improve the point. Play continued in favour of Warrington, and a favourable opportunity was riven to them to gain the lead by a free kick, but Broad v failed to land a goal,and half time arrived with the teams on level terms, each having scored a try. Hill kicked off after the interval, but owing to several of the Warrington team being offrside,a scrimmage was ordered In the centre. Stockport were the aggressors for a time, and Wilcock stopped a dribble of their ferwards. Burton, however, sent them to the right about, and for fully three-quarters of this portion of the game they were kept busily 'defending their lines. Passing by Hill Thornton and Saunders was ended by the latter being tackled by Robinson, and a free kick to Broady raised the hopes of the Warringtoniane. The kick, however, sent the ball over the line, and Robinson touched down. This brought no relief to Stockpmt, for Warrington were again to the fore, and every attempt to break away was promptly nipped in the bud, Burton being especially prominent. A grand chance of scoring now presented itself, when Nevins passed across a scrimmage to Houghton, who passed back to Burton,he to Cross, and the latter to Fos- ter. The latter was, howevetnot quite quick enough in getting away,and Bailey manaeed to tackle him in time, and a grand chance was lost. The scrimmage which followed this almost ended dieastrously for Stockportfor thelorwards carried it in fine style' and Forster was very unfortunate in being tackled by Bailey just on the line. itockport again tried to break away, but they found the übiquitous Burton re- peatedly in their way. In fact, the kicking running, and tackling of Burton was the feature of the match, the spectators affirming that a in ne brilliant centre tnree quarter had not been on the Stockport ground this season. After defending desperately, but successfully for more than 25 minutes, a long kick by Chorlton gave Stockport relief, and though they failed to cross the half way line just then they were not Tars so hard pressed as they had been. Houghton and Hee- rlen gained successive marks but these came to nothing, and people were making up their minds that the garne would be a draw,when a cheer from the spectator drew attention to the fact, that the Stockpot.; forwards had crossed the half way line with no ene to oppose them. They bungled their chance, however, and immediately afterwards the whistle blew with the teams still a try each to their credit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WIDNES v. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>EIeCUI</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes on Saturday, before a gate of about 5,000 persons. Th 9 following were the teams :—Widnes: Golding, back ; Brennan, Rispan, Nevitt, Archer, three- crafters • Barber and Drummond, half-backs ; Whelan, Johnson, Emery, Frster, Fraser, Peters, Handley, and Dorbyshire, forwards. Oldham H. Eagland, back; Bradbury, H. Varley, S. Lees, and P. Lawton, three- q•larters ;A. L.-es and J. Lawton, half-backs; E. iionsnr, T. Furniss, E. Hughes, J. Merrill, R. Edwards, R. Wylie, J. Lee and A. Childs,forwards. Barber started for Widnes, who were the first to gain any appreciable advantage,a scrimmage ensuing in the visitors' territory. Brennan missed a return, the opponents' forwards charging him, but nothing tangible resulted. S. Lees missed a good pass, but Widnes again came alonrr, and for a few minutes peened the visitors in their 25. Emery put in a good dribble, the ball Was returned, and at the other end it went into touch. Widnes were awarded a rise k,ck, but it was of no avail, and scrimmages were frequent in midfield. Ultimately Oldham became dongerous,hut Barber intercepted splendidly,and punted the leather away. The ball, however, continued to hover in close proximity to the Widnes goal, and on a couple of occasions Oldham pressed very severely and experienced bard luck in not scoring. Archer collared Bradbury when that player was almost on the line, and from a scrimmage Furniss, Hughes, and Edwards ex- ecuted a round of passing that came to nothing, Nevitt neutralizing the effort. Widnes essayed an attack,but a free kick was awarded to the visitors,who sprinted away. A fine pass by Lees to Br, dbury was badly taken by the latter player, but for this he shortly afterwards atoned, and as a result Golding's services were requisitioned. The locals were showing up well, and from a scrimmage Whelan and Barber carried play well into the Oldham 25, where another scrimmage was formed. From a pass by Drummond, Widnes were within an ace of scoring, Bradbury receiving the oval and threading his way among his opponents. He was, however, floored, and from a subsequent attack, the same player kicked dead. A succession of rushes ensued, at the end of which the home team initiated a series of unsuccessful attacks upon the visitors' citadel. As a diversion Lees rushed the leather to Golding, but the former was collared, and a scrimmaae was formed near the Widnes 25. The ball hovered around' the goal, but eventually' went into touch, end the vi histle sounded for half-time—Widnes nil, Oldham nil. On the resumption of hostilities Old- ham were the first to press, but this was only t( mporary. At this juncture Brennan received an unpleasant knock, and play was suspended for a brief interval. Varley got into dangerous proxi- mity to the Widnes goal, and in the struggle</p>
        <p>that ensued Fraser was badly hurt, his collar bone being contused. A few minutes afterwards A. Lees secured a try,which was beautifully negotiated by Rag- I, land, Oldham thus scoring the first goal. Roused by , this reverse the home team made a strenuous effort to equalise, and Drummond executed a piece of neat play, finishing up with a punt: but they experienced the - hardest luck. A brilliant run by the Oldham forwards looked dangerous, but Guiding collared in time,and Wid- nes visited the other end. Johnson and Emery elicited I loud cheers through a piece of tine pissing, and how it I was a try was not secured was remarkable. In mid-field Drummond intercepted a couple of opponents, and from a free kick Wipes transferred operations to the other end. Varley prevented further progress with a strong punt,and despite the fact that the locals were showing up well,the visitors were undoubtedly playing the better game. Bradbury tenured another try, but Eagland's kick was feeble in the extreme. A mid-field punt by 1 that player a minute later just missed its mark. It was I now evident that the home team were being played out, Fraser's absence being keenly felt. Golding returned a tall punt from &amp;Wand, to which Childs and Wyllie re- plied by some clever work, ending in Oldham registering anothertry, which was fructified by Ragland. The borne team were pow demoralised, and a few break-aways made by Widnes were of a very half-hearted description. The whistle sounded "time," with the result Oldham wo goals anl one try to Widnes nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>RUNCORN v. LEIGH.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>Thom teams opposed each other on Saturday. More than usual interest was entered in the event, seeing that bothclubs are running very close in the competition. The Bros: Faulkner were unsble to take their place in the Runcorn team. However, with such capital reserves, nothing much was feared, and W. Pendlebury filled the vacancy at half back very creditably, whilst W. Gayter, on the wing.showed great resource. The followingtwere the Runcorn team :—Jolley. full back ; T. Warder, G. Robinson, H. Myers, and W.Gayter,three-quarter backs ; S. Abrams and W.Pendlebury, half backs ; A. Farmer.J. Langley, A. Taylor, S. Walker, G. Moores, S. Johnson,F. Gayter, and R. Reece. forwards. There would Ise fully 4,000 spectators present when Sin:th set the ball in mo- tion. From the outset play opened very fast, and the ball was quickly taken from one end of the field to the other. Jolley, the Runcorn full back, was particularly prominent for his fine kicking, and both sets of forwards were very smart in the loose. Mollineux ini- tiated a fins . passing movement, which was taken part in by Anderton, Parker, and Wall- work, but very little ground was gained. The Leigh forwards carried a scrimmage, but lost pos- session of the ball, and Walker, Moores and Farener,with a fine passing run, reached the Leigh 25. Hera Smith was injured and had to retire, and although he came on the field again after half-time, and took up a position amongst the three-quarters, he was of very little use, and was compelled to leave the field. At the interval no impression had been made on the score sheet. The second half was fast and exciting, and directly after the re-start Runcorn made repeated attacks on the Leigh citadel, the fine passing of the forwards and packs being met by equally fine tackling. For fully ten minutes the pressure was maintained, and then Anderton effected relief with a neat kick to centre. Here some capital passing was shown by the Leigh backs. M'Carthy broke away and threw to Spillane, who transferred to Ander- ton, and he to Wall work, who on being challenged returned to Anderton, the latter's pass to one of the for- wards being missed. Leigh again attempted a passing movement, but Robinson intercepted, and the Runcorn backs smartly handled the ball forward and backward to one another, and the Leigh line was again endangered. M'Carthy relieved with a tall kick to centre, and then Welder secured, steadied himself, and dropped a beauti- ful goal. Except for a good run by Coop Leigh were now outplayed, and it was only by effective tickling ' that the Runcorn backs were kept out. The last few ' minutes were very exciting, and after a fine bout of passing W. Gayter got ever at the corner, but Myers failed to improve the point,and the referee then sounded time, leaving Runcorn winners of a well-contested game ' by one dropped goal and one try, or seven points, to nothing.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" v. STOCKPORT " A."—The bright day on Saturday brought about 2,000 spectators to wit- nose the match between the above teams, on the Wilders- pool enclosure. Half-an-hour after the advertised time —3.3 o—the teams were lined on the field as follows : Warrington : Full back, Evans; three-quarters, Carey, Le wless, " Tommy " Barnes and Gleave ; half backs, Peden and Price; forwards, Savage, Monks, Hassell, Hammon, Scholtze, Thorniley, Fligham and Boyle. Stockport: Full back, Murdock; three-quartere,Walker, Vernon, Johnson and Hasnett ; half backs, Roberts and Kemp ; forwards, Miller, Roberts, Shorrocks, Gerald°, Parnell, Nuttall, Casey and Levey. Warrington kicked off, with their backs to the pavilion, and immediately forced their way ta the visitore 25 flag where a few scrimmages and line outs took place. From one of these Thorniley secured and passed to Savage, who crossed the line with ease. The try re- mained unconverted and Stockport, freshening up, re- versed the position of the game and made a dangerous rush which Evans neatly checked. For a moment or two the visitors were in the ascendancy and being awarded a free kick they almost dropped a goal. Carey I was the next player te give relief which he lc eeriplieheti by kicking into touch. Off-side play by a Stockport threequarter gained a free kiek fer Warrington and carried the play again across the line. Even play en- sued and Carey securing made a brilliant run but was floored on the point of scoring. This put Warrington in possession of the game, Carey however making a mull which looked dangerous. Lawless equalised by carrying the ball down the field at top speed, and by dodging his opponent scored the second try. Barnes,with one of his old-time kicks, neatly converted. Carey. Lawless, and Barnes, showed up well after this, while a Stock. port forward was just " grassed" on the line Price and Schol'ze relieved and half-time arrived with the score reading, Warrington, 1-1-0 ; Stockport nil. Even play initiated play in the second half of the game, and Barnes' trickiness was prominent. A reverse soon came, however, for Vermin, Stockport, got posses- sion of the oval which he succssfully carried across the line, but the attempt to convert was a failure. Soon after, Lawless got away, and had traversed half the field before being obliged to kick into touch. From this point Warrington had the bet of it and another oppor- tunity presenting itself Lawl, is snapped up the ball and amidst loud cheers scored • another try. Barnes, from a difficult place, failed to coevert. Gleave next had hard lines as, having got p )eassion, he raced away to- wards the line with a clear field when unfortunately he slipped and the chance was bet. Another good run by Lawless and a pass to Carey who kicked a minor, closed the match,with Watrington winning a well-fought game by 1 goal 2 tries (11 points), to Stockport 1 try (3 points.) Rtnecome " A" v. Letent "A."—Played at Runcorn before a fair attendance. A. Clarke started on behalf of Runcorn, the ball being returned to centre, a scrimm- age ensuing. J Lightfoot soon after got away and kicked to Ratcliffe who was tackled before he could re- ply. By short rushes Leigh got well to the other end, where Runeorn was awarded a free kick, which was taken he Butterworth. Hatton returned by a short kick into touch. Runcorn were awarded another free kick, Butterworth tried for goal.but the hall went outraide and Ratcliffe touched down. Immediately after the restart Butterworth made his mark and had another attempt at goal,the ball again going wide and Hatton touched down. Leigh went away with a rush end Roberts kicked into touch near the Runcorn line. Clarke soon afterwards made his mark, but kicked into touch, gaining very little ground. Good dribbling by Shaw took matters to the Leigh end, where Cottam made his mark and sent into touch. The visitors again rushed away, and looked , like scoring., hut were stopped ten yards from the Run- corn line. Half time arrived with no score. After the interval Roberts kicked off and play was soon in mid- field. Exchanges of kicks between Hatton and Butter- worth ended in the latter having the advantage. The homestere now got away and took play to the Leigh line. From a kirk by Lightfoot. Brown got possession. but lost the hall before he could return. From passing be- tween Brereten and Lightfoot,the homesters should have scored, but failed at the critical moment. Leigh now reversed matters, and were quickly in the Runcorn half, a.•.d kicked over the Runcorn line, where Hall ontraced Butterwor'h and scored. Roberts made a poor attempt at goal, the hell going up the field. The homeetere now had the best of matters, and Liehtfnnt from a free kick made a good attempt at goal, the ball going short. A series of scrimmages took place under the Leigh posts, but the viaitors eventually found relief. Runcorn were awarded a free kick and Lightfoot landed a goaewhieh equalised the snore. Play now became fast and exciting, the visitors making desperate efforts to score, but Runcorn got away to the centre, where the whistle blew, the score being—Runcorn, one penalty goal (three points) ; Leigh. one try (three points).</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RICRRATION V. RIRIE/471112AD WAwDElllk. "k" Ti*.—Thee teams met at Runcorn on Saturday. The visitors' captain won the toss and elected to play down hill, Whitney kicking off. At the onset the game wss very evenly waged,hoth sides playing a good open game, and the backa had plenty of work. The Wanderers' for- wards being the heavier had the advantage in the scrim- mage, but the home hacks were very safe, and up to the interval nothing had been scored. On restarting, the Birkenhead men increased their pace.and for a short time penned the Recs.in their twonty-five,Arden rushing over for the viaitnrs in a very good position. The kick at gra!, however,was a miserable attempt:And the visitors, had rn be satisfied with a three points lead. The Earle was con- tinued in a spirited manner the home tacks nutting in some splendid passing. On one occasim Sutton got away nicely and passed out to Rearne.who was brought down en the line. The Rocs were now having the beat of the game. and Cook and Ellnpley were nearly in, but Ing- harn,the Cheshire County Reserve back,proved a barrier to the hnmn men's efforts, and relieved time after time. The Runcorn men again took up the attack,and Summer- field getting the ball from a scrimmage made a dash for the line and got over near the corner. Whitney took the place kick, and only missed the mark by about a foot. The game ended in an even'draw. Final score—Runcorn Recreation, one try and one minor; Birkenhead Wan- derers, one try one minor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>TIIEBROOK V.ST. ELPHlN'S.—Et.Elphin'e were unfortu- nately forced to travel to Tuebrook on Saturday, with a very weak team, Robson, Tickell,and Thomson being too ill to play. However, substitutes were found, and a rattling good game was witnessed,neither s:de being able to claim the verdict when the whistle went, the score th eing three each. Tuebrook only scored the equalising goal on the call f time. The Saints altogether, after making al. lowances,played splendidly. Hatton, J. Duck worthond Dugdale scored the visitors' goals,the latter with a beau- tiful shot, and P. Colley and J. Turner were perhaps the best of a capable set of backs. The "Saints" supporters were well pleased with the display of their pets, their combination being very fine. Sr. MARY'S RESERVE V. REICII-STRRET MISSION.—On the ground of the former. The visitors started the game and broke away, but were driven back by Burke. Not to be denied, the Mission forwards worked the ball back, and Hewitt shooting high up, Meakin followed up and rushed O'Brien through with the ball in his possession. The ball was again started, and the homesters' right wing broke away, and Meakin scored. Half-time— Mission, two goals ; St. Mary's, one. Burke resumed, and Howard, getting possession, shot through out of O'Brien's reach. Meakin and Baines added goals. Mal—Bank-street Mission, five goals ; St. Mary's, one oat, GRAPPENHALL V. WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S. On Saturday Sc. Mary's journeyed .to Grappen- hall to play off their League match with the club of that name. It was four o'clock when Grappenhall started the game, and for a time the play was fairly even until the Saints' forwards got pos- session and by good combination was very near scoring on several occasions, the slippery state of the ground spoiling several good chances. Eventually the superior tactics of the Saints were not to be denied, the first goal being scored after twenty minutes play. Rigby scoring again before half time, when the score read—St. Mary's, 2; Grappenhall, 0. The second half was a repetition of the first, St. Mary's having all the play, the visitors' full backs being seldom called upon, the ball being kept In the Grappenhall ground during the greater part of this half, the Saints putting on four more goals, a pleasant though rather one-sided game ending—St. Mary's, 6; Ctrappenhall, 0. OIJOHTEINGTON PARK V. PATRICROFT.—PIaved at Patricroft. The home learn were quite Irresistible, and in the first thirty minutes scon-d five goals, the visitors seldom being dangerous. In the second half play ruled very even, each goal being taxed in turn, but neither side could score, and time was called with the score— Patricroft five goals, Ouglatrington lark none. Sr. PAUL'S RESERVE T. BE VISRY RESERVE.— This match was played at Bewsey on Satur- day. Grant kicked off at 330. A free kick against the Saints was sent in frost of Seearer, and headed through by Rigby, one of St. Paul's full backs. The sides were evenly matched, and some good play was shown by Lave', who scored, Longshaw snor- ing number two. Half-time score, St. Paul's four, Bew- sey one. Bewsey now played well; and Harborne sent in a good shot, which Shearer had no chance with, and then Grant kicked the ball out of the goal-keeper's hand, making the score three for Bewsey, The score at time read St. Paul's five, Bewsey three. HOLY TRINITY V. FRODSHAM IST.—The above teI.MS tried conc:usions with each other on the ground of the former on Saturday. The Trinitarians started, and at once made tracks for the visitors' goal, Miller shooting over the bar. The Frodsham men gradually worked the ball into the home quarters, but the homesters cleared in fine style. Smart passing by the Trinity backs resulted in Miller having another shie, which was again too high. The ball was taken to the ocher end by the accurate kicking of the visitors, who experienced hard lines in front of goal, Taylor relieving his side in the nick of time. Half-time arrived with the score sheet blank. The second half was very evenly contested. Bpth goals were visited in tura, but nothing substantial could be notched, and a goad game ended in nothing being scored.</p>
        <p>STOCITO4 EIRATH v. FARNWORTEL—PIayecI at Stockton Heath. The visitors winning the toss, Hallows started play for the Heathens. Both goals were in turn attacked, and then Farnworth with ;the aid of a free kick got dangerous. Probert relieved the pressure,and the visit- ing custodian was called upoa to save. For a brief period the homesters pressed, but they failed to penetrate the defence. The Farnworth Quintette getting possession raccd away, bat their shooting was bad. Fast play en- sued. Johnson eventually put the bill through for the liomesters. The visitors played up hard, buc. failed to score, half time arriving with the Heathens leading by one goal to nil. The second half opened in favour of Stockton Heath, the ball on one occasion being past the goalkeeper. An appeal for hands in goal against Stock- ton Heath was answered in the affirmative, bat the penalty kick was safely cleared by the home custodian. Give and take play ensued, and then Farnworth broke away and s, )r,kd, an appeal for the ball being over the goal line net h iug upheld. Etch side in turn strived to gain the lead but nothing else was scored, a good game ending in a draw of one goal each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE</p>
        <p>Rtnoconw Assoctenow v. WIDNES PARISH. —Runcorn journeyed to Waines on Saturday, to play their return match with the Nrish team, under the auspices of the rington and Ihstrict Amateur League. The teams q.-re as follows :—Rancorn : Goal. Hough ; full backs, Carter and Green; half backs, Pye, Paton and Bloor ; forwards, C. Burrows, Walker, Bailie, Jones, and Pea- cock. Widnes : Goal, O'Bietne ; full backs, White and Clibbon ; half backs, Maddison, Risley and Ramsay ; forwards, Nain,Mellonship, Stewart, Linceley, T. Davies. Bailie started, aed the play was soon rather too close to O'Bierne for the homesters to be comfortable, and before many minutes Bailie secured and shot right across goal. the ball striking the upright and through the goal, the custodian having no chance. Stewart kicked off, but the home forwards failed to get away. Paton secured and sent ahead, where Walker obtained hold and made straight for goal. O'Bierne rushed out and cleared, at the same time kicking Ramsay in the face. The Blues were soon again up field, and the ball travelled across goal several times. Burrows finally taking charge of the affair and placing another goal to the Blues' credit with a neat shot. Stewart sent amss the fold, and Green and Carter for once misjudged the size of the sphere,and gave Nain a grand chance—result a good goal. This put a better appearance on the game from the homesters point of view, and they played up supremely, Stewart scoring from a tree kick. Half-time arrived with the game in favour of Runcorn by three goals to two. The game finally ended in favour of Runcorn by four goals to two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>TEIE NORTHERN RUGBY UNION.</head>
        <p>TEIE NORTHERN RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Emergency Committee of the Northern Rugby Union was held on Monday evening in Manches- ter. Mr. H. H. Waller (Brighouse Rangers) presided, ani those present included Messrs. J. Nichol (Halifax), A. Fattorini (Manninghami, J. Clifford (HuddersfiAld), J. H. Smith (Widnes). J E. Warren (Warrington), E. Chesty (troughton Rangers), M Mills (Stockport), and J. Platt (Oldham), honorary secretary. The busi- ness included the consideration of the dispute between the Leigh and Widnes clubs, arising out of their match played on the 15% inst. The duly appointed referee failed to attend the match, and one of the touch judges, Mr. Hope,of Tyldesley, acted in his place. This gentle- man allowed a try to Widnes which Leigh alleged to he entirely unfair, and deman ied that the contest should be declared void. On the other hand Widnes, who wen the match, contended that they asked the officials of the Leigh club to consent to the fixture being drawn prior to the game being commenced, and were met with a re- fusal. They therefore held that the result should count in the table in their favour. The losers claimed that the match should be declared void on the ground of the general incomoeteucy of the leferee. The latter in turn reported the Leigh spectators for unruly conduct. After a lengthy deliberation the Committee reprimanded the Leigh club with regard to the latter complaint, and re- quested the officials of that organisation to nut warning notices about the ground. Evidence with regard to the circumstances under which the match was played was heard, and the Committee decided that the match should stand. Leigh consequently forfeit their cLposit of £5.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>ACC/DENT AT BANE QUAY STATION. _ _</head>
        <p>ACC/DENT AT BANE QUAY STATION. _ _</p>
        <p>At 9.15 on Tuesday rnornitig some empty pas- senger carriages and goods waggons at Bank Quay Station were in process of being shunted with a view to their removal from the main London line on to the east siding, facing Parker-street. Owing to some apparent misunderstanding of the fog signals—a light engine steamed up and ran with such force into the rear of some of the empty pas- senger carriages as to telescope two of them and block the line. Immediately after the accident had happened a breakdown gang was summoned, and under the instructions of Mr. Starkey, the station- master, the main line was cleared of the debris with commendable despatch, the remnant of the second passenger coach being dragged on the aiding. No one was injured by the collision.</p>
