        <p>The Independent Labour party of the Manchester dis- trict held a demonstration on Sunday in the Free-trade Ball. Amon e the reeolotions admits,' was on eic 'rens- int,' the opinion that the time arrived for the /medal affirmation of that part of the programme which relates to the natlooali4ation of railways, minerals, and lands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>THE CAIRO•S'CRI4MT LECrIIRES FOR THE PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>MR. JOHN CROSFIELD ON JAPAN. The Cairo-street schoolroom was crowded on Monday evening when the second lecture of the series got up by the Rev. W. H. Drummond, entitled " China, Japan, and the Corea." was given by the Rev. S. A. Steinthal (chair- man of the Manchester Geographical Society). Mr. John Crosfield, J.P., occupied the chair. The CHLIFMAN said that Mr. Drummond had told him that he did not need an introduction and he dare say that he did not—most of them had seen him once in their lives before. (Hear, hear and laughter). But he had great pleasuse in introducing to them hie friend Mr. Steinthal of Manchester, who had come over to give them a lecture on " China, Japan, and the Corea." (Ap- planae.) As some of them knew, he (the Chairman) had been round the world with his wife and spent six delightful weeks in Japan. Egypt was else among the places they visited and he thought that for the magni- ficent historical ruins of Karnak, the tombs of the kings, ike., that country ranked first in the world. They could I net find anywhere, any ruins that were equal to the ruins of Karnek,which dated back 6,000 years and stood on the ground of the dawn of civilisation. Japan was creite the opposite in that respect as it was what they might call a modern country: It was remarkable in that the men, women and children formed the happiest race on the face of the earth. (Appleuee.) He would not have believed—and they would hardly believe him when he sail that every person in the country WAS contented. (App'ause.) They had a saying in Lancaenire that "Contentment was a continual feast," and they saw it in Japan. As he had said to his workmen the other day, the British were a great nation and they had a right to be proud of Old England which had done a great deal to civilise the world,but they were not "in it" with Japan. As a nation we were not near them. They were sober, industrious, happy, and contented ; they had no workhouses, no poor houses, and there was not a poor person to be seen; everybody seemed perfectly happy, contented, always smiling, and the speaker could not previously have believed that there was a nation in such a delightful condition as J ipan. (Applause.) Their great lesson to the British was in their sobriety and industry. He had at home some of , their screens and vases which he had shown Mr. R. ' Garnett that morning, and which perfectly asteniehed him. if they offered £lO,OOO for such work they could not get it done in Eegiaud—we could not do it. The Japs had an extraordinary knowledge—their knowledge of art sad the ways to imitate nature on a screen or panel, were almost incredible. The most beautiful things he ever saw in his life, he saw iu Japan. There, they had a moral law of their own. Every child was bound to maintain his parents. (Applause.) What e good example that was to some of them in this old country. Here they would find pretty often that sons objected to support their parents, but not so in Japan. (Applause.) And this law was made by themselves and not by any Government—(applause)—therefore they had no need of workhouses. Mr. Crosfield went on to say that the men who carried him in a chair for 20 ' minutes—and he was no light weight—(laugnter)—vrere perfectly satisfied with 2s. a day. It was extraordinary. (Hear, hear.) They now came to scenery. He had seen i the scenery of Europe pretty well in Switzerland. Italy, and the Pyrenees, and had also been in Algiers and other places, but he had never seen more beautiful scenery them they had got in Japan. He and his wit, went round Fujiyama—the sacred mountain—a moon tam n 12,000 ft. high and 120 miles round the base. They took a week to do it in. There were no hotels, and they had te sleep in a Buddhist temple the first night, and the priest disturbed them at 4 o'clock in the morning with saying his prayers. (Laughter.) In addition they had to carry their beds, pillows, and food, and other thins they required. They went where never an English lady had been seen before. (Applause). The people in the villages, every man, woman and child, came out to stare at his wife as if she had been a wild animal. (Laughter.) At the village they stayed in when Mrs. Crosfield was going to bed at 10 30 at nieht—there were no doors but sliding paper shutters—four girls pulled a shutter back to pet their heads thrtiugh to see bow she , got her clothes off. (Laughter.) The Japanese ladies required nethine except what English people would call a kind of dressing gown—(laughter)ant were not such heathens as to wear corsets—things they would never dream about. (Laughter and hear, bear.) On one occasion they went down a river, 540 miles in a fiat- bottomed boat, over beautiful water and rapide,at times going about 20 miles an hour, and throughout between tree covered mountains; and among the trees was the wild azalia, red, mauve and white. They might have been planted by man to get the effect. Anotte;ir place they went to wan Nikota most charming and interesting spot. It was the site of the finest wood temples there were in Japan. Those temples ware most gorgeous things, covered with gilt and must have cost a fortune to build. Inside a temple there was no altar and nothing to attract the eye but there the people came to say their prayers. At Niko there was the tomb of the Japanese god,Shinto, and to reach it the speaker and his wife had to mount up 200 stone steps. A most impres- sive and solemn spot it was, because the stone steps were moss-grown and surrounded by magnificent trees of 120 to: 130 feet in height, as straight as a ship's mast, while the dark green foliage at the top gave a solemn appearance to the scene that was impossible to describe. Mr. Crosfield concluded by saving that he hoped it his health permitted, in another two or theee years' time, to be able to revisit that charming country, (Applause.) Mr. STSINTHAL then gave his lecture, dealing ex- haustively with the laws, government, religion, manner- ism and customs of the people of China, Japan and the Corea. Councillor F.W.Meeks exhibited a large number of eeleured and photographic slides,which Mr. Steinthal explained, and which added largely to the attractiveness of the lecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>STOCKTON HEAT H.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEAT H.</p>
        <p>APPLETON PAUOCITIAL COMMITTEII.--A meetinglof this committee was held on Friday last week, at the London Bridge Inn, Stockton Heath, Mr. H. Eyes occupied the chair, and there were also present :—Messrs. Coppock, fledgecock, Davenport, Pearson, Ashton Hayes, J. Whitlow, Richardson, and Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. Farrington (inspector of nuisances), and Mr. Swinton (surveyor). A letter from Mr. Potts,clerk to the Cheshire County Council, was read with regard to the construc- tion of the proposed sewer to be constructed jointly by the Parochial Committee and the Corporation of Warring ton, hoping that the agreement would be completed and the work carried out as quickly as possible. It was re- ported t hat Mr. Pemberton bad commenced to erect cer- tain houses in Pitre-st., although the plans thereof had not been approved by the District Council and that he was intending to construct a drain from such houses under *he hark y‘rds, contrary to the express instruc- tions of the Inspector. It was resolved that the Clerk write to Mr. Pemberton and point out that in the event of his constructing such drain in such position, proceed- inns would be instituted against him. Mr. Davenp-mt gave notice that at the next meeting he would movo a resolution 30 to V.. duties appertaining to the otilice of Insp-ctor of Nuisances with the view to more att.( ntion bwing d.voled to the work.—The. minutes were a(lopted at •.,1 e meeting of the Runcorn Council on Tuesday.</p>
