      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>:ALARMING COLLISION AT PRESTON.</head>
        <p>:ALARMING COLLISION AT PRESTON.</p>
        <p>An alarming collision occurred at Preston railway station at three o'clock on Sunday tnerning. The driver of a goods train which was standing oa a loop line to allow the Scotch express from Carlisle to pass mistook a signal for the express for his Own signal to advance and put on steam. The express came up at the moment,and the engines crashed into each other at the ' junction of the loop. Both engines were greatly dams aged,au empty carriage next to the express engine was overturned,and the next carriage was telescoped by a sleeping car. The escape of all the passengers without injury and almost without shock must be regarded as marvellous, as some of them were buried in the debris of the wrecked carriages. rho driver of the goods train was severely bruised, but the others escaped uninjured. All the passengers proceeded to Loadon two hours later. The disabled express engine was, it may be stated, the " Hard wicke," which took such a prominent part in the recent railway race.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BANKRUPTCY COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Mr. Registrar Ridgway. BANKRUPTCY OF A LOCAL TRADRSMAN.—MT. Henry Blanchard Summers, lodging at 481, Knutsford-road; Latchford,and lately carrying on business as a boot and shoe dealer at Bridge Foot, as Summers Bros., and at 32, Winwick-street, as the " Cash Boot Company," appeared to undergo his public examination. Mr. A.Browne was present on behalf of the bankrupb, aud Mr. 13uckten on behalf of certain creditors. The bankrupt stated to the official receiver that he commenced business atout April, 1892, as a boot and shoe dealer, at Bridge Foot, War- rington, with a capital of £25, which o,s had saved. He took a branch shop at 32, Winwick street, Warrington, in September, 1894, and traded at the first-named shop as" Summers' Brothere," and at the last-named as" The Cash Boot Company." Prier te commencing business he I was assistant to his father, who was a boot and shoe dealer. B4nkrupt accounted for his deficiency of £Bl4 Gs., and £25 capital as follows :—Estimated loss on tradine, £284 184. ; estimated household . and personal expenses, £223 15s. ; estimated legalcosts and expenses, £2O; estimated extra interest on extended bills, £5 10s. ; and anticipated loss on ieallsation of stock aied fixtures, £125 3s. He alleged he had not kept any cash book, or ledger. He further stated he only took stock on the first January, 1893 ; but did not know what became of the stock book,and that he had not, while in business, made out any balance sheet.or statement ot assets and liabilities. He alleged he only first became aware of his insolvency about June last ; and that he had not contracted any-debts with such knowledge. The un- secured liabilities were : as to £777 17s. 11d.,for goods; as to £29 18s., for money lent; and as to £ll 17s. 9d., for clothing, printing, and advertising.—ln reply to Mr.A.G.Potter,the assistant official receiver,the bankrupt alleged as the causes of his insolvency, bad trade, want of capital, keen competition, and always being pressed for money. He was not able to buy in the best markets, and paid too dear for his goods. He was first aware of his insolvency in June when he could not meet a bill rendered by a Mr. Phillips. His liabilities were £Bl9, and his assets £205, or Sc. in the L. He had had great difficulty in meeting his bills since September 1894. He had thought that he could "pull round" by saking two shops at different parts of the to wn,but they did not pay as well as he expected. Two-tuirds of his 'liabilities had occurred since October 1894, and the only justification he could make was that he hoped that he could improve his business. He had only gained about 30s. per week since that date, from which he had to pay the rent of the shops and other expenses. Since Janu- ary 1893, he must have been living on his creditors, and was therefore practically insolvent since that date. The examination was then closed. ' BANKRUPTCY OF A TRELWALL :FARMER.—MT. Henry Cooke, Village Farm, Ttielwall, farmer, also appeared to undergo his public examination. Mr. A. Browne was present on behalf Of the bankrupt. The gross liabilities were stated to be £1024 19s. sd.,and the assets £147 Ns. The bankrupt alleged as the causes of failure, " low prices, and floods caused by the Ship Canal during con- struction." He stated that he had carried on business as a farmer at Thelwall, since 1874. He had not filed the prescribed deficiency account saying that his bank- ruptcy was principally caused by loss through the forced 'sale of his property by the Sheriff's officer, and also by interest on loans. He only first became aware of his insolveacy in July last, and he had not contracted any debts since. The unsecured liabilities were £899 Sc. 4d. for Money lent and interest, £ll4 Os. Li. for goods and work done, and £8 145., for wages, and £5 for law costs. The bankrupt's brother was scheduled as a credi- tor for £550 for money lent, and £2OO far interest and with regard to this claim, the bankrupt said that his brother held a bill of sale dated July 30, for £750 on fanning stock, produce, furniture; &amp;c., at Village farm, Thelvsall. ' This property had since been realised by the Sheriff's officer, who disputed the security.- The bill of sale, it appeared, was executed on July 30, and was given to secure the sum of £5OO which had been ad- vanced. as alleged by the bankrupt, about eight years I ago, and to secure the interest in arrear.—Mr. A. G. Potter having cross examined the bankrupt at some length, the examination was closed.</p>
        <p>A CYCLING TOUR IN THE LIKE DISTRICT. [By ONE OF THE TOURISTS.] The beautiful weather of September tempted three well-known members of the Warrington C.C. to the above rainy district for a cycling tour, this being the third time they had attempted to get to Keswick but had hitherto failed, after passing Ambleside, owing to rain. The run from Preston to Lancaster is very fine, and after doing justice to a good dinner at the King's Arms Hotel, Lancaster, we resumed our journey. After passing Carnforth we began to feel the fresh breezes from Morecambe Bay, and hedgerows give way to white stone walls and the houses being mostly built of the same material everything has a very clean appearance. Bootle-le-Sands we found full of trippers from Morecambe, who made the quiet country place quite lively. After passing through Silverdale we reached Arnside at which place we took the train over the viaduct to Grange-over-Sande. There we again mounted our machines, and after admiring the lovely view of the bay from the hill above Grange we made for the mountains, and the first night found us at the comfortable hotel at Newby Bridge. Next morning we were out at six o'clocit and launched a boat in the river opposite the hotel, and for two.hours had a lively time catching peich which abound in the river. After breakfast we continued our journey, making a tour round Greenod. We then proceeded through lovely noun- ! try and reached the foot of Coniston (Lake Bank) about dinner time. Coniston village was passed at 1 sunset, Coniston Old Man Mountain showing out with fine effect. We then followed a char-a-bane , which was leaving for Ambleside, and which place 1 we reached at dusk after a ride which we shall long I remember. Next morning we were out early fishing in Windermere at which place we found the boatmen very obliging as they found us with tackle of every description and a boat for 6d. per hour. Breakfast over we again resumed our jour- ney,and soon reached the pretty village of Gras- mere after which we mounted a very steep hill called Duavail Raise, from which we had a splen- did view of the village in the valley below. (I may here say that the roads are very narrow and ' steep in places, and the utmost care is necessary it being positively dangerous to ride down a number of hills). We at last got a view of Thirlmere (Man- chester Water Works) at the foot of which the mighty Helvellyn towers, the ever changing views completely bewildering the tourist. We here got on the new road made by the Manchester Corpora- tion at a cost of £30,000, the length of which is nearly six miles and I think the only level six miles of road in the lake district. The remains of the old road may be seen in the valley gradually being • covered by water. We at last arrived at Keswick, a quaint Cumberland town with an old market in the main street, and as it was Saturday afternoon we saw it in full swing. At night we went down to the lake (Derwentwater) which is without doubt the Queen ot the English lakes, and being moon- light we went out for a row my friend remarking that it looked different from Bridge-st. or Butter- market-st. On Sunday morning we turned our backs for home, the weather being splendid and the autumn tints making the country look grand, the people being busy gathering the hay which is al- ways very late in this district. Retracing our road through Thirlmere this time on the opposite side of the lake, we reached Bowness as the sun was fast disappearing and at this place we spent the night The next morning we mounted our " jig- gers "and bid goodbye to the lake district which we were very loath to leave, having got quite used to the quiet but beautiful country,not having ridden a single mile of barren road since leaving Lancas- ter. At this latter place I parted with my two companions,myself going north for a few more days and my friends making for Morecambe, rather sorry that they were leaving me behind in such a nice country.</p>
        <p>TRIED AND PROVED. Sufferers from tar;Nei, Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the Back Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaint. Dlseass of Kidneys Rimider Stone, Sciatica, Rheumatism and Gout will find a positive Cure in Holroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and if not satisfied your money will be returned. Prices Is. lid, of all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps, from HOLROYD'S ffedical Hall, Checkheateu, Yorks. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them write to ithe Proprietor and a boa will be sanknext post.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. K. Graham, of Bucharest,read a paper before the London Chamber of Commerce, on Monday on the financial and trading capabilities of Roumania as a market for English manufactures. He said that corner ot Europe was deserving of more attention here. In the course of the discussion the Chairman (Sir A. Rollit, M.P.) asserted that this country was very inadf quately represented abroad, generally by means of commercial attaches ; and Mr. Graham showed that the interests of German manufecturers in Roumania were represented by a large staff at the Bucharest Legation, while our Consul could not interfere in such matters.</p>
