      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>I.ettero to tin eilitor.</head>
        <p>I.ettero to tin eilitor.</p>
        <p>- - CHRISTIANITY ANALYSED. • "By their frifts ye shall know them." ir,—" And again," says my fair but anonymous criti 'you would have people live and die without religion Excuse me, Madam ;by no means so. On the contrary, I would have people more relis.o ma, even at the expense o same of the existing show piety. I hold it quite possible and consistent to expose the fabulous foundation —the impracticable and rationally untenable dogmas of Christianity—whilst advocating the following of the meek and lowly Jesus. For, my dear madam,l maintain that a great and fundamental distinction exists between Jesuism and Christianity or as I prefer to call it to avoid misundereanding—Christism.. The former is solely of the great Teacher and is the element of good in Chris- tianity, whilst Christism is mainly, if not wholly, an unwarranted human accretion. Christism and its peculiar dogmas of atonement, resurrection and redemp- tion, is the work of man's untutored imagination— founded upon certain quite imaginary cosmic and tradi- tional occurrences. And, as man created them, so he is now destroying them—by showing the mythical nature of their origin. But, while scientific investigation is rapidly sweeping man-made Christism into the limbo of detected errors, Jesuism emergesf rom the fire unscorched and unscathed. While Christism is shrinking daily before the witherinc, glance of science, Jesuism, and all that its lovers hold dear, all that tends to the making of charac- ter, still stands unabashed and unabased before men. Jesuism is for the individual, for oneself, for chastening and ameliorating character and conduct. Christism is a thing of the churches, of ecclesiastic pomp and empty ritual; while the h-ayy indictment lies. against it of being the great cause of the too common neglect of its more Amiable sister, the cause too of the bickering,s, the jeal- Oasies,the wars anitha rnartyrings of Christendom. Who ever heard of men fighting or burning their brother men In defence of the maxim 's Do as ye would be done, unto," or " Love thy neighbour as thyself ?" The time msy come when men will not thus culpably prefer the drosi to gold—the time when there will not be found a class of men earning a livelihood by striving for the recrudescence of that ugly ulcer, aboriginal ignor- ance and its concomitant evils, which makes religion and morality rest upon exploded myths and marvels, to the dishonour of God and the confusion of minkind. No, Madam, I would not "have people live and die without religion," but rather that they should adhere to and more and more constantly practice the scriptural injunc- tion, and entirely rational precept, "Prove all things; hold fast that which is good." And Christ-ism stands to-day convicted of the foul miseries, murders, and martyrdoms sl justly imputed to the heterogenious com- pound called Christianity. This damning " proof " is supplied alike by history, scientific critical investigation, and common human experience. Can we then be wrong in rejecting what is thus proved to be bad and holding 1 fast—aid all the faster, to that which is good? lam fully persuaded that the second coming of Jesus would be a sad event to the high fiaminicals who take his name in vain and lose the hest part of his teaching beneath the mazes of a barbaric ritual.—Yours truly, JOHN PEERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>HELSB Y WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>HELSB Y WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>-- At Runcornillural Council on Tuesday a letter was read front Messrs. Potts, Potts and Gardner, solicitors, Chester, respecting the Helsby Water Company, and expressing the hope that the District Council would grant the company permission to lay their pipes along the Alvanley-read without any further adjournment of the question. If, however, they should appoint a com- mittee to confer with the directors of the company as Mr. Ashton had intimated the Council might do, they would suggest that a committee of the Parish Council should also be invited to attend. With regard to the cottage property, the aolicitors pointed out that thei company were prepared to supply water to everyone who wished to take it from them, and on making cal- culations it would be seen that the price the company intended charging, namely is. 61. per 1,000 gallons, would enable cottagers to obtaia a sufficient quantity of water at a very reasonable cost. They did not sc the necessity of providing the District Council with a larger plan than the 25 inch ordnance map.— The Clerk read a letter he had sent the Water Company respecting the matter, in which he pointed out what might be t'-.e result from a sanitary point of view, and lotherwise if the permission asked for was given. It stated that from the circular issued by the company, so far as cottages were concerned the prices were pro- hibitive, inasmuch as they proposed to supply by meter and to charge is. Bd. per 1,000 gallons, which meant taking the average number of occupants of a cottage at 5, and reckoning the supply at 10 gallons per head per day, the result would be a total per annum of £1 15a. While by section 62 of the Public Health Act, 1875, the local authority could only require the owner of a cottage to provide a water simply when it could be furnished at a cost not exceeding 24. per week, or It such other cost as the Local Government Board might on the application of the local authority determine under all the circum- stances of the case, to be reasonable. There are 97 houses in the township which do not exceed the rate- able value of £7 per annum, and under this scheme the District Council would be in a position that they would have 99 cottages in this township wtihout water sup- ply for it might betaken for granted that no cottager would willingly pay £1 15s. a year for a water supply, and the District Council would have no power to compel him to take it at such a cost. The Rural District Council charged in the contributory places of Frodsbarn and Frodsham Lordship as follows : Houses of a rateable value not exceeding £7 per annum 2d. and 3d. per wesk respectively. The Runcorn Water Company (now in the Urban District Council of Run- corn) whose limits 0e supply extended to Weston and Halton cottages nf £7 rental did not pay more than 24. per week and of £lO rental not more than 3d. If the District Council granted the facilities asked for without restriction as to prices to be charged the result might be that they might have a water company established and the works so constructed as to supply all the better houses with wat,r while the cottages would be without a suppic and the District Council might be called upon by the Local Government Board to construct wurks to supply them which could only be done by purchasing the Company's works at peasibly an exhorbitant price, or by constructing new works, and in the latter case there might only he the revenue from the cottages to pay the expenses. He submitted before allowing the Flelsby and District Water Company to proceed further with their scheme that the Rural District Council should fully consider the matter in all its bearings,and he would sug- gest that a small committee he appointed with instruc- tions to consider first, the desirability of the District Council carrying out a scheme of a water supply, second, the terms uprn which the District Council would be pre- pared to aff rd facilities to the Helsby and District Water Company fe.r carrying such a scheme. A long discusaVn took place on the matter, taken part In by the Chairman, Messrs. Heywood, Aaron, J. Bran- dreth, Woodward, J. Darbvshire, A. Greenway, Baxter, and .Ines. A resolution was moved for the ap- pointment of a committee to consider the matter to which Mr. Derbyshire moved an amendment that the Company have permission to lay their pipes where they required.—Mr. Greenway seconded the amendment,and said if the water scheme. were carried out it would pro- vide a number of people at Alvanley with a supply of water. On being prit to the vote the amendment was lost. The following were selected to compose the com- mittee :—Measra. Brandreth,Heywood, Wllliams,Ellison, Greenway, Woodward. Hall, Wright,Baxter, Derbyshire. and Stokes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>TECH PAROCHIAL COMMITTER --Thp Rev, G. D. Wray presided at the meeting of this Committee on Saturday. The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Board with rtfPrerre tri the pollution of Halton Brook. when, on the motion of Mr. Jeacock, seconded by Mr. Looker, it was rewlved that the Clerk be instructed to write to the Local einvernment Board to point out that the Committee were aasitred and felt very confident that the scheme sugoratril 13,-• the Wdical Officer of Health would prove sufficient, and that the Local Government would be of the .1 ATP opinion ;f they were to send an officer to report 'anon it.—Mr. Wright stated that he was not at the meeting because h., NORA not aware that it was being held. He did not reeeilis the usual notice.—The Clerk said that his assistant who attended to this work had told him that the notice was posted.—Mr. Wright moved, Mr. Stokes seconded the confirmation of the minutes, and they were paaseri.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PENIZETTI.</head>
        <p>PENIZETTI.</p>
        <p>FERRETTI AND SWEEP COTTAGE GARDEN SOCIETY. A meeting of the e mmittee of 'ht. abovo .ocietv wad- held in the P..rketh El mentary Schools, Mr. Robert Garnett, J.P., in the chair. The balance sheet wag pre dented by Mr. John Jarkaon, treasurer, and Ft bowed that the income from ell .ources, i^cluding a balance from the previous year of £1 155.. amounted to £l3 18g. 4d. The eznenditure side ahowed that £8 18g. Bd. had been spent, the lArgegt item being for prize money. balance of £4 19g. 81. wag lrft in the treasurer's hands. The Chairman congranlated the treasurer and secretary on the able manner in which they had conducted the financial portion of the socioty's business.</p>
        <p>After three years anent in negotiating with the South- port"Tramways Comnany for the purchase of the liner, the Southport Town Council determined on Tuesday night to ask the Ro,rd ,f Trade to fix a price at which the Corporation shalt takeover the rails. The Turkish newspapers at Constantinople publish the Imperial decree sanctioning the reforms in Armenia The inaLeetinn nr prisons. the formation of a mixed body of ool;re and gendarmerie in each vilayet, the pro- tection of the Artneni4ne atraintd the Kurds, the settle- rnent of the nnmati." on lands provided by the Gevernment.theentaMishment of a board of supervisien, the atvlition f fnteed Nhour,andlreforms in the wiles- lON of the taxes are assosaced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
