      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>EARLESTOWN IND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN IND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEWTON-IN-MAKERFt ELD ST. PETER'S CENTRAL</p>
        <p>DISTRICT NATIONAL 6CILOOL. At a special meeting of the Committee of Man- agement of this school, held on Wednesday, Mr. Robert Stone, J.P., of Newton Park, was appointed a manager in the place of the late Mr. McCorquo- dale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>ACCIDENT TO A CHILD AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A CHILD AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning a boy five years ot age son of Mr. George Bailey, cf Earle-st., Barlestown, was standing on a buffet looking through the kitchen window watching the snow, when the buffet canted over, and the boy fell to the ground, break- ing one of his legs. Dr. Mouncey was called in and set the injured limb.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>MERRIONS.</head>
        <p>THE MERRIONS.</p>
        <p>We would draw attention to the advertisement in another column, announcing the coming of the Merrions, who will on Saturday and M mday even- ing next give their most amusing entertainment. The lierrions are not by any means strangers at Earlestown, and there is no doubt a very large com- pany will assemble at the Town Hall, on the even- ings named to enjoy the good things to be pro- vided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ME. W. H. JUDE AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>ME. W. H. JUDE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the Town Hall at Earles- town was well filled to hear a musical recital by Mr. W. H. Jude of Liverpool, entitled "The music of humanity." The programme was rich, and being cleverly executed was thoroughly appreciated. it included national, fireside, and modern pianoforte, music, Christianity and music, rustic scenery in Brittany, Shakespeare and man's ingratitude, Anton Rubinstein, and the child's birthday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>FIRE AT EA.RLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT EA.RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday nig-hi--ab—o-d-t-Ten-O;ClOCk an assistant at the grocery establishment of Mr. James Ince, Market-st., was lowering the shop window blind, when it came in contact with the gas, and in a moment was ablaze. The flames shot up,and there was danger of the premises being set on fire. The curtain was torn down, and carried into the street, and the flames stamped out. There was a good number of people about,and the affair caused some excitement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>DRUNKENNESS.</head>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newton, on Monday morning, before Colonel Wood. John Brady was charged with being drunk in Earlestown on Saturday night. Thera was also a previous charge of drunkenness on December Bth, 1894, but be neglected to answer to the summons.—lie was now fined 5s and costs for both cases,and in default to be sent to gaol for seven days in each case.—lt the Earlestown police court on Tuesday morning before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, Martin Calister, of no fixed residence, was charged with being drunk in Righ-st., Newton, on Monday afternoon. He was fined 2s. 6d. and costs. EARLESTOWN LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>A meetin—g7f-ltiberals was held in the Central Committee rooms, Cross-lane, Earlestown, on Wed- nesday evening to further consider the project of opening a Liberal club. Some discussion tooic place and it was ultimately decided to run a club as a Company with a capital of 1,000 shares of 101. each. It is, however, intended to issue at present only 300 shares, and it is anticipated that these will be rapidly taken up. Mr. W. R. Renshaw, the registration agent, has been appointed secretary and Mr. Joseph Emmett, treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>EARLBSTOWN WESLRY GUILD.</head>
        <p>EARLBSTOWN WESLRY GUILD.</p>
        <p>At the weekly meeting of the above guild held on Thursday evening', presided over by Mr. J. IL Profit, a paper on " Selt-reliance," was read by Mi se Littler, of Runcorn. There was a large attendance of members, and the paper which was ably and gracefully written,was much enjov ed by thoze pre- sent. An animated discussion followed, in whic', the Chairman,Messrs.Millington, T.Wheatley,Speak- man, Mrs. Burgess, Misses Marrow, Alderson, and Newman took part. The essayist replied to the various points raised in a very able manner.</p>
        <p>1.0. G, T. (EBENEZER LODGE.) The usual weekly session of the Karleston gbe- nezer Lodge was held in the Cc-operative Hall on Tuesday evening, The session was called to order at 8 o'clock and was presided over by Bro. W. Hatton c.T.. Bro. W. J. Taylor, LB., then read the digest of S. W. Lancashire DistriCt Lodge, after which the executive officers of No. 1 Convention, S.W. Lancashire, were responsible for the evening's programme. Bro. Quarter-Master-Sergeant Roberts presided. and gave a very interesting address. Bro. John Singleton gave a song. and Bro. Francis, of St. Helens, also gave an address,after which a song was given by Bro. W. Brown. Sister Mrs. Witty also sang a solo ; Bro. J. Taylor gave a Lancashire read- ing. A recitation was given by Bro. Drew. Thanks were afterwards tendered to the executive officers for their programme, the lodge closed in the usual way, the members having spent a very pleasant evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>GOOD TIMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TIMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive flail, on Monday evening. Bro. J. Hatton pre- sided, and there was a very large attendance of members an-1 visitors. One candidate was restored to membership and several others proposed. Bro. E. Newman, L.D., read circulars from the G.C.T. and District Secretary. Bro. Barker gave a very satis• factory report of the recent lodge anniversary, showing a fair balance in hand. The officers for next quarter were elected,and will be installed next week. The programme of the evening was "Short readings," Bro. James Hopkins being responsible. An excellent selection of appropriate items was sub- mitted and read by Bros. G. S. Warburton, W. H. Dale, 0. Clarke, J. Cutler, B. Barker, W. Reynolds,J Hatton, S. Parker, E. Newman, and Sister H. Cor- dingley. Thanks to Bro. Hopkins closed a very en- joyable meeting. NEWTON DIVISION CONSERVATIVE ASSOCIATION. _ . .</p>
        <p>The quarte.ly meeting of the Council of this Association was held at Newton on Saturday after- noon, when there was a large and representative attendance of delegates from various parts of the division. Mr. T. D. Grimke-Drayton presided, and amongst those present were the Hon. T. W. M.P, Messrs. T. J. Gillespie, W. Valiant, James Smith, Robert Stone, W. Holland Owen, Alderman T. Sutton, and others. The Chairman congratulated the meeting upon the substantial gain at the recent revision.—Mr Valiant moved a resolution which was seconded by the chairman and unanimou.ly passed, congratulating the Hon. T. W. Legh, M.P., upon the very great honour conferred, not only on himself, but upon the Newton Division of Lanca- shire generally, by his being selected to move the Address to the Crown in the House of Commons. The on. T. W. Legh in responding briefly touched upon the result of tile revision, and also took an opportunity of again thanking those who worked so hard for the Unionist cause at the election in July last. Referring to the very kind remarks that had just been made, and the resolution they had so kindly paused upon his being selected to move the Address in the House of Commons, he did not think the honour conferred was owing to any merits of his own, but as an acknowledgement by Her Majesty's Government, of the splendid work done for the Unionist cause, not only in the Newton con- stituency but throughout Lancashire.</p>
        <p>FARM FIRE AT WINW/CE.-ACCIDENT TO THR "MAJOR."</p>
        <p>On Saturday night a fire brcke out on Mr. Kul- shaw's Highfield Farm, Virinwick, by which all the farm buildings, implements,produce,and two stacks of corn were totally destroyed. A full complement, of firemen, under the direction of Superintendent Turner. on receiving the summons, promptly turned out with the steam fire engine Major. When some distance from a private road to the farmand about 150 yards from the fire, one of the wheels of the engine ran on to the footway, the engine being overturned. Some of the firemen were thrown vio- lently to the ground, whilst others jumped, but,for- tunately,all fell clear of the engine. Superintendent ' Turner sprained his wrists, and fireman Tickle was . also injured The engine, which had full steam up, , was soon got into its right position, when it was found that it had sustained little damage. When , the scene of the fire was reached it was found that water could not be procured nearer than from A pit 500 yards distant. The brigade had only 300 yards of hosepipe with them,and the engine conse- quently could not be got to work until additional bose had been brought from Warrington a distance of about three miles. By this time the fire was rag- ing furiously and had got a firm hold. The attention of the firemen was then turned to saving an adjoin- ing house, in which task they were successful. Obstructions were not yet, at an end, for after about I four hours' hard work the tubes in the boiler of the engine burst, rendering it useless. The flames,how- ever, were at this time practically subduettbi4t were not finally extinguished until about nine o'cUk on Sunday morning, the brigade having had a trying time of it, rain and hail falling heavily all through the night. The cause of the fire is unknown, and the damage, which is not yet estimated, but must be considerable, is covered by insurance.</p>
        <p>VULCAN WESLEYAN CHAPEL. On Sunday special services in connection with the anniversary of the above place of worship were held in the chapel on Sun- day. ihere were good congregations and sermons were preached morning and evening by the Rev. J. T. Waddy, or Prescot. :Special hymns were sung by the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 2, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 2, 1895.</p>
        <p>ANOTHgIi SI7NAWAT HOUR AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening shortly after seven o'clock I considerable commotion was caused in Market-st., by a runaway horse. A horse and lurry belonging to Mr. Stone, Newton Park was standing in tidy- dock-street, in front of the greengrocery shop of Mr. Roberts, when it suddenly bolted, having pre- sumably been startled by the music from several shows on the market grouno. It set off at full speed along Oxford-street and down back Market- street, and in turning the corner into the opening leading into Market-Areet the lurry came into con- tact with the opposite wall, with the result that the shafts were torn away from the lurry. The horse continued on its course, with the shafts and dashed across Market-street through Mr. Forster' yard, emerging into the back street through the smpll gateway, sma,hing the door. It raced along the back street, and finally came to a stand in Wellington-street. Fortunately no further damage was done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EA.RLESTOWN. (BY AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR).</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EA.RLESTOWN. (BY AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR).</p>
        <p>The Town Hall clock has been somewhat erratic lately, so far as the lighting up arrangement is con- cerned. During la:t week, as soon as it became dusk, the 'only guide yon could obtain as to the time from the public timekeeper, was the tolling of the bells at the quarter hours and hours. Whether it is objecting to the extravagance of the council- , lors in reducing the price of gas, I cannot say, but certain it is that it has been in darkness for several nights lately. I noticed a slight glimmer on Wed- nesday night, but judging from the way ;.t was bobbing up and down, I should fancy it was hold- ing high revel, but on the stroke of nine, it sud- denly vanished and again all was darkness. Now what is the use of a public clock if you cannot see the time after dark. But even whea it is illumin- ated, it is really only an apology for a light, which I think the quality of the gas has something to do with. Apart from this however lam of opinion that the c:rick should be regularly illuminated, and r, is the duty of the councillors in whose ward the row a Hall is situated to see to it.</p>
        <p>At last it has been decided to start a Liberal Club. It will be started by a Company with a subscribed capital only a portion of which how- ever is to be called up at present. The shares are to be 10s. each, spread over four monthly payments, that is, 2s. 6d. on application, 2.5. 6d. allotment, and the remainder in two calls. I think this is about the best arrangement that could be arrived at, for if the working-man takes up shares, he is sure to see that his money is not thrown away, and if the working-men of Golborue can make a club pay on these lines, it can surely be done at Earlestown. I believe encouraging support has been promised,and ere long I hope to see a flourishing Liberal Club and established on a sound financial basis.</p>
        <p>I see the Conservatives had a day out on Satur- day, and they brought the member for the division down to Newton to congratulate him upon the honour conferred on him by allowing him to move the address to the Crown in the House of Commons at the re-assembling of Parliament. They also revealed to him that they had Rained at the last revision of voters, and shook hands with each other with great gratification over the fact. John Bull is always represented as being as a gullible nature, but I never thought that a certain section were so easily gulled over a statement in regard to a revi- sion. It is all very well to put down a few figures and subtra:t one from another and record the result as a gain, but there are such things as real and actual gains, and mere fictitious gains. I am always sceptical about such matters, and shall remain so until the end of the chapter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>BUSINESS OF TUE NEXT MEETING.</p>
        <p>A monthly meeting of this Council will be held at the Town Hall. Earlestown. an Monday next, when tha fol- lowing minui-ea of committees will tss conAidered. THE HIGHWAY% &amp;C., COMMITTEE AND THE MEDICAL ostucza's REPORT.</p>
        <p>This Committee's minutes contain the following mat- ter. :</p>
        <p>, The Surveyor having reported the condition of the Bridges over Banker Brook below the Common Lock, it was resolved that Messrs. Collingwood. 13nrron, and W. Thompson, jun., he a sub•committee,.. inquire into the resp-maihilitv for their restoration and maintenance of the raid over them, _ _</p>
        <p>The report of the 'Medical Officer of Health, for the quarter ended September, was read to the committee. It grated that there had been registered—births, males 66, females 55; deaths, males 27, frmales 20. The birtha were at an annual rate of 34.6 and the deaths 13 6 per thousand of the estimated population, but the deduction of the deaths of two strangers brought the death-rate down to 12 8 per thousand. The infantile death-rate in the quarter was 4 3 per cent. The zrmotic sick-rate of notified cases WaS just Over one per 1,000; there was only one death (puerperal fever) amongst these cases. The zymatic death-rate, however, was 1.2 per I,ooo—there having been 17 other deaths (whooping cough 4, diarrhre: £3)—infectious diseases which are not notifi- able. In six of the 12 fatal cases of diarrhcet in children under five years of age " dentition " WRS certified as the Primary cause of death. The fourteen eases infectious disease were thus distributed between the wards : Town Hall ward. 3 ; Viaduct Ward 9 ; Crow-lane ward 1 • Newton ward 1 ; Wargrave ward 0. 'Mr. Gregory, the deputy inspector, appends to his report a list of persons on whom he had served notices to abate nui.anc, s and not yet complied with, and he sutfgeStl that if these nuisances are to be abated At/sager measures than he can use will have to be brought to bear on them, lie was very pleased to be able to inform them that with one little exception the whole of the district was free from all cases of infectious disease. His local tolls for the last month were £3 13s. 4d. ; market £17125. 61.; total £2l ss. 101. This month which embraces five weeks i., local tolls, £5 12s. 7d. ; market, £2O 19s. sd. ; total, £26 12s. Od. During the last month they removed from the district 156 loads of pails, 102 loads from ashpits and 181 loads of dry rubbish, total 439 loads, This month they had removed 184 loads of nails, 133 load.; from aslapits and 226 leads of dry rub- bish, total 543 loads. Several pig keepers in Legh-street having failed to comply with the notices for the removal of thei, pigs, it was resolved that the Medical Officer of Flealth and Mr. Brierlry inspect the same, previous to taking legal pr-iceedings against them. Mr. Gregory's attention was specially directed to the inspection of the slaughter housAi and bake houses in the district.</p>
        <p>Plan. for new washhonces to Mr, Thomas Gregory's hottscs in S. ffelons-road, and an amPnded plan of six hnuaes in Wargrave New-road, for Mr. A. L. Collinge, were examined and passed : Mr, Collinze to be called upon to complete the adjoining premises to the previous approved plans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>OAR AND WATER commtrrp.s.</head>
        <p>THE OAR AND WATER commtrrp.s.</p>
        <p>The Gag an—d-Viater.Commtttee have passed the follow- resolutions That as mains and lamps be extended over Newton Common from the Ladces to the man in Wharf-road near Common 1.,,,ek alcng Park-road from Wargrave to the junction with Willow-road ; along Southworth-road from Willow Bank to the township boundary at Lowton junction. The carhon,zing account for the past month was read, giving fuel used 257 tons 12 cwt., gas made 2,650,100 foot, being an averagr of 10,144 per ton of fuel used. That tender.fc r tar were acain considered, and it was resolved that the amended aim. of Messrs. R. S. Clare and GI., Liverpool, at 21g. 64. per ton on rail, be ac- cepted for the current year. Mr. Swami was instructed to keep up the quality of the gas to 20 candle..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.</p>
        <p>The Schonl Attendance Committee's minutes contain the fonnann,-; cplarterly statement, ending September, of attendances :</p>
        <p>_ ...._____. ... . Corresponding NflMe Or School. No on Average Pr: Qqarter Books. Attenaance. Cent. Last year. St. Peters D.. Infarts l4O 103 Gas street Wargrave Pb. Infants Parlestown District... 380 --</p>
        <p>........,......... M. Infanta... 310 Wesleyan Do. Infanta Roman Catholic _ _</p>
        <p>Do. Infants Vulcan Infants Totals 895. 2A32 _.. ,_ . Sept..' ---- ____ 2265</p>
        <p>Totals Sept., 1894. 2556 TECHNICAL INSTReCTION The Technical Instruction Commite report that a letter dated 15th October, was read from Mr. Bennion, County Director 0 Technical Instruction stating his committee has derided not to recommend any additional grant beyond the amount already allotted to the various districts for the current session. It was resolved that the letter be azknowledee, stating that the committee are considering their position, and will communicate further.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN, NEWTON, HAXDOCK, &amp;c., BUILDING SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN, NEWTON, HAXDOCK, &amp;c., BUILDING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The thirty-second annual general meeting of this society was held in the district schoolroom, Earles- town, on Thursday evening. Mr. F. Harwood pre- sided, and there was a lair attendance of members. The report and balance sheet which was as under was adopted.—° The directors have again pleasure in submitting to the shareholders a satisfactory report and statement of accounts for the past year,certi- fied by the auditors. The profits for tne year amount to £1,705 12s. 11d., which with the undivided .balance troin last year of £2,342 19s. 51. shows a disposal amount of £4,048 12s. 4d., from which the directors re- commend the payment of a dividend at the rate of 5 per cent, per annum, absorbing £1,622 7s. 11d., leaving a balanceof undivided profit of £2,426 48. 5d., which is increased over last year by £B3 53. Ten members have redeemed their property, representing repayments of capital p.mounting to £3,148 10s. 8.1. Two lots of pro- perty only on hand. The number of members on the books is 402, holding 3,258 i shares." The Chairman remarked that they had done a steady and safe business. They would aoserve that they had advanced during the year £4,954 oa what the directors were satisfied was go)d security, as against £9,656 in the previous year. It nad been a great pleasure to the directors to be in a position to de- clare a dividend t 5 per cent, which he might say had been fairly and honestly eareed. and carried to the re - serve fund £B3 55. They were aware that last year they had to take from the reserve fund £6 6s. to enable them to pay 5 per cent., and they now stood in the posit-on of having refunded to six guineas and added to reserve fund £76 19s. The reverse fund now stood at £2,428. He might say that since ths report was printed, they had disposed of one lot of property, so that they had only one lot lett on their hands and the reserve on that one account was over 400 per cant. (Hear, hear.) The whole of the retiring officers were then re-elected. Mr. Fowler (solicitor) apologised for the absence of Mr. H. Davies. It was a good many years since Mr. Davies had missed a meeting before, and Mr. Kirkconnel was not well, being laid up with bronchitis. He could congratulate the Society upon the report which he held in his hand. The dividend of 5 per cent, was not of ten seen now-a-days, particularly with Building Societies. Fines £l9 3s. 81. on profit and loss account, considering the amount in the Society, was a small sum indeed. He then proceeded to refer to the new Act regulating Build- ing Societies,which came into operation on January lit., 1895, and pointed out that en the 25th August, 1896, their Society would pass out of existence unless it was incorperated under the now Act. In future, when the Society had been registered, instead of having trustees they would be a corporate body with seal, and he thought they would find the Act would be very bene- ficial. It would be a very simple matter to dissolve the Society and to register it under the Act. All they would have to do would be to call a meeting of the members, and they would appoint some official who would apply to the registrar to register the Society. The registrar weuld enquire into the bona-fides of the Society, and if satisfactory be would register it. Mr. Gilmour said the directors had had the Act under consideration for some time with the object of putting their rules in accordance with the Act, and it was their intention to put the Society on the best basis possible. _ _</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks to Mr. Fowler and the Chairman, terminated the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>_.•___-. WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>_.•___-. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>HOUBEHOLDLINEttS, hemmed and marked free of charge. —and W. Dutton, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Adat, STREET ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternogn. as a boy named George Hemer was trying to jump on to a lurry near to Froghall Bridge he slipped, one of the wheels pass- ing over his body, severely crushing him. The in- jured lad was at once removed to the Infirmary, where he remains as an in-patient.—A man named Thomas Williams was also received in the institu- tion the same day suffering from a broken leg. Tbe injury was caused by a fall. On Saturday morning Mrs. Dakin, of Grappenhall, and another lady were proceeding down Bridge-street in a phaeton when the pony bolted, and dashed into a barrow, which was being wheeled along the road by a lad named Starkey, of Wash-lane. Starkey was knocked down, and was immediately removed to his home,when he was found to have received no injuries beyond a severe shaking.</p>
        <p>TAMPS.—J. S. GARLAND), Ironmonger. Warrington.</p>
        <p>BTOVIIS.—Iron Merebssit. GARLAND. Bridge:street, Warrington. RE-STA.RTING OF A WIGAN IRONWORKS.</p>
        <p>We are informed that one of the three puddling forges at Wigan—which belong to the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company, Limited, and which have been stopped for abcut 12 months in consequence of the recent depressed condition of the iron trade was restarted on Tuesday. A novel system has bcen introduced by dividing the working day into three shifts of eight hours each, instead of two shifts of 12 hours as heretofore, and it is hoped if this sys- tem is carried out satisfactorily, it may be the means of keeping these works in permanent opera- tion, for the material benefit of a large body of iron workers in the neighbourhood who have for a long time been very short of employment.</p>
        <p>Swum Ilimmure, Hand £2 18s. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. With handsnm, walnut coven—J. and W. DurToN, 16 and 18, Sankey-A.. Warrington —Advt.</p>
        <p>A LATCHFORD LADY FOUND IN THE CANAL.</p>
        <p>On Friday night Mrs. Hazlehurst, of 343, Knuts- ford-road, Latchtord, who had been paying a visit to some friends in Thelwall left with the intention of catching the 6.45 p.m. train back to Latchford, but by some means, however, she got into the Bridgewater Canal. It is not known how long she was in the water, but she was found at 640 in an anconscioue condition by a man named Worthington and s porter employed by the London and North-, Western Railway Company et Thelwall Station. She was taken into a waiting-room at the station,where every effort was made to restore animation by Mr. J. Kyte, stationmaster, and others. The unfortun- ate lady's condition was, however, considered so serious that she was at once conveyed to the Warrington Infirmary.</p>
        <p>A YEAR'S WORK :AT THE WARRINGTON COUNTS</p>
        <p>COURT.</p>
        <p>In the annual return from county courts, issued on the 23rd October, it is shown that in the year 1894 there were in England and Wales 1,128,806 plaints entered, for a total amount of £3,308,201, in reapect of which judgments were obtained for .£1,779,882. This represents the largest amount of business done in the county courts in any year since their establishment. The following is a detailed statement of the work done in the above- named county court from the Ist of Jannary to the 31st December. 1894 :—Plaints entered for £2O and under.4,69B ; exceeding £2O. but not exceeding flity,37 ;exceeding £5O, by agreement, 1 ; actions determined with a jury, 2; actions determined without a jury, 3.130 ; for pl-tintiff, 1,419 ; for plaintiff, by admission or default, 1,776; for defen- dant, 37; judgment summonses issued, 1,743 ment summonses heard, 1,073; warrants of com- mittal issued. 526: debtors imprisoned, 17 execu- tions on goods, &amp;c., issued, 1,041 ; total for which plaints were issued, £10,800; amount for which judgments were obtained,Zs,74s ; amount paid out to suitors, £5,504 ; amount of costs £382 ; fees under schedule A, £1,578; fees under schedule B, £B5. Trial by jury in the county court is on the decline, the average of jury cases last year being about one in 700.</p>
        <p>itovernoLD LiNexs,hemmed awl marked free of chair —J. andW. DUTTON. .lankey-st., Wirrinzton.—Aivt. A FATAL FALL DOWNSTAIRS.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest at the Parade- room on Tuesday morning on the body of Ellen Hughes, aged 76 years, widow, Orchard-street, who died at 10 p.m. on Saturday night by a fall down the stairs at her residence.—Joshua Hewitt. son of deceased, said that on Saturday, his mother went to bed at eight o'clock. At about five minutes to ten, he took the lamp into the deceased's room, and then went to a neighbour's house, and it was while hs was out that the accident happened. She was killed instantaneously. She had been troubled very much with rheumatiani.—One of the jurymen: If you, Mr. Coroner, saw the stairs, you would corn- damn them.—Several otherjurymen also expressed the opinion that the stairs were not fit fcr men, woman or child.—Ellen Ogden, granddaughter, said that she took deceased to bed at eight o'clock on Saturday. When deceased fell downstairs, witness was in the kitchen. She rushed to the botton of the stairs, and found that she was dead. Mr. W.,ods was the owner of the house. There were 13 steps on the stairs.—The jury thought that the stairs were so dangerous, that some improvement should he made, but the coroner said that he knew of no power to compel owners to alter their stairs. The best thing that could be done was for Corporations to endeavour to acquire byelaws to prevent such accidents in future, but like level crossings on rail- I ways, they could not deal with those houses which were already erected. The jury could, however, suggest something to the owner of the house, whereby the stairs could be made safe. The jury returned a verdict of accidental death and sug- Igested to the owner of the house that a hand rail be provided along the stairs.</p>
        <p>f</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>• RUGBY FOOTBALL. Warrington gained their first point away from home on Saturday, and Stockport their first point at home,the game between the two ending in a draw of a try each. Warrington were unfortunate in not winning outright, for they had superior stamina to the Stockport men, and thus held out the longest. It was only the inability to score when the pinch came which robbed Warrington of a victory. Stockport commenced with a dash and looked as though they were going to give Warrington a hot time of it, and they did for the first quarter of an hour,during which they scored the try. They, however, fell away afterwards, and were very rarely dangerous. Warrington on the other band went right to the finish, and much praise must be given to the Stockport defen- ders for being able to keep their opponents out with the exception of once.</p>
        <p>Wilcock was the fortunate scorer for Warrington, although the chief credit for the try is due to Nevins who dashed to the line but was unable to ground the balLand thus gave an opportunity to Wilcock, which that player took advantage of. It was a somewhat lucky try, but nevertheless Warrington deserved it for they had made a , long attack upon the Stockport quarters. It is too I rarely that Dame Fortune smiles upon Warrington, and she did not smile so much on Saturday. Warrington had a very anxious time the last minute of the match, and it almost appeared as though Stockport would have pulled the game out of the fire, and it was only a bung- ling on the part of the players which prevented them. Had they done so, Warrington would have had fearfully hard lines, for they were the best team on the day's play. However a draw is better than a loss, and War- rington have gone up two in the League and now occupy a respectable position.</p>
        <p>The Warrington forwards after the first quarter of an hour had the upper hand of Stockport and were much smarter. Conspicuous among them were Saunders, Thornton, Hill, and Burrows, and of course Wilcock who scored the try. A harder work,ng set of forwards it would be difficult to find. Wer,, they a little heavier they would take some beating. Nevins and Houghton worked hard at half back and were certainly better than Allen and Harrison.</p>
        <p>Burton was the shining light of the threequarter line. It is a fact that he never played a better game since he commenced to play with the Warrington team. He was here, there, and everywhere, and it seemed impos- sible for him to do wrong. The Stockport spectators were simply astounded, and blamed him for their being robbed ot victory. They were loud ia their praisers of his display and said that there had not been a better centre threequarter on the ground this season. They were not far wrong either, for his play was a treat to witness.</p>
        <p>The remaining threequarters were not so brilliant but they were safe. Barber was unfortunately unable to play, and this handicapped the team, for with Burton in such form, had Barber been in good form also, Stock- port would have had something to do. Forster who played in his place played a fair game, but he was a little too eager, and lost several chances of scoring through this fault. Cross was also very safe and is well worth his place in the team. Broady, who was unwell, played very well under the circumstances. Heesom had not a very large amount of work as Burton and Cross relieved him well, but he was not found napping.</p>
        <p>Warrington are sadly in want of a goal kicker, and several penalties and marks might with a good kicker have been turned to advantage on Saturday. Eight tries have now been scored by Warrington and yet only one of these have been "placed." A little practice at this would be of benefit to the team.</p>
        <p>As above mentioned, Warrington have risen in the League table, and now are fifteenth in the list,having the same number of points as Hunslet who are fourteenth. They have a tough job on hand to-day, for they have to meet Wigan at Wigan. The latter have as yet suffered defeat from no Lancashire team,although they have had two draws. The only teams which have beaten them yet in the Northern League are Manningham and Wakefield Trinity. They have only played three games at home, and never a point has been scored against them. They are, however, but poor scorers, and only a solitary try has been scored by them at home, and their home record is one win and two pointless draws. Warrington made a pointless draw last season at Wigan,and as Wigan only drew with Rochdale Hornets on Saturday last, Warring- aon ought at least to draw or even win to-day. The fol- lowing is the team chosen to do duty for Warrington : Heesom, full back ; Barber, Cross, Burton, and Broady, three-quarters ; Nevins and Houghton, half backs ; Bur- rows, Saunders, Fairhurst, Hill, Thornton, Wilcock, An- drews, and Barrett, forwards.</p>
        <p>A most sineular fact fact about the matches on Satur- ,l.% in the Northern League was that only one team— Manningham,who beat their neighbours,Bradford—were victorious at home. It is generally thought that the home teams have the pull, but last Saturday's results quite upset this idea. Four matches—St. Helens v. Ty Idesley, Rochdale Hornets v. Wigan, Hunslet v.Leeds, and Stockport v. Warrington, were drawn, while the re- maining six matches were lost by the hon-.e teams. Hud- dersfield, Widoes, Leigh, Batley, Wakefield Trinity, and Halifax all went under before their supporters, while Hull, Oldham, Runcorn, Liversedge,Broughton Rangers, and Brighouse Rangers won away from home. It will probably be a long time before such a result occurs again.</p>
        <p>Although the visiting teams were so saccessful, the results on the whole were very close. Rochdale Hornets and Wigan ended as they began, nothing being scored, and in three matches each side scored a try. The two Rangers in the League each won by a goal,and Manning. ham beat Bradford by four points.</p>
        <p>The most decisive match was that in which Oldham beat Widnes by 13 points to nil, but a lot of credit is taken away from this victory by the fact that up to about three quarter time, neither side had scored, and then a Widnes man was hurt and had to retire for the rest of the game,doring which Oldham put on two goals and a try. Liversedge,the scoring team, scored 13 points to Batley's 3, while Hull put on 11 to Huddersfield 3.</p>
        <p>Leigh are falling from their high estate, and now in- stead of beingsecond,with the best record in the League, they are only fifth. They have suffered two successive home defeats by the Widnes and Runcorn, and they will respect the two neighbours on the Mersey in future. Runcorn have now taken their place as leaders of the Lancashire and Cheshire section and are now fourth on the table.</p>
        <p>I The appeal by Leigh to declare the match with Widnes void has failed, as everyone expected. They not only have to lose their deposit of £5, but they have to post notices on the field warning the spectators of rowdy behaviour. They have therefore lost more than they Rained by the appeal.</p>
        <p>For scoring, Liversedge would be hard to beat. They commenced the season very badly, for they were de- feated pointless in the first three matches. In the re- maining six matches they played, they have scored 81 points, or an average of 14i per match. Had they corn- meneed the season in their present form they wluld have passed their" century "before now.</p>
        <p>The following is the present list of points of the various clubs in the Northern Leagues : _ _</p>
        <p>Score. Score. Pts Phi. Won. Lost. Drn. For. Ag'st. Beghouse Ran. 9 ... 7 ... 1 ... 1 66 ... 6 ... 15 Bradford Halifax Runcorn 9 5 2 2 . 79 ... 32 ... 12 Lelgh Manningham 9 ... 6 ... 3 ... 0 ... 63 ... 39 ... 12 St Helens 9 5 2 2 . 33 30 12 Llversedge 9 .. 5 ... 3 ... I 87 ... 34 ... II Leeds Oldham Wigan 9 . 3 2 4 27 ... 21 ... 10 linll Tyldesley Kunslet 9 3 5 1 .52 51 ... 7 Warrington Batley 9 2 5 2 .. 31 62 . 6 Widnes Stockport Br'ght'n Ran 9 ... 2 ... 6 ... 1 ... 30 ... 48 ... 5 Huddersfield 9 ... 2 ... 7 ... 0 ... 35 ... 69 ... 4 Wakefield T g 2 ... 7 ... 0 ... 22 .. Si ... 4 Rochdale H 9 ... I ... 6 ... 2 ... 16 ... 96 ... 4</p>
        <p>The Lancashire and Cheshire section is as follows : Score Score Pts Pld. Won. Lost. Drn. For. Ag'st. 1 Runcorn St. Helens 6 4 1 . 1 ... 24 ... 12 ... 9 Wigan Leigh Tyldesley Stockport Rochdale H 5 ... 1 ... 2 ... 2 ... 9 ... 38 ... 4 Warrington Widnes Oldham 13'g'ht'n Ran 6 ... 0 ... 5 ... 1 ... 7 —134 ... 1 The Yorks section is as follows :</p>
        <p>Score Score Pt.. Pld. Won. Lost. Drn. For. /VV. Vghouse Ran. 6 ... 5 ... 1 ... 0 .., 35 ... 3 ... 10 Leeds Manningham 6 ... 4 ... 1 ... 0 ... 37 ... 13 .. 13 Hull 5 4 2 0 .. 20 ... 13 ... 6 Liyersedge 4 . 2 2 0 .29 13 4 Halifsx Batley 5 2 3 0 . 23 35 4 Hunslat 5 1 3 1 .21 29 . 3 Bradford 3 . 1 2 . 0 ... 15 ... 18 ... 2 Wakeß'd Trlty 5 ... 1 ... 4 ... 0 ... 9 ... 50 ... 2 Huddersfield 6 .... 1 .... 5 ... 0 ... 19 ... 40 .... 2</p>
        <p>111111MMEMIIIM</p>
        <p>Another win crowned the efforts rf the Warrington " A " on Saturday, when they beat Stockport "A " team by 1 goal, 2 tries to a try. Unlike the game of a fortnight ago this match was not a run-away one by any means. Stockport became dangerous several times and the wonder was that the score was not more like that of the two first teams. The homesters' advantage lay. however, in having such good attacking nu-n as Lawless and Carey,who were ever to the fore, while the forwards were more than a match for the opposing eight. An increased number of spectators lined the field and with the addition of fine weather the match was essentially a good one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>STATUS OF A NORTHERN RUGBY UNION PLAYER. –</head>
        <p>THE STATUS OF A NORTHERN RUGBY UNION PLAYER. -</p>
        <p>At the quarterly meeting of the Northern Counties Athletic Association, which was held in Manchester on Wednesday night, the status of the Northern Rugby Union players was discussed, and it was finally resolved "That an amateur athlete does not forfeit his amateur status by playing with or against Northern Union club), but the acceptance of payment for broken time for play- ing at football renders him ineligible to compete as an amateur athlete."</p>
        <p>The effect of this is that the delegates to the Amateur Athletic Association meeting are instructed to vote in favour of athletes who play with Northern Rugby Union clubs retaining their status, providing they do not ac- cept payment for the broken time. The meeting of the Amateur Athletic Association will be held in London some time next month, and the action decided upon at the meeting on Wednesday will. doubtless, have a great effect upon the ultimate decision arrived at.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>WESTON PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>WESTON PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Parish Council of Weston was he'd on Friday evening in the church schoolroom. The Rev. Canon Wood presided,and there were also present Rev. D. S. Bowen, Messrs. Savage, Moore, Tudor, Ball, Sill, Sweetman, Basnett, Clarke, Guest, Vose, and J. CeHier (clerk).—Tne first business of importance was to receive the report of the committee appointed to con- sider the nightsoil question. In presenting the report, Mr. Sill said all the sehernes which had been put before the Council were carefully considered in detail. The committee thought that after the very slight encourag ment given to their last presentment to the District Council, it would be almost useless for them to send up another one on the same lines. They heard that it re- ceived very scant courtesy, and the committee were of the opinion that the Council should form itself into a Parochical Committee. Under those circumstances, the District Council would, no doubt, call upon them to draw up a scheme,and give them power to carry it out. He moved the following resolution "That the necessary steps betaken for the purpose of forming a Council into a Parochial Committee so that it shall have power to draw up a scheme for the consideration of the District Council and see that such scheme be carried out."—Mr. Vose seconded.—An amendment was pro- posed by Mr. Moores that the adoption of the report be deferred, and that the District Council be asked to pro- vide two deposit banks. Be thought if that were done it would bridge them over the difficulty.—Mr. Barrow seconded the amendment.—On being put to the vote, six voted for the amendment and five for the original motion. It was further decided that the Clerk write to the Clerk of the Roncorn District Council submitting the decision of the Council, and also to inform the district councillors for Weston of the wishes of the Council.— Mr. Ball, in accordance with notice given, proposed that the trustees for the management of the Orred Charity be increased to three members, one additional trustee to be nominated by the present sole trustee and one by the Parish Council, and that application be made to the Charity Commissioners for their approval of the same.— Mr. Vose seconded the resolution.—The Chairman said the Act did not compel the Council to deal with chari- ties, it left them at liberty to do as they wished in the matter.—The Rev. D. S. Bowen said he would like to ask whether any complaints had been made as to the way the charity had been distributed during the past year. He was present the previous year when it was distributed, and he did not think any question was asked with regard to religion, pffitics, or anything else. He did net see why they should appoint any aclffitirrial trustees.—Mr. Moore wished to know whetner thsre was anything in the will, expressing the wish of the ef the donor of the charity as to who sh-uld distribute it P He thought it was for the industrkus poor of the township. The Chairman said the general impression was that Miss Orred was in the habit of giving presents at Christman time to tolerably well-to-do people, and that when she left the money it was not her intention to relieve distress, but that industrious and well-behaved people living within the township of Weston should get a little gratuity at Christmas. He thought it would be a misfortune if it degenerated into giving it in the shape lof relief. It was, however, an exceedingly difficult matter to refuse help to people when they came to you in distress—Mr. Ball said he had been told that the Charity was intended for the honest industrious poor who kept themselves off the rates. The Chairman said Sit would have beers a misfortune if they had carried that out last Christmas.—On being put to the vote there voted for the resolution, six ; against, five.—The Chair- man said he would not vote because he happened to be the sole trustee himself.—The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>AN OBSTREPEROUS VEGETABLE VENDOR.</head>
        <p>AN OBSTREPEROUS VEGETABLE VENDOR.</p>
        <p>On Monday befcre the Mayor and Alderman Fran- comb, James Ellison, 17, Aiken-st., was charged with having on the 19:h Oct., in his p issession a weighing instrument not stamped ;he was also charged with re- fusing to allow Inspector W. H. Wallington to examine a pair of scales he had for trade.—Defendant pleaded guilty to both charges.—lnspector Flare said that on the 22nd September last, defendant presented a pair of scales at the Market office to be stamped,but on examin- ation they found they could not stamp them, and de- fendant was told to get another pair. On the 12th inst he was found using the scales in a street off Priestley - st. The inspector told him to get a new pair and deten- dant made use of bad language. On the 19th witness sent his assistants out with instructions to take the scales from the defendant. They found him at Bank Quay and asked him if he had the scales. Defendant said not, but a pair was found under a sack in his cart. Mr. Wallington got hold of them but defendant seized the other end and would not let the inspector examine them. He, however,noticed that they were a different pair and unstamped. Defendant a`i last ran away with them. Hews! accordingly summoned, that he had given the wrong address.—Defendant said that he could not afford a new pain—The magistrates inflicted a penalty of 5. and costs in the first case, and ss. including the costs in the second (16s. 6d. in all).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-02_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>G.HINEItAL-NEWS.</head>
        <p>G.HINEItAL-NEWS.</p>
        <p>The bethrothal of Princess Maud of Wales to Prince Carl, second son of the Crown Prince of Denmark, is officals, announced.</p>
        <p>The issue of £500,000 of Glasgow Corporation 24 per cent. stock has been taken up. The average price ob- tained was £lOO. is. per £lOO of stock. The body of James Tonney, the Blackpool waiter who has been missing since the murder of his wife on the 13th inst., was on Monday morning picked up near the Fleetwood Landmark.</p>
        <p>A s tnauinary fight is reported between some 2,500 Zulus and Shangans employed on mines In the Rand. At least four natives are stated to have been killed. The Lunacy Commissioners have made an order for the release of Miss Lanchestcr, the young woman be- longing to Battersea,whose friends had her removed to a lunatic asylum a few days ago in order to put a stop to an intimacy between her and a Socialist named Sulli- van.</p>
        <p>The Salford School Board laid the memorial-stones on Stturday of two new schools which are to cost, the one £7.268 and the other £8,852. Damage estimated at £12,000 was caused by fire at the North British Railway Company's stores at Cowlairs, near Glasgow, on Sunday. It is stated that the -Clyde master engineers have de- cided to lock-out 25 per cent. of their men on Tuesday next. This step has been taken owing to the pressure put upon the Clyde firms by the employers in Belfast.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON TEE HEALTH OF WARRINGToN. Week ending 12th October, 1895. During the week, which was the 41st of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths Warrington 38 31 Latchford 4</p>
        <p>Total 3B</p>
        <p>The excess of deaths over births was 3. The death rate from all causes was equal to 31 8 per 1,000, against 38 6 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 161 the corresponding week of last year. The death. rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 182 per 1,000, against 20.4 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 2 7 the corresponding week of 1894. The ages of mortality were- 0— 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 15 16 3 1 35</p>
        <p>The deaths included-1 from scarlet fever, 7 froTi, measles, 12 from diarrhcea, 3 from lung disease, 3 from brain disease, 1 from old age, 4 from debility. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Saarlet fever, 9 cases. enteric 2 cases.</p>
        <p>Total number of patients in hospital on Oct. 12h-41 METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 49.9 Fah,, and therefore 5.6 degrees lower than the pm cedieg week. The maximum temperature 61F., was recorded on the 6th inst., and the tnimimum temperature 39 F., on the 6'h Oct. inst.</p>
