        <p>, THE STARS. 'Tie night, and thick as daisies spread O'er English meadows green, The s' ars of heaven, like di'monds, shed Their lustre on the scene. For still with undiminished powers, And eyes that never sleep, O'er all this wondrous earth of ours ==.• They nightly vigil keep. And peaceful are the scenes displayed ,1 . Beneath their gentle eyes : A silent world in s umber laid, And still more silent skies. 0 gems of night, I loved ye when ' The world to me was new, And, artless in the ways of men, My cares were light and few. earlyrisE,krwe yn m e, glhatodrhenaiadnngwds hcdor ssl yappgaponenader leer, As day came slowly on. And when returning night again Your shining host revealed, I watched ye,through my window Pane' Till sleep the vision sealed. Theno f whencep as pleasing we ecraemtcle dreamsand how I dreatnea' : While your dark heaven more distant Waled' And vaster tar than now. Even yet, in life's declining years, These tailing eyes will turn, Sometimes in joy, sometimes in tears, , To where your splendours burn. ' - And while I gaze I seem to see Some gleams responsive there. Some kindly glances bent on me Poor mortal bowed with care. My friends like leaves in autumn fall, Frail things of mortal birth ; And soon the grave will cover all I know and loos on earth. But, far beyond the reach of tiine. Ye suffer no decay ; No changes mar your scenes sublinlet Your orbs pass sot away. Then 0, while lasts my " little while," And sorrows round me press, Still cheer me with the kindly etnne That makes these sorrows less. And when I render up the breath A power Almighty gave. Look on me in the hour of death, And shine upon my grave. H. W. CIaINDINSIS6. FURNISH AT JOHN fIARRO 122 BRIDGE-S TROT, WARRINGTON. bD IR. HARROP wishes to draw th:l arott:nttoi°ll the public of Warrington and thst pplieel IMPROVED CREDIT SYSTEM. GOODS 'uattlera" tl LOWEST CASH PRICES for prompt monthi9 or upon the following easy terms : virSElOl. ..e5 WORTH ON GOODS 28.6 d• £lO WORTH.